Because $X/H$ is compact, and $\varphi$ is $H$-equivariant and proper, the quotient $Y/H$ is compact as well. Hence
so is the quotient of the set  $\{(y,y') \in Y\times Y; d_Y(y,y') \leq A(B+1)\}$ by $H$. So the  $H$-invariant function \eqref{eq fn sup Sl} is bounded on the latter set.
Together with the estimate \eqref{eq est d bigger 1} for the case where $d(x,y)>A(B+1)$, and the fact that $F_{f,n}$ is a decreasing function, this implies part (b).
