%Disorder is often seen as detrimental to collective dynamics, yet recent work has shown that heterogeneity can enhance network synchronization. However, the constructive role of disorder in stabilizing nontrivial patterns remains largely unexplored. In this Letter, we show that intrinsic frequency heterogeneity can induce stable Cyclops and cluster states in Kuramoto networks of rotators with higher-mode coupling, even though these states are unstable in the identical oscillator case. Cyclops states, introduced in [Munyaev et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 130, 107021 (2023)], feature two synchronized clusters and a solitary oscillator, forming a configuration requiring a delicate phase balance. Surprisingly, introducing frequency heterogeneity stabilizes these patterns across a relatively broad range of detuning values, making them emerge robustly from a wide set of initial conditions. Using a collective coordinate reduction, we develop a mesoscopic approach that connects microscopic frequency structure, captured by a parameter quantifying the influence of the solitary oscillator, with mean-field cluster-level stability. This enables accurate prediction of favorable frequency distributions even in small or finite-size networks that typically resist standard mean-field analysis. Our results demonstrate that heterogeneity need not be compensated by other forms of disorder or external tuning to support complex cooperative states. Our work expands the known constructive roles of disorder and provides a framework for analyzing other multi-state dynamics in broader classes of heterogeneous oscillator networks.

