The following lemma is straightforward but very useful.
\begin{lemma}
     $\lambda_i(e_B) $ is the orthogonal projection from $ \FF $ onto the subspace of vectors consisting of those in $L^2B$ or those beginning with $ L^2M_i^o$, where the indices are interpreted as in $\mathbb{Z}_2$. In particular, we have $ \lambda_1(e_B)\lambda_2(e_B)  = \lambda_2(e_B)\lambda_1(e_B) = e_B$, where the last operator $e_B$ in the equation denotes the orthogonal projection from $\mathcal{F}$ onto $L^2 B$.
     %and $ \lambda_{i+1}(e_B)\lambda_i(e_B) $ is the orthogonal projection onto the subspace of vectors in $ \FF $ beginning with $ L^2M_i^o $.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: relation between T and Vij}
    \begin{equation*}
        T^*V^*_{i,j} = V^*_{i+1,j}V_{i+1,j}T^*V^*_{i,j},\quad V_{i,j}T = V_{i,j}TV^*_{i+1,j}V_{i+1,j},
    \end{equation*}
    where the indices are interpreted as in $\mathbb{Z}_2$. Moreover, we have
    \[ V_{i+1,j}T^*V^*_{i,j} = \begin{cases}
        \lambda_{i+1}(e_B^\perp)\lambda_i(e_B) = \lambda_{i+1}(e_B)^\perp,\quad i=j\\
        \lambda_i(e_B),\quad i\neq j
    \end{cases} \]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    The proof for the first claim follows directly from the definition of $T$. Indeed, for $ T^*V^*_{1,1} $, we notice that $ T( \KK_{1,1} )\subseteq \KK_{1,2} $. But since $V^{*}_{1,1}$ maps onto $ \KK_{1,1} $, and $ V^{*}_{i,j}V_{i,j} $ is the orthogonal projection onto $ \KK_{i,j} $, we must have $TV^*_{1,1} = V^{*}_{2,1}V_{2,1}TV^*_{1,1} $. The other cases follow in a similar way.

    The second claim is also straightforward if one assume $B$ admits a faithful normal state. The proof with a general n.s.f.\ weight $\varphi$ is the same as the state case if one carefully represents vectors in  $D(\HH,\varphi) $ for a given $B$-module $\HH$. To see this, we check for $ V_{2,1}T^*V^*_{1,1} $. For a vector $ \xi\in L^2B $, we have $ V^{*}_{1,1}\xi = \xi\in L^2M_1\subset\KK_1 $ is a vector with tensor length $1$. But as $T^*$ vanish on vectors with tensor length $1$, we must have $T^*V^{*}_{1,1}\xi =0$. Now, for a vector begins with $ L^2M^o_2 $: $ \xi^o \otimes \Xi \in L^2M^o_{2}\otimes_B \cdots\otimes_B L^2M^o_{i_n}$ where $ \xi^o\in L^2M_2^o $ and $ \Xi\in D'( L^2M^o_{1}\otimes_B \cdots\otimes_B L^2M^o_{i_n},\varphi ) $, we have
    $ V^*_{1,1}\xi^o\otimes \Xi = \varphi^{1/2}\otimes \xi^o\otimes \Xi\in L^2M_1\otimes_B L^2M_2\otimes_B \cdots \otimes_B L^2M_{i_n}$. Therefore, $ T^* V^*_{1,1}\xi^o\otimes \Xi = \xi^o\otimes \Xi\in L^2M_2\otimes_B \cdots \otimes_B L^2M_{i_n} $ and $ V_{2,1}T^* V^*_{1,1}\xi^o\otimes \Xi = \xi^o\otimes \Xi $. This shows that $ V_{2,1}T^* V^*_{1,1}$ is identity on $ \lambda_{2}(e_B^{\perp})\lambda_{1}(e_B)\FF $. On the other hand, for a vector begins with $ L^2M_1^o $: $  \xi^o\otimes \Xi = \xi^o\otimes \Xi\in L^2M_1^o\otimes_B L^2M_2^o\otimes_B \cdots \otimes_B L^2M_{i_n}^o$, we have $ V^*_{1,1}\xi^o\otimes \Xi = \xi^o\otimes\Xi \in L^2M_1\otimes_B L^2M_2\otimes_B \cdots \otimes_B L^2M_{i_n} $. But since $ \xi^o\in L^2M_1^o $ is centered, we have $ T^*V^*_{1,1}\xi^o\otimes \Xi = 0 $. This shows that $ V_{2,1}T^*V_{1,1}^* $ vanishes on $\lambda_{2}(e_B)$. The cases for different $i,j$ follow from the same computation.
\end{proof}





We define a u.c.p.\ map $\Phi\colon \mathbb{B}(\mathcal{K})\to \mathbb{B}(L^{2}M)$ by $\Phi(x) = \frac{1}{4}\sum_{i,j,k,l=1}^{2} V_{i,j}xV_{k,l}^{*}$, so that the support projection of $\Phi$ is no larger than the orthogonal projection from $\mathcal{K}$ onto $\bigoplus_{i,j=1,2}\mathcal{K}_{i,j}$. 
Therefore, $p\geqslant \supp(\Phi)$ and thus $\Phi(p) = 1$. 


By Lemma \ref{lemma T*aT}, each (nonzero) word in $ N_1\cup N_2\cup \{T\} $ can be reduced to the form
