    \section{Introduction}
    The detection of gravitational waves from a binary black hole merger \cite{Abbott_2016} and the releasing of the images of the supermassive black holes M87* and SgrA* \cite{2019,Event_Horizon_Telescope_Collaboration_2022}, indicate that experimental gravity has entered a new era probing the strong-field near-horizon regime of black holes \cite{Abbott_2016,Abbott_2018,Abbott_2018-2,Abbott_2019,Abbott_2019-1,Psaltis_2020,Vagnozzi_2023,EHTsgraV,V_lkel_2021}.
    These groundbreaking observations provide new opportunities not only to test general relativity (GR) in its most extreme conditions, but also to search for imprints of fundamental physics beyond GR, including signatures of dark matter, quantum gravity, or cosmological scalar fields \cite{Giddings_2018,Abbott_2022-dark,Hirschmann_2018,Abbott_2023,Abbott_2021-cos,V_lkel_2021}. To interpret these horizon-scale images, the Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration (EHTC) has developed a comprehensive library of general-relativistic magnetohydrodynamics (GRMHD) simulations to model the hot and magnetized accretion flows around Kerr black holes, which is a stationary and axisymmetric vacuum solution in GR \cite{2022ApJ...930L..16E,2019m87V}. By comparing synthetic images with observational data, an effective null test of GR has been achieved \cite{EHTsgraVI}. Nevertheless, current observations still cannot completely rule out the possibility of deviations from GR, leaving ample room for alternative theories of gravity. It is interesting and necessary to probe possible observational distinguishable signatures from these theories of gravity deviating from GR, such as, theories incorporating additional scalar or pseudoscalar fields, which are motivated by string theory or dark sector physics. 

    The bright ring-like structures in images observed by EHT encode a lot of critical information on spacetime because the bright ring is formed by the strong gravitational lensing of lights emitted by the accretion flow around black holes. 
    Therefore, these structures together with the shadow diameter of black holes can be applied to make direct constraints on black hole spacetime geometry and to test the Kerr black hole hypothesis \cite{2019m87V,EHTsgraV}. Simultaneously, the polarization structures in black hole images offer another independent diagnosis for testing gravitational theories through their sensitivity to the underlying magnetic field configuration.  One of important ingredient in polarization images is the distribution of the electric vector position angle (EVPA), which can obtained by analyzing the parallel transport of polarization vectors in curved spacetime. Recently, a set of coefficients $\beta_m$ are introduced to quantitatively characterize the polarization mode distribution.
    The coefficients  $\beta_m$ are generally complex numbers and the magnitudes of $\beta_m$ quantify the degree of $m$-wise rotational symmetry in the EVPA distribution around the ring, while the phase angle $\angle\beta_m$ measures the average orientation of the symmetric component relative to a reference pattern \cite{m87VIII,Palumbo_2020}. The  coefficient $\beta_2$ can serve as an effective tool to identify the magnetic field orderliness and the turbulence level of the accretion flow, which could enhance tests of gravitational theories \cite{m87VIII,Palumbo_2020,Abramowicz_2013}.

    In the Einstein-Maxwell-Dilaton-Axion (EMDA) theory of gravity, there exist additional scalar-dilaton field (dilaton) and  pseudo-scalar axion field, and both of them are coupled to the spacetime metric and the Maxwell field. These scalar fields originate from string theory compactifications and play significant roles in both early-universe inflation and the late-time acceleration of cosmic expansion \cite{Sonner_2006,Catena_2008}. A variety of black hole solutions have been derived within string-inspired low-energy effective theories \cite{Gibbons:1987ps,PhysRevD.43.3140,Kallosh_1993}, and many of them deviate notably from those predicted by GR. For example, the static and spherically symmetric black holes in these theories often possess non-trivial charges related to the dilaton and antisymmetric tensor fields. Moreover, the uncharged rotating black hole in string theory exactly matches the Kerr solution of GR  \cite{1986bhmp.book.....T}. However, the rotating and charged black hole solution in EMDA gravity corresponds to the Kerr-Sen metric, which resembles but is not equivalent to the Kerr-Newman solution of GR. Given that string theory could offer a framework for unifying fundamental forces, it is significant to probe  observational signals of Kerr-Sen black holes, which could serve as a valuable indirect test of string theory. Actually, the possible observable effects of Kerr-Sen black holes have been extensively investigated \cite{Hioki_2008,PhysRevD.75.023006,PhysRevD.94.084025,PhysRevD.97.024003,PhysRevD.75.023006}, including strong gravitational lensing effect and black hole shadows. The polarization rotation phenomenon of circularly polarized light has been investigated in the spacetime of Kerr-Sen black holes \cite{narang2020testkerrsenmetricblack}. Additionally, using a geometrically thin accretion disk model around the Kerr-Sen black hole, the influence of the dilaton parameter $r_2$ on radiation intensity and black hole shadow properties has been studied in ref.\cite{Feng:2024iqj}. Recent investigations imply that there exist a geometrically thick disk around a real black hole, such as M87* and Sgr A*. Moreover, the emission properties of the accretion flows can significantly alter its observable morphology. To more realistically study the imaging properties of black holes illuminated by the complicated accretion flows, the numerical computation of GRMHD equations is required because it can capture the dynamical properties of accretion flows in the geometrically thick disk. However, this consumes substantial computational resources. Therefore, as a semi-analytical model, the RIAF model remains highly favored \cite{2003ApJ...598..301Y, Pu_2018,2022A&A...667A.170V,jiang2023shadowsloopquantumblack}.  
    RIAFs typically characterized by their sub-Keplerian nature are the preferred mode of accretion flows for SgrA* because it can simulate its images in millimeter-wave band which reasonably match with the observational data \cite{2003ApJ...598..301Y}. Therefore, in this paper, we will focus on studying the images of RIAFs around Kerr-Sen black holes and exploring the influence of the dilaton parameter as well as the disk thickness on these images.

    The paper is organized as follows: In Sec. II, we briefly introduce the RIAF model in the spacetime of a Kerr-Sen black hole. In Sec. III, we will extract the bright ring's features from black hole images generated by GRRT and probe the influence of the dilaton parameter and the disk thickness on these images. In Sec. IV, we will apply $\beta_2$ mode to analyze how the dilaton parameter and the disk thickness affect the polarization structures in the images of Kerr-Sen black holes. Finally, we end with a summary.

    \section{MODELING RIAF DYNAMICS around Kerr-Sen black holes}
    \label{sec:2}
    The stationary and axisymmetric black hole solution in Einstein-Maxell-Daliton-Axion theory is represented by the Kerr-Sen metric, whose metric in the Boyer-Lindquist coordinates has the following form \cite{Bernard_2016,Ghezelbash_2013,PhysRevLett.74.1276}
    \begin{eqnarray}
        \label{metric}
        \begin{aligned}
        \mathrm{d}s^{2} & =-\left(1-\frac{2Mr}{\tilde{\Sigma}}\right)\mathrm{d}t^{2}+\frac{\tilde{\Sigma}}{\Delta}\mathrm{d}r^{2}+\tilde{\Sigma}\mathrm{d}\theta^{2}-\frac{4aMr}{\tilde{\Sigma}}\sin^{2}\theta\mathrm{d}t\mathrm{d}\phi \\
         & +\sin^2\theta\mathrm{d}\phi^2\left[r(r+r_2)+a^2+\frac{2Mra^2\sin^2\theta}{\tilde{\Sigma}}\right],
        \end{aligned}
    \end{eqnarray}
    where
    \begin{eqnarray}
    \Delta=r(r+r_2)-2Mr+a^2,\quad\quad\quad \tilde{\Sigma}=r(r+r_2)+a^2\cos^2\theta.
    \end{eqnarray}
    Here $M$ represents the black hole's mass, and $a$ is its rotation parameter. The dilaton parameter $r_2$ is related to the black hole's electric charge $Q$ and the string tension $\alpha'$ by $r_2=\frac{\alpha' Q^2}{8M}$. The electric charge of a Kerr-Sen black hole fundamentally is affected by the axion-photon coupling \cite{Banerjee_2021}.
    As the dilaton parameter $r_2$ vanishes, the metric (\ref{metric}) reduces to that of Kerr black hole. When the rotation parameter $a$ disappears, the spacetime is reduced to a static spherically symmetric black hole determined exclusively by its mass, electric charge, and the asymptotic dilaton field \cite{YAZADJIEV_1999,PhysRevD.43.3140}. 
    It is noteworthy that the Kerr-Sen spacetime Eq. (\ref{metric}) can also be derived through a Newman-Janis transformation\cite{Newman:1965tw} on the spherically symmetric solution in pure dilaton-coupled gravity \cite{YAZADJIEV_1999,PhysRevD.43.3140}.
    The radii of black hole horizons satisfy condition $\Delta = 0$ and have the form
    \begin{equation}
        \label{horizon}
        r_{\pm}=M-\frac{r_2}{2}\pm\sqrt{(M-\frac{r_2}{2})^2-a^2},
    \end{equation}
   where $r_+$ and $r_-$, respectively, correspond to the cases of the outer and inner horizons. It is clear that this solution describes a nonextremal black hole for $r_+>r_-$, while a naked singularity appears if $(M-\frac{r_2}{2})^2<a^2$. 

    In general, black hole images are jointly determined by both the black hole parameters and the plasma around the black hole \cite{Younsi_2023,_zel_2022}.  For the current observed targets of the EHT including M87* and Sgr A*, their surrounding hot plasmas are considered to be part of a RIAF. For simplicity, here we consider only a semi-analytical optically thin RIAF model\cite{Yuan_2003,Pu_2018} around the Kerr-Sen black hole and probe the corresponding images. This model features a sub-Keplerian velocity distribution in the accretion flow and a vertical magnetic field configuration \cite{2024ApJ...964L..26E}. Combining with GRRT technology, we can simulate Kerr-Sen black hole images using the \texttt{ipole} \cite{Noble:2007zx,moscibrodzka2017ipolesemianalyticscheme} code. As in other black hole spacetimes, the virtual observation camera is positioned at a distance of 1000 gravitational radii ($r_g$) from the black hole, with a field of view of $200\times200$ microarcseconds ($\mu$as). The simulation generates the black hole images by tracing photon geodesics in the Kerr-Sen spacetime and solving the radiative transfer equations at 230 GHz, where the radiation in this band is predominantly dominated by synchrotron emission originating from the thermal electrons. Here, we treat SgrA* as a target object, so the black hole mass is set to $4.3\times10^6 \rm M_\odot$ and a distance between the black hole and Earth is set to $8.3 \rm kpc$ \cite{Event_Horizon_Telescope_Collaboration_2022}. Moreover, we also adopt the magnetically arrested disk (MAD) model to mimick disk configuration as in \cite{Chen_2022} because the MAD model is supported by the current EHT observations \cite{2022ApJ...930L..16E}. The plasma is treated as undergoing free-fall motion within the innermost stable circular orbit (ISCO) and behaving a sub-Keplerian rotation outside the ISCO. The angular frequency in the free-fall state is $\omega_{\rm FF}=g^{t\phi}/g^{tt}$ and the Keplerian angular frequency is $\omega_{\rm K}=u^\phi/u^t$. The sub-Keplerian angular frequency is given by a linear combination of the two parts, i.e.,
    \begin{equation}
        \omega_{\rm SubK}=\omega_{\rm K}+(1-K)(\omega_{\rm FF}-\omega_{\rm K}),
    \end{equation}
    where $K$ is the Keplerian factor. The radial velocity of the inflow plasmoid is $u_{\rm FF}^r=\sqrt{|-(1+g^{tt})g^{rr}|}$ in the free-falling case, and is $u^{r}_{\rm SubK}=(1-K)u_{\rm FF}^r$ in the sub-Keplerian case.

    The thickness of the accretion disk is characterized by the dimensionless parameter $H \equiv h/R$, where $h$ is the vertical thickness of the flow and $R$ is the radial scale of the flow \cite{Pu_2018}. For MAD-like accretion flows, we here select $H=0.3$, $0.1$, and $0.05$ as representative values to simulate black hole images \cite{Chen_2022}. In our simulation,  the normalized electron number density  $n_{e,th}^0$ and  the electron temperature $T_e^0$ are appropriately are chosen to $n_{e,th}^0 \approx 10^7 \mathrm{cm^{-3}}$ and $T_e^0 \approx 10^{11} \mathrm{K}$, respectively. Those ensure that the observed flux of thermal synchrotron radiation near 230 GHz matches the measured value of Sgr A* (approximately 3 Jy) for each set of parameters \cite{Pu_2018}.
