    \section{EXTRACTED RING FEATURES in images of  Kerr-Sen black holes }
    \label{sec:4}

     It is necessary and critical to extract ring features from black hole images generated by GRRT. The ring-shaped structure in images are often characterized by a set of parameters that govern its geometric morphology and brightness distribution. In terms of the works of the Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration (EHTC) \cite{2019ApJ...875L...4E,EventHorizonTelescope:2019ggy},  these feature parameters include the ring diameter and its width, azimuthal angle, the asymmetry and fractional central brightness of ring. The ring diameter and azimuthal angle derived from different imaging methods exhibit the highest consistency, while other parameters fluctuate due to variations in the imaging pipeline. Therefore, to simplify the discussion, we here focus on the  ring diameter to analyze the influence of the dilaton parameter on the Kerr-Sen black hole images.
     Fig. \ref{fig:1} displays the optical images of a Kerr-Sen black hole illuminated by a prograde RIAF accretion disk. There exist two common features in both unblurred and blurred images. One of them is the central dark region, whose boundary encloses the dark area corresponding to the direct emission image of the event horizon, i.e., the inner shadow \cite{Chael_2021}. The other is the bright ring marking the critical curve of the black hole shadow. Comparing subplots with fixed spin parameters in each row in Fig. \ref{fig:1},  it is found that higher values of the dilation parameter $r_2$ generally result in smaller bright rings and shadow sizes.

%stage of analysis, feature extraction 

    We here employe the REx algorithm to quantify the morphological properties of ring-like images, which is implemented in the \texttt{eht-imaging} library \cite{chael2019simulating}. 
    \begin{figure}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=16cm]{fig_3.png}
        \caption{Distribution of the bright ring size on the $r_2-a$ plane for the blurred images of Kerr-Sen black holes with different inclination $i$ and disk thicknesses $H$. The left, middle, and right panels at each row correspond to disk thicknesses of 0.05, 0.1, and 0.3, respectively. The top, middle, and bottom rows correspond to inclination angles of $5^\circ$, $15^\circ$, and $30^\circ$, respectively. }
        \label{fig:3}
    \end{figure}
    In Fig. \ref{fig:2}, we present the unwrapped ring profiles of the simulated images of Kerr-Sen black holes for three different values of the dilation parameter selected in Fig. \ref{fig:1}. It is shown that the ring diameter decreases with the dilation parameter $r_2$. It must be pointed that the observed ring diameter is affected by the resolution of  the observation instrument. Therefore, we blur the theoretical image to the typical EHT resolution (convolution with a 20 $\mu$as Gaussian beam) \cite{EHTsgraVI,Chael_2018} and analyze the corresponding features in the black hole images.

    In Fig. \ref{fig:3}, we present distribution of the bright ring size on the $r_2-a$ plane for the blurred images of Kerr-Sen black holes with different inclination $i$ and disk thicknesses $H$.
    The gray triangles represent results of the ring diameters extracted by the REx algorithm  from our GRRT simulations. The solid and dashed black lines respectively correspond to $d=51.8\mu as $ and its $1\sigma$ uncertainty range (i.e., $\pm 2.3\mu as$), which are from the observation of EHT.
