    \section{Polarization structures in images of Kerr-Sen black holes }
    \label{sec:5}

   The study of polarized images of black holes provides essential constraints for probing the distribution of matter near the event horizon and the related physical processes, such as, the geometry of magnetic fields and the corresponding dynamics of magnetized plasma \cite{m87VIII,2019m87V,2024ApJ...964L..26E}, and so on. In general, the polarized emission stored in light beams can be expressed in terms of Stokes quantities $I$, $Q$, $U$, and $V$ \cite{1996AAS..117..161H,Smirnov_2011}. The quantity $I$ represents the total emission, and other quantities $Q$, $U$, and $V$ describe the polarized portion of the emission.
   With these quantities, the electric-vector position angle (EVPA) is defined as 
    \begin{equation}
        {\rm EVPA} = \frac{1}{2}{\rm arctan}(\frac{U}{Q}),
    \end{equation}
    and the linear polarization fractions $\rm |m|_{net}$ can be expressed as
    \begin{equation}         |m|_{\mathrm{net}}=\frac{\sqrt{\left(\sum_jQ_j\right)^2+\left(\sum_jU_j\right)^2}}{\sum_jI_j}, 
    \end{equation}
    where the sums are over all pixels $j$ in the images. 
    In Fig. \ref{fig:7}, we employ a simplified vertical magnetized Sub-Keplerian flow model to simulate the polarization images of Kerr-Sen black holes with different inclination angles for fixed disk thickness $H=0.3$.
