    We employ the general relativistic ray-tracing (GRRT) method to investigate the emission characteristics of radiatively inefficient accretion flow (RIAF) around Kerr-Sen black holes. Our study reveals that both the bright ring diameter and shadow size of Kerr-Sen black holes decrease monotonically as the dilaton parameter $r_2$ increases. By varying the viewing inclination and accretion disk thickness, and incorporating observational constraints from the Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) on SgrA*, we place bounds on the parameter $r_2$. The results show that, compared to the effect of inclination, disk thickness has a more significant impact on the bright ring size, thereby imposing a tighter upper limit on $r_2$. Specifically, increasing the disk thickness leads to a reduced maximum allowed value of $r_2$, and the permitted spin range shifts toward lower values under current EHT observational constraints. Moreover, to explore potential observational signatures distinguishing Kerr-Sen black holes from Kerr black holes, we compare their shadow images under different inclinations and disk thicknesses. We find that the shadow morphologies are nearly indistinguishable, making it challenging to differentiate the two purely based on imaging. Furthermore, we analyze the polarization structure near Kerr-Sen black holes by decomposing the azimuthal $m=2$ mode and calculating observable polarization quantities ($\angle\beta_2$, $|\beta_2|$). The results show that the polarization coherence power in the $m=2$ mode, $|\beta_2|$, decreases monotonically with increasing $r_2$. This decline is influenced by the black hole spin: it is more gradual at high spins ($a > 0.5$) and steeper at low spins ($a < 0.5$). At the same time, the polarization angle $\angle\beta_2$ increases monotonically with $r_2$, although the overall EVPA structure (twisting pattern) remains qualitatively unchanged.

