\title{Synergistic Motifs in linear Gaussian systems}
% \thanks{A footnote to the article title}%

\author{Enrico Caprioglio}
 \email{e.caprioglio@sussex.ac.uk}
 % \altaffiliation[Also at ]{Department of Informatics, University of Sussex, Falmer, Brighton BN1 9QH, UK}%Lines break automatically or can be forced with \\
\author{Luc Berthouze}%
 % \email{l.berthouze@sussex.ac.uk}
\affiliation{%
Department of Informatics, University of Sussex, Falmer, Brighton BN1 9QH, UK
}%


\date{\today}% It is always \today, today,
             %  but any date may be explicitly specified

\begin{abstract}
Higher-order interdependencies are central features of complex systems, yet a mechanistic explanation for their emergence remains elusive. For linear Gaussian systems of arbitrary dimension, we derive an expression for synergy-dominance in terms of signed network motifs in the system's correlation matrix. We prove that antibalanced correlational structures ensure synergy-dominance and further show that antibalanced triads in the dyadic interaction matrix of Ornstein–Uhlenbeck processes are necessary for synergy-dominance. Our results demonstrate that pairwise interactions alone can give rise to synergistic information in the absence of explicit higher-order mechanisms, and highlight structural balance theory as an instrumental conceptual framework to study higher-order interdependencies. 
\end{abstract}

% \keywords{Motifs, higher-order interdependencies}%Use showkeys class option if keyword
                              %display desired
\maketitle

Complex systems are characterized by collective properties that transcend those of their individual components \cite{Jensen2022}. In information theory (IT), these collective properties are formalized as higher-order statistical interdependencies. Information is decomposed into a low-order \textit{unique} component and two higher-order components: \textit{redundancy}, which reflects the shared information locally available from multiple sources; and \textit{synergy}, which captures information that emerges exclusively at the non-local (collective) level \cite{Williams2010, IncePED, Gutknecht2021}. Statistical synergy appears in many disparate fields—from quantum many-body systems \cite{vanEnk2023, Javarone2024} to baroque music \cite{Rosas2019}— and has been suggested as an important feature of both natural \cite{Sporns2022, Luppi2022} and artificial information processing systems \cite{Proca2024, Tax2017}. Yet, despite being observed ubiquitously, what underlying Hamiltonians or mechanisms are needed for synergistic information to emerge remains an open question \cite{Rosas2022, Neri2025}.

Recent work focused on the relationship between higher-order mechanisms and higher-order behaviours \cite{Battiston2021, Milln2025}. While higher-order mechanisms enhance the formation of synergistic information \cite{Robiglio2025}, they are not necessary for it to arise \cite{Barrett2015, Faes2017}. Synergy can be observed in systems displaying complex phenomena---such as in the disordered phase before a phase transition \cite{Marinazzo2019} and in the presence of frustration in Ising models \cite{Scagliarini2022}, or in oscillatory systems with competitive interactions \cite{Luppi2024Competitive}---without explicit higher-order interactions. Understanding how low-order mechanisms can produce statistical synergy is crucial for developing accurate models of complex behaviour and for clarifying the role of higher-order mechanisms.

Gaussian systems often serve as an analytically tractable starting point to analyse complex models. In this regime, Barrett \cite{Barrett2015} found some special cases in which synergy-dominance is ensured, offering some insights into the correlational structure and low-order mechanisms underpinning the formation of statistical synergy. For example, a system possesses positive net synergy if two elements both correlate with a third but are uncorrelated (or anticorrelated, as shown in \cite{Liardi2024}) with each other. Remarkably, it is the sign patterns of the linear relationships that ensure synergy-dominance in these cases, rather than their absolute values.

Motivated by these insights, we represent the \textit{correlational structure} of Gaussian systems as signed graphs—a mathematical framework central to structural balance theory (SBT) \cite{Harary1953, Cartwright1956}. Despite SBT's application to study information processing systems such as societies \cite{Healy1973} and the brain \cite{Saberi2024}, its connection with information theory, to the best of our knowledge, is unexplored. From the correlation matrix $\Sigma$, we define a complete weighted signed graph $G$ with adjacency matrix $W = \Sigma-\mathbb{I}$. $G$ is balanced (respectively antibalanced) if every triangle's edge-weight product is positive (respectively negative). In our notation, let $\boldsymbol{w}^k$ be the set of all closed walks of length $k$ in $G$, and for $w = (i_1,i_2,\dots,i_k,i_1)\in\boldsymbol{w}^k$ define the weight of a walk as $d(w)=\prod_{i=1}^kW_{w_i,w_{i+1}}$. Then $G$ is balanced (respectively antibalanced) if $d(w)$ is positive (respectively negative) for each $w\in\boldsymbol{w}^3$.

In this letter, we use the O-information metric $\Omega$ \cite{Rosas2019} to derive a synergistic condition for static Gaussian systems and show that, in any dimension, systems with an antibalanced correlational structure ensure synergy-dominance. Extending this to linear dynamical systems, we show analytically (for $N=3$) and numerically (for larger $N$) that antibalanced interactions in Ornstein–Uhlenbeck processes are necessary for the manifestation of synergistic information, even in the absence of explicit higher-order interactions.

We start with the definition of the O-information (short for ``information about organizational structure" and denoted as $\Omega$), which has been proposed as a readily computable measure to demarcate synergy- and redundancy-dominated systems \cite{Rosas2019}. Conveniently, $\Omega$ can be written in terms of the total correlation (TC) \cite{Watanabe1960} of the system and the TCs of its subsystems with an element removed \cite{Varley2023}
\begin{align}
    \Omega(\bX) = \sum_{i=1}^N TC(\bX_{-i}) - (N-2) TC(\bX), \label{eqn:o-info-only-tc}
\end{align}
where $\bX_{-i} = \bX\setminus{X_i}$. The TC is defined as the Kullback–Leibler divergence from the joint distribution $p(X_1,\dots,X_N)$ to the independent distribution $p(X_1)\dots p(X_N)$ (equivalently $TC(\bX)=\sum_jH(X_j)-H(\bX)$), and quantifies the amount of collective constraints in the system due to statistical interdependencies \cite{Rosas2019, Varley2023}. From this expression, $\Omega(\bX) < 0$ if constraints due to higher-order interdependencies are prevalent in the system, which are only captured by the TC of the whole (i.e., when no elements are removed). In contrast, $\Omega(\bX) > 0$ if constraints due to low-order interdependencies are dominant. These two cases can be shown to be related to the relative abundance of synergistic information and redundant information, respectively \cite{Rosas2019, Varley2023C}. To gain some insights into the patterns of relationships between variables that characterize synergy-dominated systems, we consider the linear Gaussian case, which enables the analytical treatment of the system's entropies.

For Gaussian systems, we use the differential entropy \cite{Cover2005}, $H(\bX)=\frac12 \ln\left[(2\pi{e})^N\det(\Sigma_{\bX})\right]$, to write the O-information as (see Appendix \ref{sec:gaussian-O-info})
\begin{align}
    \Omega(\bX) = \frac{N-2}{2}\log\Big[\det(\Sigma)\Big] - \frac{1}{2}\sum_i\log\Big[\det(\Sigma_{-i})\Big],
    \label{eqn:o-gaussian}
\end{align}
where $\Sigma_{-i}$ indicates the system's covariance matrix with the $i$th variable removed. To focus on the patterns of relationships between variables independent of their scale, here we study standardized covariances $\Sigma$ (positive definite matrices with ones along the diagonal i.e., correlation matrices) without loss of generality. To study Eq. (\ref{eqn:o-gaussian}), we follow a similar approach as in \cite{Barnett2009, Novelli2020}, and expand the log determinants using the matrix identities and Mercator series
\begin{equation}
    \log\big[\det(\Sigma)\big] = - \sum_{k=2}\frac{(-1)^k}{k}\tr[W^k],
    \label{eqn:log-det-approximation}
\end{equation}
where $W = \Sigma - \mathbb{I}$ and $\tr[W^1] = 0$. Importantly, this approximation converges only if the spectral radius of $W$, denoted as $\rho(W)$, is strictly less than one. As noted in \cite{Barnett2009}, this applies for the case of weakly coupled systems in which correlations are expected to be small. Noticing that $\sum_i^N\mathrm{tr}[W_{-i}^k] = (N-k)\mathrm{tr}[W^k]$ for $k=2,3$ (see Appendix \ref{sec:appendix-trace-identity}) we can use Eq. \ref{eqn:log-det-approximation} to rewrite Eq. \ref{eqn:o-gaussian} up to order three as
\begin{equation}
    \Omega(\bX) = \frac16 \tr[W^3] + \mathcal{O}(W_{ij}^4).
\end{equation}
This approximation suggests that the sign of the O-information is related to the structural balance of the system's linear relationships due to the leading term $\tr[W^3]$. Indeed, in what follows, we use SBT to prove that systems with a correlational structure represented by an antibalanced graph must be synergistic.

As per the \textit{structure theorem for antibalance}, a graph $G$ is antibalanced if it can be partitioned into two non-overlapping sets of vertices, $V_1$ and $V_2$ (at least one non-empty), such that edges between vertices in the same set are negative and edges between nodes in distinct sets are positive \cite{Harary2007}. Consequently, if a graph is antibalanced then $d(w)>0$ when $w$ is a walk of even length $k$, and $d(w)<0$ for odd-length walks. To see how we can use this, let us use Eq. \ref{eqn:log-det-approximation} to rewrite Eq. \ref{eqn:o-gaussian} as
\begin{align}
    \Omega(\bX) &= \frac16 \mathrm{tr}[W^3] + \frac{N-2}{2}\Bigg[- \frac{\mathrm{tr}[W^4]}{4} + \frac{\mathrm{tr}[W^5]}{5} \dots  \Bigg] \notag \\
    &\quad + \frac12\Bigg[\frac{\sum_i\mathrm{tr}[W_{-i}^4]}{4} - \frac{\sum_i\mathrm{tr}[W_{-i}^5]}{5} +  \dots \Bigg]. \label{eqn:O-info-expansion-no-simplification}
\end{align}
For $k>3$, unlike the $k=2,3$ cases, the sum $\sum_i\tr[W_{-i}^k]$ does not reduce to a simple function of $\tr[W^k]$. Instead, we simplify Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-info-expansion-no-simplification}) by writing $\sum_i\tr[W_{-i}^k]$ in terms of the closed walks (or motifs) in the graph representing the correlational structure of the system $W$. To do this, we write the trace of $W^k_{-i}$ as
\begin{align}
    \tr[W_{-i}^k] &= \sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k}d(w) - \sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}_i^k}d(w) \label{eqn:trace-element-removed}
\end{align}
where $\boldsymbol{w}_i^k$ is the set of closed walks of length $k$ that include element $i$ at least once, and $\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k}d(w) = \tr[W^k]$. Then, after summing Eq. (\ref{eqn:trace-element-removed}) over all elements $i$, the second term on the RHS of Eq. (\ref{eqn:trace-element-removed}) includes each walk $w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k$ with multiplicity $|w|$ in the overall sum, where $|w|$ denotes the number of distinct vertices in $w$. Thus we obtain
\begin{align}
    \sum_i\tr[W_{-i}^k] = N\tr[W^k] - \sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k}|w|d(w). \label{eqn:sum-over-i-traces-with-i-removed}
\end{align}
For $k=2,3$, each $w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k$ has exactly $k$ distinct vertices, allowing us to write Eq. (\ref{eqn:sum-over-i-traces-with-i-removed}) in terms of $\tr[W^k]$ only. However, longer closed walks can have repeated vertices, e.g., $w=\{1,2,1,2,1\}\in\boldsymbol{w}^4$ with $|w|=2$. In general, if $k$ is even each closed walk has at least $2$ distinct vertices, while if $k$ is odd each closed walk has at least $3$ distinct vertices. Substituting Eq. (\ref{eqn:sum-over-i-traces-with-i-removed}) into Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-info-expansion-no-simplification}) we obtain (full derivation in Appendix \ref{sec:link-IT-SBT})
\begin{align}
    \Omega(\bX) &= \frac12\sum_{k=3}^\infty\frac{1}{k}\Big[(-1)^{k-1}\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k}(|w|-2)d(w)\Big]. \label{eqn:O-information-final-walk-expansion}
\end{align}
This expression renders explicit the link between the O-information and the motifs in the correlational matrix of a Gaussian system. Namely, even-length closed walks with $d(w)>0$ and odd-length closed walks with $d(w)<0$ contribute synergistically, while even-length closed walks with $d(w)<0$ and odd-length closed walks with $d(w)>0$ contribute redundantly. Therefore, Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-information-final-walk-expansion}) is minimized if $G$ is antibalanced, since for every closed walk $w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k$ in $G$ we have $(-1)^{k-1}d(w)\leq0$, ensuring the system to be synergy-dominated. In the Appendix \ref{sec:link-IT-SBT} Fig. \ref{fig:o-info-final-walk-approximation} we show how this expansion approximates Eq. (\ref{eqn:o-gaussian}).

Our first result highlights the role of antibalanced correlational structures in synergistic Gaussian systems and provides a sufficient condition for synergy-dominance. We now explicitly consider what kind of patterns of interactions in linear processes can lead to synergistic correlational structures. Following a similar approach as in \cite{Barnett2009}, we consider continuous Ornstein-Ulenbech (OU) processes which can be written as
\begin{equation}
    d\bX(t) = -\bX(t)\cdot(\mathbb{I} - A)dt + d\boldsymbol{\mathcal{W}}(t) \label{eqn:OU-process}
\end{equation}
where $A$ is the interaction matrix, $\mathbb{I}$ is the identity matrix and $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{W}}$ is a Wiener process with covariance matrix equal to $\mathbb{I}$. If $A$ is symmetric and Schur stable, the covariance matrix of the resulting dynamics from Eq. (\ref{eqn:OU-process}) can be written as \cite{Barnett2009} (see also \cite{Novelli2020, Lizier2023}):
\begin{equation}
    \Sigma^\star = \frac12(\mathbb{I} - A)^{-1}. \label{eqn:OU-solution}
\end{equation}
Then, the correlation matrix can be written as $\Sigma = D^{-1}\Sigma^{\star}D^{-1}$ where $(D^{-1})_{ij} = 1/\sqrt{(\Sigma^\star)_{ij}}$ if $i=j$ and $0$ otherwise. With this expression for $\Sigma$ in terms of the interaction matrix $A$, we can use our previous results to show what interaction matrices lead to synergy-dominated systems.

Let us consider a system of size $N=3$ with no self-interactions, such that $A_{ii}=0$. Then, $\Sigma^\star = \frac{1}{2T}C$ where $T=\det(\mathbb{I}-A)$ and $C=\mathrm{adj}(\mathbb{I}-A)^{\mathsf{T}}$ is the adjugate of $\mathbb{I}-A$, while $(D^{-1})_{ii}=\left(\frac{2T}{C_{ii}}\right)^{1/2}$. Thus, each element of $\Sigma$ can be written as
\begin{equation}
    \Sigma_{ij} = 
\begin{cases}
    \frac{C_{ij}}{\sqrt{C_{ii}C_{jj}}} & \text{if } i \neq j \\
    1 & \text{if } i = j 
\end{cases} \label{eqn:sigma-elements-OU-process_N-3}
\end{equation}
where for every pair $i,j\in\{1,2,3\},\;i\neq{j},$ and $k = \{1,2,3\}\setminus\{i,j\}$ we have $C_{ii} = 1-A_{jk}^2> 0$, and $C_{ij} = A_{ij} + A_{ik}A_{jk}$.

Now, consider the synergistic condition ($\Omega < 0$) for general correlation matrices of size $3$. For simplicity, let $a=\Sigma_{12},b=\Sigma_{13},c=\Sigma_{23}$. Then from Eq. (\ref{eqn:o-gaussian}) the system is synergy dominated if
\begin{equation}
    2abc < (ab)^2+(ac)^2+(bc)^2 - (abc)^2 \label{eqn:N-3-synergistic-condition}
\end{equation}
where the RHS is always greater than zero since $|a|,|b|,|c| < 1$. In general, the antibalanced correlational structure $abc < 0$ is a sufficient condition for $\Omega<0$, as expected, and some solutions exist for $abc\geq 0$ (not shown). In what follows, we demonstrate that antibalanced interaction patterns are necessary for the system to be synergistic, covering the full spectrum of synergistic solutions.

To do this, we return to the interaction matrix $A$ and substitute the elements from Eq. (\ref{eqn:sigma-elements-OU-process_N-3}), written in terms of $A$, into the condition in Eq. (\ref{eqn:N-3-synergistic-condition}). To simplify the following expressions we let $A_{12}=x, A_{13}=y, A_{23}=z$. With some algebraic manipulations (see Appendix \ref{sec:dynamical-systems-appendix-N-3}), Eq. (\ref{eqn:N-3-synergistic-condition}) can be shown to reduce to the form
\begin{align}
    \frac{k_1^2k_2}{(1-x^2)(1-y^2)(1-z^2)}>0 \label{eqn:DS-synergistic-condition-N-3-simplification}
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
    k_1 &= (-1 + x^2 + y^2 + 2 x y z + z^2), \notag \\
    k_2 &= (x y z)^2 - (x y)^2 - (x z)^2 - (y z)^2 - 2 x y z. \notag
\end{align}
Since the denominator in Eq. (\ref{eqn:DS-synergistic-condition-N-3-simplification}) is always positive and non-zero, and the $k_1$ term is squared, the synergistic condition reduces to $k_2>0$, or equivalently
\begin{align}
    -2xyz > (xy)^2 + (xz)^2 + (yz)^2 - (xyz)^2. \label{eqn:DS-synergistic-condition-N-3}
\end{align}
Note the RHS of Eq. (\ref{eqn:DS-synergistic-condition-N-3}) is always greater than zero since $|x|,|y|,|z|<1$. Thus, for Eq. (\ref{eqn:DS-synergistic-condition-N-3}) to be satisfied we require $-2xyz>0$, i.e., for the system to be synergistic we require the interaction matrix $A$ to be antibalanced.

We numerically confirm this result via a numerical parameter exploration of $x,y,z$ (Fig. \ref{fig:OU-N-3-results}). We compare the closed form solutions for $\Omega(\bX)$, obtained by substituting Eq. (\ref{eqn:sigma-elements-OU-process_N-3}) into Eq. (\ref{eqn:o-gaussian}), with the numerical O-information, denoted as $\Omega(\bX)^\star$, computed using the correlation matrix obtained from the simulated OU-process.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figures/fig1_N_3.pdf}
    \caption{Numerical $\Omega(\boldsymbol{X})^{\star}$ (left) and theoretical $\Omega(\boldsymbol{X})$ (right) results for $3$-dimensional Ornstein–Uhlenbeck (OU) processes with varying interaction matrix elements $A_{12}=x,A_{13}=y$ and fixed $A_{23}=z = 0.25$. Insets depict the unweighted complete signed graphs, with adjacency matrix $\text{sign}(A)$, corresponding to each quadrant. Solid (dashed) edges indicate positive (negative) connections. Graphs are unlabeled since $\Omega(\boldsymbol{X})$ is order invariant.}
    \label{fig:OU-N-3-results}
\end{figure}

Next, we asked whether antibalanced interaction structures promote the emergence of synergistic information in larger dynamical systems. For systems of arbitrary dimension $N$ the elements of $\Sigma$ can be written in the same form as in Eq. (\ref{eqn:sigma-elements-OU-process_N-3}). However, each $C_{ij}$ is much more complex in higher-dimensions, as it consists of the minor of $A$ multiplied by its cofactor, making analytical insights much harder to obtain. Consequently, we performed extensive numerical explorations for systems of size $N\in[4,5,6,7,8]$. Since the O-information is order invariant, for each $N$ we studied all possible non-isomorphic complete signed graphs (graphs were constructed using the dataset in \cite{mckay_dataset}, see also Appendix \ref{sec:appendix-OU-process-methodology}). This allowed us to systematically study all possible systems of size $N$ and determine the most synergistic interaction structures. We present our results in Fig. \ref{fig:N-8-OU-process-results} for $N=8$ (see Appendix \ref{sec:appendix-extra-figures} for other system's sizes).

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figures/new_fig2_1000.png}
     \caption{Mean $\Omega(\boldsymbol{X})$ as a function of the number of antibalanced triangles in the interaction matrix. The lower bound indicates the lowest $\Omega(\bX)$ encountered in our numerical exploration across all configurations with a specific no. of antibalanced triangles. Each data point for the mean $\Omega(\bX)$ is coloured according to its corresponding mean structural energy value (Eq. (\ref{eqn:structural-energy})), interpreted here as a measure of (anti)balance. Lower bounds are coloured with the energy value of the configuration that resulted in the lowest recorded $\Omega(\bX)$, not to be confused with the lowest recorded energy (not shown). Each interaction matrix $A$ was generated with spectral radius $\rho(A) = 0.9$ (see also Appendix \ref{sec:appendix-OU-process-methodology}).}
    \label{fig:N-8-OU-process-results}
\end{figure}

For any $N$ we studied, we found that the O-information is related to the number of antibalanced triangles or synergistic motifs in the interaction matrix $A$. In particular, balanced interaction structures (with $0$ antibalanced triangles) always result in redundancy-dominated systems, while antibalanced structures (with $N(N-1)(N-2)/3$ antibalanced triangles) consistently result in systems with the lowest mean $\Omega$.

In summary, we proved that antibalanced pairwise interactions are necessary for synergy‐dominance in linear Gaussian systems without higher-order mechanisms, and that antibalanced correlational structures ensure synergy-dominance. In doing so, we reveal SBT as an instrumental conceptual lens for studying higher‐order interdependencies. Our analytically informed interpretation of the relation between SBT and IT is in line with observations of synergy in the classical Ising model and in coupled oscillatory systems, in the absence of higher-order mechanisms. In the Ising case, synergy peaks at the onset of the disorder-order phase transition \cite{Marinazzo2019}---driven by the emergence of conflicting spin alignments \cite{Scagliarini2022}. Similarly, competitive interactions in oscillatory systems were found to be crucial for the emergence of synergistic subsystems \cite{Luppi2024Competitive}. Taken together, our results demonstrate that purely pairwise interactions can give rise to higher-order behaviours and that these behaviours can be detected by simple correlational structures (motifs), thereby advancing our understanding of the relationship between mechanisms and behaviours in complex systems.

The expansion of the O-information (Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-information-final-walk-expansion})) in terms of signed motifs of the correlation matrix shows that antibalanced correlational structures minimize $\Omega$. This result can be applied directly to empirical functional datasets where the Gaussian assumption holds---such as in whole-brain functional studies---to identify the most synergistic subsystems (but not all, see Eq. (\ref{eqn:N-3-synergistic-condition})), possibly improving current methods based on simulated annealing \cite{Varley2023} or exhaustive \cite{Varley2023C} search.

Beyond applications, our theory could bridge the gap between approaches investigating higher-order phenomena and indicate novel research directions: ($i$) it clarifies how purely pairwise (low-order) mechanisms can give rise to higher-order interdependencies (synergy); ($ii$) since the normalized leading term in Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-information-final-walk-expansion}) is equivalent to the SBT measure of a network's structural energy \cite{Marvel2009}
\begin{equation}
    U=\binom{N}{3}^{-1}\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^3}\sqrt[3]{d(w)},\label{eqn:structural-energy}
\end{equation}
our theory can explain why information-theoretic studies, such as \cite{Luppi2022, Varley2023, Varley2023C}, reach similar conclusions as in SBT-based neuroscience studies, such as \cite{Saberi2024}. Finally, it will be interesting to uncover the network-level dynamics---distinct from dynamics on networks with fixed topology---that lead to the formation of antibalanced interaction structures. In social balance theory, the formation of balanced triangles has largely been linked to higher-order mechanisms (e.g., triadic interactions), with only recent work showing that a simple pairwise homophily-based mechanism suffices \cite{Pham2022}. Whether similar network mechanisms exist for the antibalanced case is an interesting and unexplored research question.

