\subsection{Walk expansion and link with SBT}\label{sec:link-IT-SBT}
Note that we can rewrite Eq. (\ref{eqn:sum-over-i-traces-with-i-removed}) from the main text as
\begin{align}
    \sum_i\tr[W_{-i}^k] = (N-2)\tr[W^k] - \sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k}(|w| - 2)d(w) \label{eqn:sum-over-i-traces-with-i-removed-v2}
\end{align}
since each closed walk $w$ has at least $2$ distinct vertices for even $k$ and at least $3$ distinct vertices for odd $k$ (recall also $\tr[W^k]=\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k}d(w)$). Then, from Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-info-expansion-no-simplification}), we substitute in Eq. (\ref{eqn:sum-over-i-traces-with-i-removed-v2}) to obtain
\begin{align*}
    \Omega(\bX) = \frac16\tr[W^3] &+ \frac12\Bigg\{-\frac14\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^4}(|w|-2)d(w) -\frac16\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^6}(|w|-2)d(w)- \dots\Bigg\} \\
    &+ \frac12\Bigg\{+ \frac15\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^5}(|w|-2)d(w) + \frac17\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^7}(|w|-2)d(w) + \dots\Bigg\}.
\end{align*}
This expression can be more concisely written as
\begin{align*}
    \Omega(\bX) &= \frac16\tr[W^3] + \frac12\sum_{k=4}^\infty\frac{1}{k}\Big[(-1)^{k-1}\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k}(|w|-2)d(w)\Big], \\
    &= \frac12\sum_{k=3}^\infty\frac{1}{k}\Big[(-1)^{k-1}\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k}(|w|-2)d(w)\Big]
\end{align*}
since $\tr[W^3]=\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^3}d(w)$ and each walk $w\in\boldsymbol{w}^3$ has $|w|=3$. Note, this expression is minimized if the correlational structure is antibalanced, since in this case $d(w)$ is negative for odd-length closed walks and positive for even-length closed walks. In Fig. \ref{fig:o-info-final-walk-approximation} we show how Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-information-final-walk-expansion}) approximates the O-information.
\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{figures/appendix/o-info-final-walk-approximation.png}
    \caption{Mean relative error between $\Omega$ computed using Eq. (\ref{eqn:o-info-only-tc}) and Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-information-final-walk-expansion}) up to walks of length $k=7$ for different spectral radii $\rho$ of $W$.}
    \label{fig:o-info-final-walk-approximation}
\end{figure}
In general, it should be noted that, since each odd-length closed walk has at least $3$ distinct vertices, in Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-information-final-walk-expansion}) each of these walks is counted at least once. This is not the case for even-length closed walks since there exist many closed walks with only $2$ distinct vertices which disappear in Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-information-final-walk-expansion}). Then, it may be useful to rewrite Eq. (\ref{eqn:O-information-final-walk-expansion}) as
\begin{align}
    \Omega(\bX) &= \frac12 \Bigg[\sum_{\substack{k=3 \\ \text{odd }k}} \frac{1}{k}\tr[W^k] - \sum_{\substack{k=4 \\ \text{even }k}} \frac{1}{k}\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k}(|w|-2)d(w) + \sum_{\substack{k=5 \\ \text{odd }k}} \frac{1}{k}\sum_{w\in\boldsymbol{w}^k}(|w|-3)d(w) \Bigg].
\end{align}
In this expression, the sign of the first summation term (order $3$ leading terms) is very similar to the structural energy metric $U$ used in \cite{Marvel2009, Saberi2024}.

\section{Dynamical Systems}
\subsection{Synergy-dominance condition}\label{sec:dynamical-systems-appendix-N-3}
After substituting the elements from Eq. (\ref{eqn:sigma-elements-OU-process_N-3}) into the condition in Eq. (\ref{eqn:N-3-synergistic-condition}) the LHS of Eq. (\ref{eqn:N-3-synergistic-condition}) becomes
\begin{align}
    2abc = 2\frac{(x+yz)(y+xz)(z+xy)}{(1-x^2)(1-y^2)(1-z^2)}
\end{align}
while the RHS
\begin{align*}
    (ab)^2+(ac)^2+(bc)^2-(abc)^2 &= \frac{(x+yz)^2(y+xz)^2}{(1-x^2)(1-y^2)(1-z^2)^2} + \frac{(x+yz)^2(z+xy)^2}{(1-x^2)(1-y^2)^2(1-z^2)} \\
    & \quad + \frac{(y+xz)^2(z+xy)^2}{(1-x^2)^2(1-y^2)(1-z^2)} + \frac{(x+yz)^2(y+xz)^2(z+xy)^2}{(1-x^2)^2(1-y^2)^2(1-z^2)^2}.  \\
\end{align*}
To simplify these, we multiply both sides by the common denominator $(1-x^2)^2(1-y^2)^2(1-z^2)^2$ which is always positive and non-zero. Taking the difference between the RHS and LHS we obtain
\begin{align*}
    &(1 - x^2) (1 - y^2) (x + y z)^2 (y + x z)^2 + (1 - x^2) (1 - z^2) (x + y z)^2 (z + x y)^2 + (1 - y^2) (1 - z^2) (z + x y)^2 (y + x z)^2 \\
    &\quad\quad - (z + x y)^2 (y + x z)^2 (x + y z)^2 - 2 (z + x y) (y + x z) (x + y z) (1 - x^2) (1 - y^2) (1 - z^2) > 0 \\
\end{align*}
This expression can be written in the form
\begin{align*}
    k_1^2k_2>0 
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
    k_1 &= (-1 + x^2 + y^2 + 2 x y z + z^2), \notag \\
    k_2 &= (x y z)^2 - (x y)^2 - (x z)^2 - (y z)^2 - 2 x y z. \notag
\end{align*}
Since the first factor is always positive, we require $k_2$ to be positive for the condition to be satisfied, as shown in the main text.

\subsection{OU process results}
\subsubsection{Methodology}\label{sec:appendix-OU-process-methodology}
To test how the O-information depends on the antibalanced structures in the interaction matrix of OU processes we studied the statistics of the resulting OU process dynamics using all possible sign configurations in the interaction matrix $A$. More precisely, we computed $\Omega$ from the covariance matrix $\Sigma^\star$ of a OU process using all possible non-isomorphic complete signed graphs as interactions couplings. Note, there are many ways to sample $A$. Here, we show additional results for interaction matrices with different average spectral radii and with or without self-interactions. Importantly, our results remain unaffected.

Note, here we studied all possible complete signed interactions networks (all-to-all couplings) since this can be done systematically. Studying all possible non-isomorphic signed graphs, including non-complete structures, is also possible. However, for non-complete graphs it is unclear how this can be done systematically, since there can be disconnected subgraphs and equivalent structures across scales. Additionally, the number of non-complete signed graphs increases with $N$ even faster than for the case for complete graphs.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{figures/appendix/all_triangles_configs.pdf}
    \caption{(\textbf{A}) All possible non-isomorphic graphs of size $N=3$ (unsigned, non-complete). (\textbf{B}) All possible complete signed graphs of size $N=3$. The product of the edges in the balanced triangles is positive, while the product of the edges in the antibalanced triangles is negative.)}
    \label{fig:triangle-configurations}
\end{figure}

Let $\mathbf{S}(N)$ be the set of adjacency matrices of all possible non-isomorphic unweighted complete signed graphs. For $N=3$ there are $4$ of these structures (Fig. \ref{fig:triangle-configurations}), for $N=7$ there are $1044$, and for $N=8$ there are $12346$ (see for instance the \href{https://oeis.org/A000088}{On-line Encyclopedia of large integer sequences}, in what follows we used the dataset available \href{https://users.cecs.anu.edu.au/~bdm/data/graphs.html}{here}). Note, from the set of all non-isomorphic unsigned graphs we can obtain the set of all non-isomorphic complete signed graphs by replacing each missing edge with a negative edge (see Fig. \ref{fig:triangle-configurations}). Each complete signed graph of size $N$ has $N(N-1)(N-2)/3$ triangles. In what follows, we use the variable $m$ to denote the number of antibalanced triangles. Importantly, two distinct structures $S_i$ and $S_j$ can have the same number of antibalanced triangles (for instance, see Fig. \ref{fig:N-4-OU-process}). For each of these structures we generated $1000$ Schur stable matrices and computed the O-information of the theoretical expected covariance matrix $\Sigma^\star$ of the resulting dynamics.

To generate a Schur stable matrices with desired sign patterns, we can sample a random symmetric matrix $M$, multiply it element-wise with the adjacency matrix $S_i\in\mathbf{S}(N)$ of the desired sign configuration, and then divide each element by the largest absolute eigenvalue to ensure stability, such that
\begin{equation}
    A = \frac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{max}}}(M \circ S_i),
\end{equation}
where $\circ$ denotes element-wise multiplication between matrices. This method ensures that the largest absolute eigenvalue of the resulting interaction matrix $A$ will be less than (but very close to) unity, i.e., its spectral radius $\rho(A) < 1$. To study interaction matrices with smaller spectral radii we can add a  parameter $\epsilon$, such that $A = (M \circ S_i) / (\epsilon + \lambda_{\mathrm{max}})$. For Fig. \ref{fig:N-8-OU-process-results} in the main text we use $\epsilon = 0.4$ to fix the spectral radius of the interaction matrices for $N=8$ to be $\rho(A) = 0.907 \pm 0.009$. To see how the spectral radius affects the O-information, see Fig. \ref{fig:N-7-OU-process} ($\epsilon = 0.4$) and \ref{fig:N-7-OU-process-small-epsilon} ($\epsilon = 0.01$) for the case for $N=7$.

Using this procedure, we collected data for $N\in[4,5,6,7,8]$, which we show in Fig. (\ref{fig:N-5-OU-process}, \ref{fig:N-6-OU-process}, \ref{fig:N-7-OU-process} and \ref{fig:N-8-OU-process-results}). As noted earlier, two distinct signed graphs can have the same number of antibalanced triangles. This can be seen clearly for the case for $N=4$ (Fig. \ref{fig:N-4-OU-process}) where $3$ structures have $m=0$, $3$ structures have $m=4$ and $5$ structures have $m=2$. Let $s(m)$ be the number of samples collected for each $m$. The data here is the resulting $\Omega$ from each sampled configuration matrix with $m$ antibalanced triangles. Then for the case for $N=4$ we have $s(0) = 3000, s(4) = 3000$ and $s(2) = 5000$. To avoid sample size effects we only used $3000$ samples for each $m$ to compute the statistics we reported (similar for the cases with larger $N$).

