\subsection{Detection algorithm}\label{sec_testing_algorithm}
Inspired by the theoretical observations in Section \ref{sec_testing_bootstrap}, we propose the following statistics.
\begin{equation}\label{eq_testing_statistics}
             \mathbb{T}_i:=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^K\Big(\frac{\lambda_i^{(j)}}{\hat{\theta}_i}-1\Big)^2.
         \end{equation}
Recap the test problem \eqref{eq_hypothesistest} in Section \ref{sec_motivation}. We propose a novel algorithm that leverages a fluctuation magnification mechanism to detect potentially spurious signals and thereby determine the true number of common factors. To achieve this, we conduct a two-round testing procedure. In the first round, we apply the fluctuation magnification mechanism to identify spurious signals from the sample matrix $S$ without distinguishing outliers in advance. As a result, some bulk eigenvalues (non-outliers) may also be flagged as spurious. In the second round, we verify whether any bulk components were mistakenly identified as spurious by employing standard methods such as \cite{onatski2010determining}, thus refining the selection of common factors.

\textbf{1. First round testing.} 
We now turn to the first round. Under Assumption \ref{ass_sigma}, it suffices to focus on the largest spurious signal under the alternative hypothesis $\mathbf{H}_a$.  As noted in Remark \ref{rem_bootstrap}, it is crucial to choose an appropriate distribution for $w$ to ensure the validity of Lemma \ref{lem_fakesignals_bootstrapped}. Typically, $w\in U[a,b]$ satisfies the following conditions.
    \begin{itemize}\label{eq_condition_w}
    \item [(1)] $(a+b)/2=1$;
    \item [(2)] $a\ge b\xi^2_{(2)}/\xi^2_{(1)}$;
    \item [(3)] $\sigma_m\gg\mathsf{T}b$.
\end{itemize}


However, in practice, $\xi^2_{(1)},\xi^2_{(2)}$ are unobservable, and $\sigma_m$ is difficult to determine. To solve this issue, we approximate the ratio $\xi^2_{(2)}/\xi^2_{(1)}$ using $\lambda_{m+2}/\lambda_{m+1}$, and $\sigma_m/\mathsf{T}$ using $\lambda_{m}/\lambda_{m+1}$. The validity of the first approximation is ensured by Theorem \ref{thm_fakesignal_limit}, while Lemma \ref{lem_realsignal_secondratio1} and Theorem \ref{thm_realsignal_clt} support the second. The algorithm can be summarized as follows.
        \begin{algorithm}[H]
            \caption{First round detection for spurious signals}\label{alg_firstround_bootstrap}
            \normalsize
            \begin{flushleft}
                \noindent{\bf Inputs:} Pre-given integer $o$, original data matrix $Y$, sufficiently large $K\equiv K(n)$.

                \noindent{\bf Step One:} Calculate the eigenvalues of $S=YY^{\prime}$ as $\lambda_1\ge\lambda_2\ge\dots\ge\lambda_{n}\ge0$. For $i=1,\dots,o$, we generate $w_{i}\sim U[a_i,b_i]$ satisfying: (1). $(a_i+b_i)/2=1$; (2). $b_i/a_i\le\lambda_i/\lambda_{i+1}$; (3). $b_i\log n\times\lambda_{i}/\lambda_{i-1}<1$, where we set $\lambda_{0}=\log^2 n\lambda_1$.

                \noindent{\bf Step Two:} For each $1\le i\le o$,
                generate $K$ i.i.d. $n\times  n$ diagonal matrices $W^{(j)}_i, j=1,\dots,K$ with entries independently sampled from $w_i\sim\mathrm{U}[a_i,b_i]$. Compute the associated matrices after fluctuation magnification $\widetilde{S}_{i}^{(j)}=YW^{(j)}_iY^{\prime}$ and the sequential eigenvalues $\lambda_i^{(j)}$, where $\lambda_i^{(j)}$ is the $i$-th largest eigenvalue of $\widetilde{S}_i^{(j)}$.

                \noindent{\bf Step Three:} Compute $\mathbb{T}_i=K^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^K(\lambda_i^{(j)}-K^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^K\lambda_i^{(j)})^2/(K^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^K\lambda_i^{(j)})^2$ for each $1\le i\le o$. Set the thresholds $\{\mathsf{L}_i\}_{1\le i\le o}$ according to $\{\lambda_i\}_{1\le i\le o}$ as $\mathsf{L}_i:=\log^2 n/n\times\mathbbm{1}(\lambda_i\le n^{1/2})+\log^2 n/n^{1-\tau_i}\times\mathbbm{1}(\lambda_i>n^{1/2})$ where $\tau_i:=\lceil\log\lambda_i/\log n\rceil$.
                Count $\widehat{\mathsf{f}}=\#\{i:\mathbb{T}_i>\mathsf{L}_i\}, 1\le i\le o$.

                \noindent{\bf Output:}  Reject $\mathbf{H}_0$ if $\widehat{\mathsf{f}}>0$.
            \end{flushleft}
        \end{algorithm}
        \begin{remark}
            Several remarks are presented in order. Firstly, based on the theoretical analysis in Section \ref{sec_testing_bootstrap}, it holds that
            \begin{gather}\label{eq_firstround_consistency}
                \lim_{n,K\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{P}(\mathbb{T}_i\le\mathsf{L}_i)=1,\;\text{under $\mathbf{H}_0$ for $1\le i\le m$;}\\
                \lim_{n,K\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{P}(\mathbb{T}_i>\mathsf{L}_i)=1,\;\text{under $\mathbf{H}_a$ for $m+1\le i\le o$}.
            \end{gather}
            %Since Corollary \ref{col_realsignals_bootstrapped} indicates that real signals will not exceed the error level $\mathrm{O}(n^{-1}\log n)$.
            In practice, we choose $K=n^3$ according to the Lemma \ref{lem_fakesignals_bootstrapped}.

            Secondly, one can also choose other random variables $w$ as magnifiers, such as binomial random variable satisfying 
         \begin{equation}
             w=
             \begin{cases}
                 a\quad \mathbb{P}(w=a)=p\\
                 b\quad \mathbb{P}(w=b)=1-p
             \end{cases},
         \end{equation}
         with parallel specified conditions as in Algorithm \ref{alg_firstround_bootstrap}.

            Thirdly, under Assumptions \ref{ass_sigma} and \ref{ass_xi}, we have $\mathbb{T}_i\ge \mathsf{L}_i$ for all $m+1\le i\le o$, indicating that heavy-tailed randomness indeed contributes to the emergence of spurious noise signals. However, since the convolution of randomness of $U$ and $D$ blurs the threshold between outliers (spurious noise signals) and bulk components (non-signals), potentially causing some bulk components to be misclassified as spurious signals. To achieve a more accurate estimation of the number of common factors, we introduce an auxiliary algorithm, which constitutes the core of the second-round testing procedure.

            %On the other hand, the convolution of randomness of $U$ and $D$ may obscure the threshold between outliers (fake signals) and bulk components (non-signals). This means we can only confirm the presence of fake signals but cannot determine their exact count, as weak fake signals may overlap with bulk components. Fortunately, we can still infer the number of common factors by leveraging the largest spurious signals as a threshold reference, while necessitating an auxiliary algorithm to calibrate a secure upper bound on their quantity. The integration of these two components—thresholding via maximal spurious signals and bounding via auxiliary  algorithm—forms the cornerstone of our proposed second round testing framework, which we detail next.

            Finally, we believe our fluctuation magnification strategy can be applied to detection spurious signals in more general cases, as long as the real signals are significantly large.
        \end{remark}

\textbf{2. Second round testing.}
The first-round detection identifies the most prominent spurious signals. Based on this, we enhance the performance of common factor analysis in heavy-tailed datasets by incorporating the first-round estimates. Specifically, we integrate Algorithm \ref{alg_firstround_bootstrap} with the methodology of \cite{onatski2010determining} (further developed in \cite{Bao2015general,ding2021spiked}) to obtain a more accurate estimate of the number of common factors in elliptical models with potentially heavy-tailed randomness. The algorithm proposed below leverages both the strengths of our first-round results and the robustness of established factor selection methods, effectively addressing the challenges posed by heavy-tailed distributions.

Denote the following statistics
\begin{equation*}
     \mathbb{G}_i:=\frac{\lambda_i-\lambda_{i+1}}{\lambda_{i+1}-\lambda_{i+2}},\quad i=1,\dots, n;
\end{equation*}
Define $r^*$ as 
\begin{equation*}
    r^*:=\inf_{1\le i\le o}\{i:\mathbb{T}_i>\mathsf{L}_i\},
\end{equation*}
from Algorithm \ref{alg_firstround_bootstrap}. Besides, we define the averaged shifting threshold,
\begin{equation*}
    \delta_n^{(i)}:=\frac{1}{r^*-1}\sum_{1\le j\le r^*\atop j\neq i}\mathbb{G}_j.
\end{equation*}

We propose the following complementary algorithm.
 \begin{algorithm}[H]
            \caption{Second round estimation for the number of common factors}\label{alg_secondround_estrealsignal}
            \normalsize
            \begin{flushleft}
                \noindent{\bf Inputs:} $r^*$, sample covariance matrix $S$ with its leading $r^*+1$ eigenvalues $\lambda_1\ge\lambda_2\ge\dots\ge\lambda_{r^*+1}$. 

                \noindent{\bf Step One:} Calculate and plot $\mathbb{G}_i,1\le i\le r^*-1$.  Calculate each $\delta_n^{(i)}, 1\le i\le r^*-1$.

                \noindent{\bf Step Two:}  Set $\widehat{r}=\sup_{1\le i\le r^*-1}\{i:\mathbb{G}_i\ge\delta_n^{(i)}\}$ if the set is non-empty. Otherwise, set $\widehat{r}=r^*-1$.

                \noindent{\bf Output:} Estimating the number of common factors at $\widehat{r}$.
            \end{flushleft}
        \end{algorithm}
    \begin{lemma}\label{lem_secondround_consistency}
        Under assumptions in Corollary \ref{col_realsignals_bootstrapped} and Lemma \ref{lem_fakesignals_bootstrapped}, based on Algorithm \ref{alg_firstround_bootstrap} and Algorithm \ref{alg_secondround_estrealsignal}, we have 
        \begin{equation}
            \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{P}^*(\widehat{r}=m)=1.
        \end{equation}
    \end{lemma}
    \begin{remark}
       Several remarks are in order. First, Algorithm \ref{alg_secondround_estrealsignal} incorporates the method developed in \cite{onatski2010determining} as an auxiliary input. This serves as a correction to our initial results when the first detected spurious noise signal lies within the bulk spectrum—indicating, in other words, that the sample $Y$ contains only light-tailed randomness. In such cases, applying the method from \cite{onatski2010determining} yields an accurate estimate of the number of common factors. The consistency of our overall methodology is established in Lemma \ref{lem_secondround_consistency}, with the proof deferred to Section \ref{sec_proof_testing procedures}. Second, the use of \cite{onatski2010determining} in Algorithm \ref{alg_secondround_estrealsignal} is primarily due to its simplicity. It can readily be replaced by other well-established methods in the literature, such as those in \cite{bai2018consistency}. 
    \end{remark}

\section{Simulation}\label{sec_simulation}
In this section, we conduct simulation studies to validate two main aspects:
(1) The overestimation of the number of common factors in EFM when noise exhibits heavy-tailed randomness, confirming the existence of spurious signals.
(2) The effectiveness of our fluctuation magnification approach (referred to as the detection algorithm in Section 5.2) in distinguishing spurious signals and correctly identifying the true number of common factors in such cases. 
Consequently, two scenarios of heavy-tailed data $\mathbf{y}$ from the model \eqref{eq_intro_elldistr} are analyzed:
(I) A typical heavy-tailed case, where $\mathbf{y}$ follows a distribution with a polynomial decay tail satisfying $\alpha \in (2, +\infty)$.
(II) A serious heavy-tailed case, where $\mathbf{y}$ follows a distribution with a polynomial decay tail satisfying $\alpha \in (1, 2]$. 
We focus on the strength of the heavy tail that arises from polynomial decay tails rather than exponential decay tails, as the former allows easier adjustment of the strength of the heavy tail via the parameter $\alpha$.



\subsection{Comparison of methodologies in common factor selection: Typical heavy-tailed case}

We first establish the settings for the typical heavy-tailed case for $\mathbf{y}$ in \eqref{eq_intro_elldistr}, characterized by a polynomial decay tail with $\alpha \in (2, +\infty)$. To represent various scenarios of heavy-tailed data, we employ multivariate t-distributions with degrees of freedom set at 4.3, 4.8, and 5.3 (i.e., $t(4.3)$, $t(4.8)$, and $t(5.3)$, respectively), all of which satisfy the condition $\alpha > 2$. We consider a high-dimensional framework with $p=1000$ (number of variables) and $n=1000$ (number of observations). Our analysis focuses on two population covariance matrices $\Sigma$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] $\Sigma_{\mathrm{I}} = \operatorname{diag}\{12, 6, 1, \ldots, 1\}$
    \item[(ii)] $\Sigma_{\mathrm{II}} = \operatorname{diag}\{24, 12, 6, 1, \ldots, 1\}$.
\end{itemize}
Here, the larger diagonal components represent the distinct common factors. It is clear that $\Sigma_{I}$ encompasses two common factors induced by $\{12, 6\}$, while $\Sigma_{II}$ encompasses three common factors induced by $\{24, 12, 6\}$.

We compare our methodology with that developed by \cite{onatski2010determining}. For clarity, we briefly introduce the testing algorithm by \cite{onatski2010determining}.
\begin{algorithm}[H]
            \caption{Estimation for the number of common factors based on \cite{onatski2010determining}}\label{alg_onatski}
            \normalsize
            \begin{flushleft}
                \noindent{\bf Inputs:} Pre-given integer $o$, threshold $\nu_n=\lceil\log n\rceil$, sample covariance matrix $S$ with its leading $o$ eigenvalues $\lambda_1\ge\lambda_2\ge\dots\ge\lambda_{o}$. 

                \noindent{\bf Step One:} Calculate $\mathbb{G}_i,1\le i\le o$.

                \noindent{\bf Step Two:} Find least $\widehat{r}$ such that $\mathbb{G}_{\widehat{r}}>\nu_n$, i.e. $\widehat{r}:=\inf_{1\le i\le o}\{i:\mathbb{G}_i>\nu_n\}$. 

                \noindent{\bf Output:} Estimating the number of common factors at $\widehat{r}$.
            \end{flushleft}
        \end{algorithm}
        In the following, we denote the testing methodology based on Algorithm \ref{alg_onatski} as "On", and our methodology developed in Section \ref{sec_testing_algorithm} as "Ma". In our simulations, to reduce computational load, we set the magnifiers $w_{i} \sim U[0.1, 1.9]$ in Algorithm \ref{alg_firstround_bootstrap} and $K=1000$ in Algorithm \ref{alg_secondround_estrealsignal}, which yielded sufficiently good results. Detailed experimental results are presented in Figures \ref{eig4.3} and  \ref{eig4.33} and Tables \ref{tab:1} and \ref{tab:2}.



Specifically, in Figures \ref{eig4.3} and  \ref{eig4.33}, the top row (a) displays the first 50 eigenvalues. The middle row (b) presents the statistics $\mathbb{G}_i$ and the estimator $\widehat{r}$ from "On". The bottom row (c) demonstrates the identification of spurious signals using our proposed statistics $\mathbb{T}_i$ in Algorithm \ref{alg_firstround_bootstrap}, where the statistics associated with spurious signals show a significantly larger value compared to real spikes. We compare behavior in scenarios where spurious signals emerge (left columns, labeled (1)) and where they do not (right columns, labeled (2)). These cases highlight the efficiency of Algorithm \ref{alg_firstround_bootstrap} in detecting spurious signals and its robustness in scenarios without spurious signals. For (c1) and (c2), the y-axis has identical starting and ending points with equal spacing to ensure a fair and visual comparison of the variance after fluctuation magnification.  Figures \ref{eig4.3} and  \ref{eig4.33} allow for a systematic analysis and comparison of model performance under different conditions, facilitating better understanding and reproducibility of our results. It is worth noting that only one large jump in the identification of spurious signals is typically observed in Figures \ref{eig4.3} and  \ref{eig4.33}, primarily due to our choice of magnifier $w$.%Although it simplifies much of the choice of magnifiers in Algorithm \ref{alg_firstround_bootstrap}, it is enough for our problem. 

