\subsection{Assessing the speed of inference}

The inference speed was further evaluated, as detailed in \eqref{eq: fps image}. Figure \ref{fig: fpga finn time} illustrates the frame rate for different models deployed on the \gls{fpga}'s \gls{pl} using the FINN framework.  \gls{mobilenet} v1, with four bits for both weights and biases, achieved speeds up to \numproduct{263 x} faster than the \gls{fpga} from \cite{Magalhaes2023} running at \qty{25}{FPS}. Conversely, \gls{cnv} only managed to be \numproduct{32 x} faster. Given that \gls{cnv} is significantly smaller than \gls{mobilenet} v1, there is potential for further optimisation and speed improvements for \gls{cnv} compared to \gls{mobilenet} v1.

Therefore, the use of the \glspl{fpga}' \gls{pl} speeds up the execution of \gls{dl} models in a manner that they become usable for robotics attention mechanisms, acting as an alerting system. At this stage, we can also prove that the use of the \gls{fpga}'s \gls{pl} instead of the \gls{dpu} deliver advantages, and the \glspl{ann} can be sped up to their maximum execution capabilities. 

