\emph{Higher resolution for harder examples.} In the previous analysis of open-source models, one of the more challenging cases was the function $f(x) = 4.67315 + \cos(\exp(x))$, where the corresponding image was generated using 100 evenly spaced $x$-values in the interval$(0.1,5)$. For larger $x$, the function oscillates rapidly, and the sparse sampling results in interpolation artefacts due to aliasing errors. The impact of this aliasing is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:dense_vs_sparse_sampling_complicated} and is clearly substantial. Given the severity of the aliasing, it is remarkable that LLM-LEx with \texttt{gpt-4o} was able to fit the function.

To assess the significance of the aliasing effect, we refitted LLM-LEx using a denser sampling of points, as shown on the left-hand side of the figure. In this case, \texttt{mistral} achieved a perfect fit, whereas the other models failed.
As \texttt{gpt-4o} executes considerably faster, we repeated the same experiment four additional times; it succeeded in three out of four runs, yielding a $4/5$ total success rate. Given the probabilistic nature of language models at non-zero temperature, this variability is unsurprising. 

