\vskip 5mm
{\bf Discussion}

{\it Anomalously high diffusivity and mean free path of IXs.}
For the estimates using the diffusion equation (Eq.~1), the IX diffusivity reaches $D \sim 10^3$~cm$^2$/s (Fig.~3). This value is significantly higher than the similarly determined IX diffusivities in time-resolved optical measurements of diffusive IX transport in vdW TMD HS, which reach up to $D \sim 0.1$~cm$^2$/s in Refs.~\cite{Li2021, Wang2021} and up to $D \sim 2$~cm$^2$/s in Ref.~\cite{Sun2022}. The anomalously high diffusivity indicates the anomalously high mean free time $\tau_{\rm m} \propto D$. In turn, the mean free path $l_{\rm m} \propto \tau_{\rm m}$ and, for the high $D \sim 10^3$~cm$^2$/s in the experiment (Fig.~3), the estimated $l_{\rm m}$ become anomalously high, as outlined in SI. IXs move with no scattering over the range $\sim l_{\rm m}$ and the high $l_{\rm m}$ indicate IX long-range ballistic transport. 

