Leveraging this universal property, we present two concrete applications:

\textbf{(i) Enhanced image diversity.} As shown in Figure \ref{fig:pn-vs-rr}, negative correlation implies diversity.
Because each antithetic noise pair drives the reverse‑diffusion trajectories toward distant regions of the data manifold, the paired‑sampling scheme markedly increases diversity ``for free'' compared with independent noise inputs, while still maintaining high image quality. The procedure is fully \emph{plug‑and‑play}: one simply feeds the model with antithetic noise vectors.

\textbf{(ii) Sharper uncertainty quantification.} Because each antithetic pair generates strongly negatively correlated samples, these outputs form an effective control‑variate pair.  We apply this idea in an antithetic estimator for downstream statistics, reducing the 95\% confidence-interval width by up to 90\% and cutting computational effort by about 100 times compared to standard Monte Carlo sampling with independent noise.




Building on the variance gains from antithetic pairs, we generalize the idea to apply quasi-Monte Carlo (QMC) and randomized QMC (RQMC). The resulting RQMC estimator often delivers further variance reduction. Although QMC has been widely used in computer graphics \cite{keller1995quasi, waechter2011quasi}, quantitative finance \cite{joy1996quasi,l2009quasi}, and Bayesian inference \cite{buchholz2018quasi,liu2021quasi},  this is, to our knowledge, its first application to diffusion models. 

Together, these results suggest that principled manipulations in the initial-noise space can be a promising avenue for further gains in diffusion model's efficiency, output diversity, and reliability.

