\begin{abstract}
%tosem摘要限定
Deep learning (DL) systems have been widely adopted in many areas, and are becoming even more popular with the emergence of large language models. However, due to the complex software stacks involved in their development and execution, crashes are unavoidable and common. Crashes severely waste computing resources and hinder development productivity, so efficient crash recovery is crucial. Existing solutions, such as checkpoint-retry, are too heavyweight for fast recovery from crashes caused by minor programming errors or transient runtime errors. Therefore, we present \textsc{DaiFu}, an in-situ recovery framework for DL systems. 
%, as a complement \hy{no need to say compkement to existing work here?}to existing crash recovery solutions for DL systems. 
%zilong: confirmed
Through a lightweight code transformation to a given DL system, \textsc{DaiFu} augments it to intercept crashes in situ and enables dynamic and instant updates to its program running context (e.g., code, configurations, and other data) for agile crash recovery.
Our evaluation shows that \textsc{DaiFu} helps reduce the restore time for crash recovery, achieving a 1327$\times$ speedup compared with state-of-the-art solutions. Meanwhile, the overhead of \textsc{DaiFu} is negligible (under 0.40\%). We also construct a benchmark spanning 7 distinct crash scenarios in DL systems, and show the effectiveness of \textsc{DaiFu} in diverse situations. 
\end{abstract}

