Related work includes static analysis techniques~\cite{DBLP:conf/sigsoft/ZhangRC0C020, DBLP:journals/tosem/NikanjamBMK22, DBLP:conf/icse/GaoLLZWY22, DBLP:journals/ase/NikanjamMKB22} and dynamic analysis techniques~\cite{DBLP:conf/sigsoft/YanCZTWW21, DBLP:conf/issre/WuSC0Q21}. 
Since avoiding crashes with limited information at the very beginning can be difficult, each of them primarily targets at some specific crashes, such as Not-a-Number (NaN) crashes~\cite{DBLP:conf/sigsoft/ZhangRC0C020, DBLP:conf/sigsoft/YanCZTWW21} or Tensor Mismatch crashes~\cite{DBLP:conf/issre/WuSC0Q21, DBLP:conf/icse/GaoLLZWY22}. These techniques are attractive since they can handle a crash even before it actually happens. However, trying them one by one to avoid possible crashes can still bring non-negligible effort and overhead. Moreover, since limited runtime information can be leveraged, some faults may be still not covered by these techniques and escape from their checks. By contrast, this paper explores to lazily handle a crash only when enough related information manifests at runtime.



(2) \textit{Fix-and-Restart} paradigm, which performs some fixes accordingly if some problems occur, and then restarts the failed DL system. Fine-grained monitoring mechanisms can be deployed to track the run-time state of a DL system and provide clues for a fix if needed~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/WardatCLR22, DBLP:conf/icse/ZhangZMS21, DBLP:conf/icse/CaoLC0TWC22, DBLP:conf/icse/WardatLR21, DBLP:conf/chi/SchoopHH21}. These techniques can take advantage of the run-time information, so the fix can be more focused. However, they usually re-run a DL system to make the fix work, which inevitably wastes computation and time. Therefore, this paper tries to avoid wasting the already-conducted computation and time during crash handling.

(3) \textit{Runtime-Recovery} paradigm, which aims at recovering a failed DL system in runtime and thus avoids a restart from scratch. Some existing fault tolerance techniques strive to achieve this through checkpoint and retry~\cite{DBLP:conf/usenix/KimJLLJ22, DBLP:conf/fast/MohanPC21, DBLP:conf/icml/0036LRJ20, DBLP:conf/nsdi/EisenmanMIMKNSA22}.
