\subsection{Comparison of  \textsc{DaiFu} and Prior Techniques} 

%In Table~\ref{tab:comparison}, we summarize the key differences between our in-situ recovery based framework \textsc{DaiFu} and prior crash recovery solutions for DL systems. Specifically, CheckNRun~\cite{DBLP:conf/nsdi/EisenmanMIMKNSA22}, Gemini~\cite{DBLP:conf/sosp/WangJZZFNW23} and JIT-Checkpoint~\cite{DBLP:conf/eurosys/GuptaKKVGKRS24} are in-house tools that are tied to specific programs (e.g., embedding tables checkpointing for recommender system or large-scale distributed training workloads) and specific infrastructures (e.g., requiring heavy modifications to underlying libraries such as Deepspeed~\cite{deepspeed}, CUDA~\cite{cuda} and NCCL~\cite{nccl}) of corresponding companies, and are not open-sourced yet. Compared with them, \textsc{DaiFu} targets at developing a tool that is applicable for general DL systems. CheckFreq~\cite{DBLP:conf/fast/MohanPC21} is also tied to a specific library (i.e., Nvidia DALI~\cite{dali}), and it invasively modifies the data loader APIs, and needs developers to refactor their program (e.g., manually find code that loads data and transform the code to use CheckFreq) and cope with the conflicts between the multi-threading libraries (for example, we find that CheckFreq cannot correctly interact with the PyTorch ``torchrun'' command for distributed training). Besides, when deciding to use Checkpoint-Retry, developers need to manually prepare what should be checkpointed (e.g., the model, the optimizer states, the intermediate states that affect the real-time configuration of the optimizer, and some evaluation results). \textsc{DaiFu} does not need this preparation to take effect, and will automatically transform the user program after a developer specifies the main function for decoration. Besides, when using Checkpoint-Retry, developers can only rely on the logs to figure out the crash root cause, but in \textsc{DaiFu}, we provide interfaces to developers  to interact with the program to understand variable values that may cause the crash. Moreover, the retry program granularity of \textsc{DaiFu} is the most fine-grained (i.e., in code line level) since \textsc{DaiFu} performs recovery in situ, so the restore time is much reduced. Besides, all prior recovery methods need to restart the program when code updates are needed, which may potentially cause a large overhead of program initialization including data preprocessing.

Table~\ref{tab:comparison} outlines the key differences between our in-situ recovery framework, namely \textsc{DaiFu}, and the existing crash recovery solutions for DL systems. Specifically, CheckNRun~\cite{DBLP:conf/nsdi/EisenmanMIMKNSA22}, Gemini~\cite{DBLP:conf/sosp/WangJZZFNW23}, and JIT-C~\cite{DBLP:conf/eurosys/GuptaKKVGKRS24} are proprietary tools tailored to specific applications (e.g., embedding table checkpointing for recommender systems or large-scale distributed training) and infrastructures that demand extensive modifications to libraries (e.g., Deepspeed~\cite{deepspeed}, CUDA~\cite{cuda}, NCCL~\cite{nccl}), and they remain closed-source. In contrast, \textsc{DaiFu} is designed for general DL systems. Similarly, CheckFreq~\cite{DBLP:conf/fast/MohanPC21} is coupled with Nvidia DALI~\cite{dali} and requires invasive changes to data loader APIs. Developers must refactor their code to integrate CheckFreq, and its incompatibility with multi-threading libraries, such as failing to work with PyTorch’s “torchrun” for distributed training, adds further challenges. Moreover, prior checkpoint-retry based solutions force developers to manually specify checkpoint targets (e.g., the model, optimizer states, intermediate states, and evaluation results). In contrast, \textsc{DaiFu} automatically transforms the user program once the main function is annotated. Furthermore, by performing in-situ recovery at the code line level, \textsc{DaiFu} significantly reduces the restore time for crash recovery. Finally, unlike previous methods that require complete program restarts for code updates, which incur substantial program initialization overhead, \textsc{DaiFu} can seamlessly apply code updates without program restarts.







