%\textbf{Support for Error Handling.} Python is an interpreted language. A Python interpreter interprets programs in bytecode, and many errors may be triggered only when the exact faulty statement is interpreted. Fortunately, Python provides a good support for error handling. Errors in runtime can be properly mapped to the built-in exception types and caught with the built-in \textit{try-except} mechanisms. \textsc{DaiFu} takes advantages of this language feature to intercept crashes at rumtime.

%\textbf{Support for Dynamic Code Changes.} Python supports the meta-object protocol and dynamic typing, so functions and modules implemented with Python are modifiable at runtime. Actually, there have been many tools~\cite{DBLP:conf/usenix/HuangXZ021, DBLP:conf/apsec/TangZ18} developed to ease dynamic code changes for python-implemented programs. \textsc{DaiFu} is benefited from such language features and inspired by these tools. However, currently Python cannot support on-stack replacement~\cite{DBLP:conf/cgo/FinkQ03} due to its implementation. Specifically, it implements code objects on the evaluation stack as read-only. As a result, dynamic code changes can only take effect when the modified function is called at the next time. Therefore, even a crash of a function can be successfully intercepted using the Python error handling mechanism, existing tools~\cite{DBLP:conf/usenix/HuangXZ021, DBLP:conf/apsec/TangZ18} still cannot modify the code of such a still-running function to repair it. In a word, current tools supporting dynamic code changes are insufficient for recovering crashes in DL systems, where long-running functions are common and susceptible to crashes. \textsc{DaiFu} overcomes this problem to provide in-situ recovery support.



