\textbf{Method.} We first search for reproducible crashing faults of DL systems from prior empirical studies~\cite{DBLP:conf/issta/ZhangCCXZ18, DBLP:conf/icse/IslamPNR20, DBLP:conf/icse/HumbatovaJBR0T20, DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2206-12311, DBLP:conf/icse/LiangLSSFD22} and technique studies~\cite{DBLP:journals/tosem/NikanjamBMK22, DBLP:conf/icse/WardatLR21, DBLP:conf/icse/ZhangZMS21, DBLP:conf/icse/CaoLC0TWC22}. For each study, we first identify whether the associated artifact provides scripts to reproduce the faults reported in the paper. Then, for each reproducible fault, we confirm whether it will cause a crash. We also validate that these cases will escape the sanity checks~\cite{pyright} by VS Code to omit trivial cases. Finally, to cover more crash scenarios, we inject crashing faults to some representative DL systems to enrich the benchmark. 
%\textbf{}. We first search for benchmarks that contain reproducible faults that will lead to crash scenarios in DL programs. Reproducing faults in DL programs is labor-intensive and time-consuming because the Python version, dependency, data and the fault triggering process should all be clearly described to allow a fault to be reproduced. According to a recent study defects4ML~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2206-12311}, only about 5.3\% of all reviewed DL program faults from GitHub and 3.34\% of reviewed DL program faults from StackOverflow posts can be reproduced. To create a DL fault benchmark that meets a high standard of reproducibility, defects4ML analyzes a series of prior DL fault studies ~\cite{DBLP:conf/issta/ZhangCCXZ18, DBLP:conf/icse/IslamPNR20, DBLP:conf/icse/WardatLR21, DBLP:conf/icse/HumbatovaJBR0T20, DBLP:journals/tosem/NikanjamBMK22} and makes great effort to find reproducible faults in them. Therefore, this paper primarily selects faults from defects4ML to reproduce, and use them to evaluate \textsc{DaiFu}. Another DL fault benchmark gDefectDL~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/LiangLSSFD22} also contains some faults that will cause crash scenarios, so we also consider it when forming our DL crash benchmark to evaluate \textsc{DaiFu}.

\textbf{Reproducing Real-World Crashing Faults}. Based on the exploration, some cases are excluded from our benchmark due to the lack of reproduction information~\cite{DBLP:conf/issta/ZhangCCXZ18, DBLP:conf/icse/IslamPNR20, DBLP:conf/icse/HumbatovaJBR0T20} or a mismatch of subjects (i.e., the technique studies~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/ZhangZMS21, DBLP:conf/icse/WardatLR21, DBLP:conf/icse/CaoLC0TWC22, DBLP:journals/tosem/NikanjamBMK22} do not provide faults leading to a crash). % Specifically, the revisited empirical studies  aim at analyzing existing faults rather than reproducing them. As a result, the associated artifacts do not contain essential information to reproduce faults reported in the paper. Some other technique studies, i.e., AutoTrainer~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/ZhangZMS21}, DeepLocalize~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/WardatLR21}, DeepFD~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/CaoLC0TWC22} and NeuraLint~\cite{DBLP:journals/tosem/NikanjamBMK22}, include a DL fault benchmark in their evaluations. However, their focus is misconfiguration that will cause incorrect functionality (e.g., vanishing gradient and low accuracy), and crashed programs are not included in their benchmarks. Since this paper targets at crash scenarios in DL programs, they are unsuitable for this study too. 
Finally, the successfully-reproduced crash scenarios come mainly from defects4ML~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2206-12311} and gDefectDL~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/LiangLSSFD22}. From these benchmarks, we select real-world faults that will cause a crash and remove duplicate cases. %Besides, some cases are test-purposed programs with only a statement that triggers a crash immediately. For these test-purposed programs, recovery is not the primary concern, so we omit them from our benchmark. 
Finally, we reproduced a total of 18 crash cases in real-world DL systems.% (including 12 \underline{API Misuse} crashes and 6 \underline{Tensor Mismatch} Crashes).%, each of which includes a buggy version and the corresponding fault-free version.% Among the reproduced crashes, 12 crashes belong to API Misuse, and 6 crashes belong to Tensor Mismatch.

