\subsection{Analysis and Methodology}\label{sec:analysis_and_methodology}
\textcolor{red}{TRANSFORMED AND MOVED TO EVALUATION OR DISCUSSION}
In-situ recovery needs to manipulate an executing program exactly at its failed point. For each DL program in the DL crash benchmark, we first identify functions that implement the entry functions (e.g., a main/train/test function). Suppose that a deterministic crash has been triggered by a specific code location (denoted as the failed code) in such functions, but it is temporally intercepted for a fix. This crash is considered \textbf{\textit{in-situ recoverable}} if the fault itself and its negative effect can be eliminated. Eliminating a fault can be achieved through revising some objects (e.g., variables or code) or the environment, which needs to be implemented by \textsc{DaiFu} (detailed in \S~\ref{sec:fix_crashes}). As for its negative effect elimination, we consider a straightforward solution. Specifically, if the revised part is code in the entry function, we re-execute all the code between the revised code and the failed code; if the revised part is a variable, we re-execute all the code relying on the revised variable.
%(i.e., re-executing all the code relying on the revised objects, and if the revised code contains code in the main-progress function, all the code between the revised main-progress function code and the failed code is re-executed). 
%Such a simple solution works only when \textbf{\textit{the re-executed part is statically idempotent
%这里原来使用幂等Idempotent这个词，但感觉不太准确
%(i.e., successive repeated executions are statically equivalent\footnote{For random number related APIs, generating / using a different random number is considered statically equivalent.} to only executing the latest one)}}. 
%and if there are objects (variables or code of other functions) needing a revision, the re-executed part should be the first to visit such objects}. 
Crashes following this condition constitute part of in-situ recoverable crashes. We investigate the existence of these crashes according to the constructed DL crash benchmark or the cause definitions themselves for some crashes. It is noted that even a crash does not follow this condition, it may still be recovered in situ with some complex solutions. However, this paper does not exhaustively enumerate every possible type of in-situ recoverable crashes and leave this as future work.
