\textbf{Finding 1: }\textit{81\% (17/21) of the studied code defect induced crashes follow the condition highlighted in \S~\ref{sec:analysis_and_methodology}, and thus are in-situ recoverable.}

We analyze the error messages of the studied code defect induced crashes to locate their failed code, and then analyze the code difference between their buggy versions and corresponding fault-free versions to locate their revised code. Then, we can locate the needed re-executed part for each crash. We check whether there are only statically idempotent operations inside it. Amongst the studied crashes, the needed re-executed part of only an API Misuse crash is not statically idempotent, and 3 NaN crashes will not crash and thus have no failed code for analysis. Other crashes are in-situ recoverable. In \S~\ref{sec:evaluation}, we will further validate this observation by fixing these crashes in situ using \textsc{DaiFu}.

\textbf{Finding 2: }\textit{Amongst the environment related deterministic DL crashes (details in Table \ref{tab:crash_classification}), the Path Not Found crashes, Library Not Found crashes and Permission Denied crashes follow the condition highlighted in \S~\ref{sec:analysis_and_methodology} and thus are in-situ recoverable, while whether an Out of Memory crash is in-situ recoverable depends on the specific fault causing it.}

We analyze the cause definition of each environment related deterministic DL crash mentioned in Table~\ref{tab:crash_classification} to understand their in-situ recoverability. For Path Not Found crashes, Library Not Found crashes and Permission Denied crashes, only the file/library loading statement needs to be re-executed to fix a crash, and the related file/library loading APIs are typically idempotent. Therefore, these three types of crashes are recoverable in situ. As for Out of Memory crashes including CPU Out of Memory and GPU Out of Memory, it depends. A CPU Out of Memory crash usually cannot be caught with the Python error handling mechanism. When it happens, the process has been directly killed by the operating system, so it is not in-situ recoverable with only the Python error handling mechanism. As for GPU Out of Memory crash, if one can finally satisfy the GPU requirement of a DL program by changing some configurations (e.g., which GPU the program runs on), the crash is in-situ recoverable. 
