%\textbf{Recovery Time Measurement.} 、
We compare different DL crash recovery methods based on how much time is spent on restoration for crash recovery with these methods. Since the occurrence time of crashes also affects the restore time for crash recovery, to ensure a fair comparison, we consistently inject runtime errors at identical training iterations for experiments of different recovery methods. The injections are uniformly distributed throughout the entire training process. %, and the crash injection intervals when training ResNet50, ViT-L/16, Swin-B, GPT2 and LLaMA-7B are set as 50, 50, 50, 20 and 125 iterations, respectively. 
We write scripts to automatically repeat the recovery process for each recovery method. The experiments for each recovery method are repeated 3 times. 
The time elapsed between the occurrence of the crash and the completion of recovery is calculated as the restore time. Specifically, we consider a recovery to be completed when the program arrives at the end of its original crashing iteration, so we record the recovery completion time as this moment.% However, repeating each of the long recovery processes of Restart-from-Scratch and Original Ckpt.-Retry is difficult to achieve since this is equal to repeating the time-consuming training for over hundreds of times. An observation is, for Restart-from-Scratch or Original Ckpt.-Retry, the recovery is achieved through repeating the training process between the program start or the latest checkpoint. Therefore, we can use the elapsed time between the program start time and the crash occurrence time as the needed restore time for Restart-from-Scratch, and use the elapsed time between the latest checkpoint time and the crash occurrence time as the needed restore time for Original Ckpt.-Retry. By doing so, we can gain the restore time needed by Restart-from-Scratch and Original Ckpt.-Retry without time-consuming repetitions.% for comparison.
