As shown in Table~\ref{tab:overall_result}, \textsc{DaiFu} is applicable to 7 distinct crash scenarios (including 31/32 of the crash cases in the benchmark). Specifically, \textsc{DaiFu} is particularly useful when developers need to frequently update their programs (e.g., add more training data, add more evaluations, or implement new training strategies) in the early stage of program development. In this stage, programming errors are frequent and can seriously affect the productivity of developers, while with \textsc{DaiFu}, the development process can be accelerated. In general, these results demonstrate that the in-situ recovery by \textsc{DaiFu} is applicable in many scenarios.%, and thus it is helpful in practice.% We will discuss the unsupported cases in \S~\ref{sec:limitation}.%and it can help save up to 99\% of progress for some crashes.% The saved progress depends on the manifestation time of a crash, so in some cases, the saved progress is relatively small. Even though there exist cases whose saved progress may be small, it is still beneficial to use \textsc{DaiFu} to vaccinate a DL program because no one can guarantee a DL program only contains some specific early-manifesting crashes. 

%Table ~\ref{tab:comparison} also displays the comparison between \textsc{DaiFu} and prior DL crash handling paradigms on the averaged saved progress on the benchmark. We can see that leveraging \textsc{DaiFu} to enable in-situ recovery can save more progress compared with \textit{Fixed-Then-Restart} and Enhanced \textit{Checkpoint-Retry}. Specifically, \textit{Fix-Then-Restart} will simply lose all the conducted computation, so its saved progress is always 0\%. As for \textit{Checkpoint-Retry}, even when it can be enhanced to handle deterministic crashes, its saved progress may still be limited. For example, for crashes rooted in checkpoint-related logic (i.e., CV-PNF, NLP-PNF and RL-PNF), the checkpointing itself fails and cannot save any progress. Besides, some programs may crash before the generation of the first checkpoint, so \textit{Checkpoint-Retry} cannot work for these cases. Compare with them, \textsc{DaiFu} can usually recover a program just from the location where it fails, so it can achieve the best saved progress among these methods.

