\subsection{Preparation Effort}\label{sec:effort}
%In this section, we discuss the human effort required by different DL crash recovery methods. 

{The preparation effort} refers to the modifications needed to enable crash recovery. Checkpoint-retry based methods require developers to decide which elements to save (e.g., model state, optimizer state, and temporary variables) and how to resume from these checkpoints. More frequent checkpointing further increases the burden, as these methods are often intertwined with underlying deep learning or driver libraries. Consequently, developers must adjust the runtime environment and rewrite portions of their code to accommodate specific API versions (for example, a re-implemented DALI dataloader in CheckFreq~\cite{DBLP:conf/fast/MohanPC21}). In contrast, \textsc{DaiFu} adopts an independent approach that is not built upon any DL libraries. Our experiments demonstrate its successful integration with systems implemented in PyTorch, TensorFlow, and PyTorch Lightning. Moreover, the automated code transformation reduces modifications to only a few clean lines, which is a significant advantage.

%\textbf{Debugging Effort} denotes the challenge of identifying the root cause of a crash. Regardless of the recovery method, developers must diagnose the issue through multiple recovery trials, i.e., formulating a hypothesis, applying a fix, and re-executing the program until the problem is resolved. Checkpoint-retry based methods require lengthy re-executions and rely solely on logs for diagnosis, increasing the overall effort. In contrast, \textsc{DaiFu} allows developers to immediately test fixes and interact with the program in situ, thereby expediting the debugging process.

%Prior work~\cite{DBLP:conf/fast/MohanPC21, DBLP:conf/nsdi/EisenmanMIMKNSA22, DBLP:conf/sosp/WangJZZFNW23} mainly focuses on optimizations within the Checkpoint-Retry paradigm, and their common idea is to make checkpoint more frequent.
%However, this idea itself is not panacea. Some extra human effort associated with it should not be overlooked, while \textsc{DaiFu} eliminates such extra effort. \textbf{(1) \textsc{DaiFu} eliminates the need of deciding what to checkpoint and resume.} For example, though Checkfreq enables frequent checkpoints, it only supports the automatic checkpointing of model and optimizer state, and only supports the resume process upon \texttt{DALIClassificationIterator}. This only covers a part of the usage scenarios. Depending on the context of a specific program, some additional states (e.g., some measurements which affects the dynamic tuning of some hyper-parameters) may also need to be checkpointed, and developers may implement data loading in a different way (e.g., using different frameworks or even their own implementation), requiring a corresponding resume process implementation. Developing a frequent checkpointing method and making it generally applicable to such a wide spectrum of DL systems is challenging. As a result, such effort will inevitably be migrated to the users, distracting their attention on designing the main logic of their own DL algorithm. By contrast, one of the design principles of \textsc{DaiFu} is to automate all the process of context saving and resume. Moreover, the automation is not coupled with any framework, but only reliant on the natural loop structure in the code. Therefore, using \textsc{DaiFu} needs less human effort. \textbf{(2) \textsc{DaiFu} does not require to handle checkpoint crashes.} During the experiments of CheckFreq, we find the frequent checkpointing iteself or its resume process may fail. This is because making checkpoints more frequent inevitably introduces complexity to program execution, associated with new bugs and compatibility issues with other frameworks. Since \textsc{DaiFu} does not rely on multi-threading and pipelining, related coordination bugs stemming from the recovery method itself can be avoided to some extent, relieving the effort needed for the debugging and fixing of the Checkpoint-Retry method itself.

