\begin{abstract}{%\color{forestgreen}
Quantum tomography at colliders makes it possible to measure the amount of entanglement and test the violation of the Bell inequality. We explain in detail how this is possible and why it offers the possibility of examining from a new angle the feasibility of local hidden variable models. } 
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}


One of the most characteristic properties of a quantum system is the possible presence of \textbf{entanglement}, that is of quantum correlations among
 observables that cannot be accounted for by classical physics 
~\cite{Horodecki:2009zz,Benatti:2010,Nielsen:2012yss,bruss2019quantum}.
As entanglement often leads to conflict with common sense, which is grounded on local realism, classical models encompassing standard quantum mechanics have been proposed in order to explain the phenomena in terms of underlying hidden variables. These variables can be deterministic or stochastic but, to mimic the probabilistic nature of quantum mechanics, only their average over a characteristic distribution enters the result of measurements in either case. 

Investigations about these hypothetical variables gave rise to lively debates---often touching intricate philosophical considerations. A first outcome of these discussions---after von Neumann's  rather abrupt dismissal~\cite{vonneumann} was shown to be premature~\cite{Hermann:1935,Bell:1964fg}---is that hidden variables cannot be agnostic about the experimental setup through which they are being measured because  simultaneous values  to all observables of a quantum mechanical system cannot be assigned in a way that respects their algebraic structure. This \textbf{contextuality} requirement comes from a theorem~\cite{Gleason:1957,Bell:1964fg,Kochen:1968zz} that severely restricts which models of hidden variables are possible. The theorem holds for Hilbert spaces of dimension larger than 2. For a two-dimensional Hilbert space, there exists a simple non-contextual model for the spin of a particle~\cite{Bell:1964fg}.  Quantum contextuality has been experimentally tested at low energies by means of photons and other devices~\cite{PhysRevLett.84.5457,PhysRevLett.90.250401,PhysRevLett.97.230401,PhysRevLett.103.040403,PhysRevLett.103.160405,Lapkiewicz:2011rpt} and more recently also at high energies~\cite{Fabbrichesi:2025ifv}.

The discussion was given new scope by J. Bell's seminal paper~\cite{Bell:1964}, in which it is shown that a large class of these contextual  models of hidden variables, those obeying a locality condition (\textbf{Bell locality}), satisfy specific constraints (the \textbf{Bell inequalities})
that can be violated by quantum mechanics~\cite{Brunner:RevModPhys.86.419,scarani2019bell}. This result makes it possible to discriminate between classical  models and quantum mechanics
by performing actual experiments~\cite{bell2004speakable,redhead1987incompleteness,bell2002quantum,bertlmann2016quantum,Genovese:2005nw}.
Many tests of this kind have been performed with various systems, both  at low energies within atomic and solid-state physics~\cite{Aspect:1982fx,Weihs:1998gy,Tittel:1998ja,Ansmann:2009ims,Hensen:2015ccp,Giustina:2015yza,Storz:2023jjx,PhysRevLett.119.010402}
and, more recently, at high energies in colliders experiments (for a review, see~\cite{Barr:2024djo}).

At particle colliders it is possible to reconstruct
the spin state  of the products of a scattering process by using the angular distributions of the momenta---as recorded by a detector---of the related final-state particles. The complete knowledge of the state, obtained through this \textbf{quantum state tomography} process, should be compared to the traditional setting of a Bell inequality test where only joint probabilities along given directions in space are measured.  For the latter, since only these probabilities are measured, the final state is only partially determined and this lack of knowledge can be exploited to devise \textbf{loopholes} in the test. At colliders, the complete knowledge of the state closes all loopholes and the Bell test becomes a simple determination of suitable observables on the reconstructed state. Because, sometimes, in physics the equations are not enough---and some explaining is required lest their meaning remains unclear---we discuss in detail the testing of nonlocality at colliders and compare it to the traditional low-energy experiments.\footnote{Before embarking on such a discussion, a methodological premise is perhaps necessary: Experiments always compare models against the data. The data are the ``real world'' and, given a model, we check how well the data fit it. There is no such a things as testing directly the ``real world''. The Bell test therefore simply checks the factorization of probabilities (Bell locality) against the data; its results can be used to exclude local theories (if the inequality is violated), as well as quantum mechanics (if the violation exceed a critical value known as Tsirelson's bound~\cite{Cirelson:1980ry}). The same holds true for measurements of entanglement.}  

