 Let us stress an important point that perhaps makes clearer the complete similarity between Bell tests at colliders and those in atomic physics with photons. All current experiments---be them  at low or high energy---must necessarily assume some model about the working of the polarimeter utilized in the measurements. It is the \textbf{polarimeter} that transforms a measured data (light or darkness in the case of photons, angular distribution of momenta in the case of the decay of a particle) into a polarization measurement. In the case of the atomic-physics tests, this equipment consists of the photon polarimeter, while  in the case of the high-energy tests, in the polarimetric vector and spin-analyzing power of a particle emitted in a decay. In both cases, its actual operation has to be determined independently of the Bell test  or a measurement of entanglement, in separate experiments providing the characteristic properties of the photon polarimeter or of the polarimetric vector. It is only after this step that the experimentally collected data---be them in the form of frequencies, probabilities or correlations---can be presented as those giving photon or elementary particle polarizations.  On the other hand, in a analytic computation or a Monte Carlo simulation, the operation of the polarimeter can be obtained through the model itself instead of being derived from some other data. For example, in the case of tests at colliders the polarimetric vector in the fully leptonic decay of the top quark is found to be one because of the chiral coupling between elementary fermions and the gauge bosons in the Standard Model. 


We are aware that the ideal configuration to test  nonlocality against hidden variable models would be, like in quantum information, a \textbf{black box} in which the inputs can be varied at will and the outputs recorded~\cite{Eckstein:2021pgm,Altomonte:2024upf}. Unfortunately, no present or planned  collider experiment satisfies these conditions. Nor do the low-energy experiments in which Bell inequality has been tested.  

\section{Quantum entanglement}



Consider a pair of charged particles originating from the decay of a neutral state. They fly apart. If at a successive time and somewhere in space you look and find a charged particle, you know for sure that somewhere else there is going to be a particle with the opposite charge. By a measurement now and here, we learn something about somewhere else that can be very far away and space-like separated. Still, this is not a sign that the two particles are entangled: their charge is an intrinsic property that they have independently of whether we measure it or not. For classical properties, such as all the conserved ones, the knowledge of the distant particle which we instantaneously acquire by measuring the one close to us simply testifies the presence of classical correlations enforced by the neutrality of the progenitor particle. 



Instead, genuine quantum correlations originate because of the linearity of the theory, which allows for superpositions of states. Consider the spin of a spin-1/2 particle in a direction determined by the unit vector $\vec n$.  Before the spin is measured, the particle can be in a superposition of the two possible measure outcomes, for instance:
$
|\Psi\rangle = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \, \big( |\!\uparrow_{\vec n} \rangle + |\!\downarrow_{\vec n} \rangle \big) \, ,
$
where $| \!\uparrow_{\vec n} \rangle$ and $| \downarrow_{\vec n} \rangle$ indicate states with the spin aligned or anti-aligned with respect to the direction indicated by $\vec n$, respectively. This means that the component of the spin along a given direction is not an intrinsic property of the fermion. 

A paradigmatic case in which entanglement can be easily studied is precisely that of a bipartite system formed by two spin-1/2 particles, yielding a joint spin state with quantum correlations that can be analyzed. For instance, take a the two-particle state
\be
|\Psi\rangle=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \Big(  |  \!\uparrow_{\vec n}\rangle | \!\downarrow_{\vec n} \rangle 
+  | \!\downarrow_{\vec n}\rangle |  \!\uparrow_{\vec n}\rangle \Big) 
\label{superposition}\ ,
\ee 
and repeat the test described before for the charged particles: neither of the two particles carries a definite value for its spin
along the vector $\vec n$, but as soon as one of the two particle is subject to a spin measurement---the result of which is completely random---we learn that the spin of the other lies on the opposite direction. 

