By taking spin measurements along different directions, the state is not defined by the conservation of the angular momentum and one detects correlations not amenable to a classical explanation. In fact, perfect knowledge of the joint spin state of the particles,
given in (\ref{superposition}), results in a total indetermination of the state of each of the constituent particles. Since this would occurs even if the two particles were separated at an arbitrarily large distance at the time of the measurement, one speaks of quantum nonlocality.


This short discussion emphasizes how the central feature of the quantum mechanical description is that particles, in general, do not have definite properties before these are measured. It is not that we lack information about them, it is the quirkiness proper of the superposition principle which allows quantum systems to exist in states which are incompatible with fixed values of these properties prior to measurements. This is in stark contrast with \textbf{local realism}, which uses hidden variables to define each and every property of a particle so as to represent an element of reality that is locally defined at all times~\cite{Einstein:1935rr}.   
