\begin{abstract}
Elite football is believed to have evolved in recent years, but systematic evidence for the pace and form of that change is sparse. Drawing on event‑level records for 13,067 matches in ten top‑tier men's and women's leagues in England, Spain, Germany, Italy, and the United States (2020-2025), we quantify match dynamics with two views: conventional performance statistics and \textit{pitch‑passing networks} that track ball movement among a grid of pitch (field) regions. Between 2020 and 2025, average passing volume, pass accuracy, and the percent of passes made under pressure all rose. In general, the largest year‑on‑year changes occurred in women's competitions. Network measures offer alternative but complementary perspectives on the changing gameplay in recent years, normalized outreach in the pitch passing networks decreased, while the average shortest path lengths increased, indicating a wider ball circulation. Together, these indicators point to a sustained intensification of collective play across contemporary professional football.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}

Football is an ever-evolving game influenced by a wide range of factors. These include the introduction of new technologies, sports attire and increased focus on physical recovery, which have enhanced individual performance \cite{seckin2023wearable}; the rising attention and investment in women’s leagues \cite{deloitte2025wsl}; advanced data systems and machine-learning methods that enable in-depth match analysis \cite{spearman2017, fernandez2018,rico-gonzalez2023mlsoccer, biermann2023}; and evolving tactical styles that shape how the game is played \cite{gonzalezrodenas2023tactics,bauer2023}. The interplay between these developments makes football an inherently complex system. The abundance of detailed match data in recent years has further fueled the growth of sports analytics as a vibrant and interdisciplinary research field. As the game continues to evolve, understanding how key aspects of the game change over time, and how these changes differ across leagues and between male and female competitions, is essential for optimizing performance over all levels of the game.

In this study, we employ both statistical and network-based methods to investigate the evolution of team dynamics and performance metrics over recent years. We analyzed a dataset of more than 13,067 matches, encompassing ten men's and women's competitions across five countries (England, Spain, Italy, Germany, and the United States) from 2020 to 2025 \cite{statsbomb_private_2025}. By focusing on team-level attributes, rather than player-specific indicators, our aim is to capture broader structural trends in playing style and match behavior. Rather than predicting match outcomes, our goal is to track the evolution of key performance indicators over time and within seasons and to assess whether these changes vary across countries and between men’s and women’s leagues.

Across these five countries, football competitions differ significantly in their structure, finances, and ownership regulations. The English Premier League is highly commercialized, with many clubs owned by foreign investors \cite{PLNow_Ownership2025}. As a result, it attracts top-tier international talent and is widely regarded as one of the most competitive leagues globally. Spain and Italy also allow for foreign ownership, but financial stability can vary considerably between clubs \cite{ft_takeovers_fall2025}. In contrast, Germany’s Bundesliga enforces the ``50+1'' rule, which ensures that club members hold a majority stake, thereby ensuring financial sustainability and fan influence \cite{Bauers2019_regulationinvestors}. Meanwhile, the United States' Major League Soccer follows a franchise-based, single-entity model with centralized ownership, no promotion or relegation, and strict financial controls \cite{guardian_mls30_feb2025}.

In addition to structural differences, national football cultures influence how the game is played \cite{wilson2008_inverting}: Spain is known for its technical, possession-heavy style; Germany emphasizes structured organization and high pressing; Italy prioritizes tactical discipline and defensive solidity; England blends physicality with speed; and the United States tends to focus on athleticism and direct attacking play. Due to these differences, developments are expected to differ between the different leagues \cite{wilson2008_inverting, goldblatt2014_game}. Women's leagues in these same countries often share structural characteristics with their male counterparts, including governance by the same national associations and affiliations with men’s clubs \cite{weatherillOwnership}. However, playing styles are generally less distinctly defined \cite{Pappalardo2021_explainingdifference}. This is partly because women’s football is still in the process of professionalization in many regions, and partly because leagues tend to be more fluid in terms of player movement and coaching styles. Due to this, stylistic identities in women’s football are still emerging, and are often driven by individual coaches or clubs rather than national traditions. 

