Women’s and men’s football competitions have not developed in parallel. Men's leagues were recognized professionally much earlier, and have benefited from decades of sustained investment, visibility, and cultural acceptance \cite{williams2003}. In contrast, women’s football was historically marginalized; in some countries, it was even banned. For example, in England, the FA prohibited women’s matches on affiliated pitches from 1921 to 1971 \cite{lopez1997}. Meanwhile, the men’s professional league began as early as 1888 \cite{williams2003}. In recent years, women’s competitions have gained significant traction, with increased media coverage, institutional support, and investment \cite{fifa2023}. Due to this recent growth, we expect more rapid and pronounced changes in key aspects of women’s competitions compared to the more established men’s game. These differences are likely to be especially evident in countries where women’s leagues have only recently become professional, such as in England, Italy, and Spain.

Football seasons can be quite diverse from year to year, due to structural shifts such as the transfers of key players or a change of the coach, and thereby the tactics. Performance patterns of a specific team can vary from year to year, especially for newly promoted clubs or following a large squad turnover. External events, such as the 2022 World Cup and the 2024 European Cup, could have a significant impact on the start of seasons, as players have less time to recover, a point often acknowledged by players themselves \cite{molango2025_calendar}.

Within a season, short-term dynamics such as player fatigue, injury, and team form could impact key aspects of the game. Some performance metrics are likely to improve throughout the season as players become accustomed to new coaching techniques or improve their physical health, while others are expected to decline due to the physical strain the system places on players. These effects could also be enhanced by external events. 


Given the multifaceted nature of modern football \cite{buldu2018using} and the need to capture both structural and temporal changes in team behavior, we employ a network-based approach complemented by traditional statistical analysis. Network science has been applied to analyze the dynamics of various sports, including football \cite{duch2010}, basketball \cite{fewell2012}, boxing \cite{tennant2020}, badminton \cite{gomez2020}, rugby \cite{cintia2016}, and water polo \cite{Passos2011}, to name a few. Among these, football has been studied the most extensively, using different kinds of network representations to describe the connections between players, teams, or leagues \cite{Ribeiro2017,buldu2018using,ramos2018,pan2024}. In this research, we construct \textit{pitch-passing networks} where nodes represent regions of the pitch and weighted, directed links connect regions between which passes occur. Link weights correspond to the number of passes exchanged between regions \cite{zhou2023improved,herrera2020pitch}. This network representation allows us to quantify team coordination patterns, spatial organization, and tactical structures that may not be apparent through conventional match statistics alone \cite{garrido2020,novillo2024}. Previous studies have successfully applied these methods to identify distinct playing styles and the temporal evolution of team dynamics, and to quantify differences in team strategies and their impact on match outcomes \cite{huang2024}. We combine these network-based measures alongside statistical performance indicators to provide a more detailed characterization of team dynamics and their evolution over time. The application of these methods to our extensive and novel dataset allows us to detect subtle yet meaningful changes in playing styles and tactical approaches, highlighting the ongoing transformation of the sport.

The datasets used in football analysis typically fall into three main categories: statistical data, which provide summary-level information like completed passes, shots, goals, and final scores; event data, which record all actions that occur during a match, including their precise timing, Euclidean coordinates, and outcomes; and tracking data, which capture the positions of all players on the pitch at high spatial and temporal resolution. Match outcome data have been used to construct networks of clubs to assess competitive influence, where each node represents a club and links indicate match outcomes (e.g., directed toward the winning team) \cite{Basini2023_assessingcompetitive}. Event data have enabled the identification of key players within a team, and the analysis of player interactions and influence \cite{pappalardo2018quantifying, pappalardo2019playerank}. Tracking data, on the other hand, have supported the study of player interactions at fine spatial scales, including the development of marking and coordination networks \cite{buldu2020football}.

In this study, we use event data to construct pass-to-pass networks. Specifically, we draw on detailed datasets provided by StatsBomb \cite{statsbomb_private_2025, li2025motif}, which include rich, high-resolution records of match events.



\section{Methods \& Data}
\label{Methods and Data}


\subsection{Dataset: Hudl Statsbomb Events Data}

The data analyzed in this work, provided by a license from \href{https://www.hudl.com/products/statsbomb}{Hudl StatsBomb}, encompasses data from more than 13,067 matches played between 2020 and 2025. These matches are from the football leagues of five countries: La Liga and Liga Femenina (Liga F) of Spain, the Premier League and FA Women's Super League of England, the Serie A and Serie A Women of Italy, the Major League Soccer (MLS) and National's Women's Soccer League (NWSL) of the USA, and the Bundesliga and Frauen Bundesliga of Germany. For each match, the dataframe contains information on the starting lineup, and all \textit{events}, which are actions occurring on the pitch, such as passes, fouls, and goal attempts, that occurred throughout the match. We chose to focus on events associated with the passes and the shots made. Passes are the main form of interactions for the players on the pitch, while shots made are the driving action in the match. These measures were divided into two different approaches: statistical- and network science-based measures. To illustrate our analytical approach and data structure, \autoref{fig:fig1} presents a comprehensive schematic of match events data and network construction using a representative 2024 match between Arsenal and Liverpool, two top tier men's teams in England. We classify team performance into three tiers (top, mid and bottom), corresponding to the upper, middle and lower third of the year and ranking. Within the following subsections, a description of which measures were taken, and their interpretation in the context of a football game, will be provided.

\subsection{Statistical approach}\label{rec:meth_stats}

We utilize a diverse set of basic and advanced performance metrics to capture the dynamics of matches across various leagues and seasons. The inclusion of fundamental statistics is crucial, as they not only provide interpretable insights into team strategies and playing styles but also serve as essential baseline features that complement more complex analytical measures. These fundamental metrics include the \textit{number of passes}, \textit{total shots}, and \textit{shot accuracy}, all of which are core indicators of a team's offensive intent and efficiency.

In addition to these, we incorporate several advanced, preprocessed measures such as \textit{expected goals} (xG), which estimate the probability of a shot resulting in a goal based on historical outcomes of similar attempts. We also analyze passing sequences leading up to shots, and categorize pass height types---ground, low, and high---offering insight into how teams vary their buildup play. Derived metrics such as \textit{passes before shot} (PBS) and \textit{passes completed per possession} (PPP) are also included to quantify the link between passing networks and shot creation.

Furthermore, we develop novel features to enrich our analysis, for instance, \textit{pass center of mass X} captures the average horizontal starting position of completed passes, providing a proxy for a team’s horizontal distribution and spatial tendencies in possession. \textit{Shot distance} is calculated as the Euclidean distance from the location of shot initiation to the center of the goal, capturing the quality and risk associated with shooting decisions.

We also compute \textit{passes under pressure percentage}, defined as the proportion of completed passes made under pressure, normalized over total pass attempts. This measure highlights a team’s ability to retain possession in high-intensity scenarios. \textit{Average pass length} is determined by calculating the Euclidean distance between the initiation and completion coordinates of each pass, providing insight into a team's preferred passing range. A comprehensive list of all statistical features employed, along with descriptions, is presented in \autoref{tab:statsbomb_features}.

\subsection{Network science approaches}
\label{Network Science Approaches}

These statistical measures were complemented and extended through the use of network analysis. A pitch passing network was produced for both teams in a match. This was produced by using the standardized pitch sizes and binning the pitch into an optimal number of regions. The 5-by-10 grid dimensions were chosen as the optimal number of regions for a pitch network is around 49 regions, which is supported by \cite{garrido2020consistency}. An example representation is shown in \autoref{fig:pitch_example}. In a network, the nodes represent the regions while the links represent the passes between these regions. These links are weighted and directional, indicating the flow and frequency of passes between regions throughout the entire match. To further simplify the network, the link weights were normalized by the total network strength. 

We analyze the pitch networks by applying graph- and node-level measures that capture the structural characteristics of a match. For node-level measures, we further calculate the mean and standard deviation. These measures quantify how the ball flows between different pitch locations, revealing patterns of spatial connectivity and preferences in team tactics. A complete overview of all selected measures is provided in Appendix \autoref{node-level}.

