\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
While various methods exist to study football's development, our combined approach captures both broad performance trends and the structural dynamics of team play. The results are organized into three parts: first, we examine the evolution of passing, pace, and gameplay; next, we explore heterogeneities across league, season, and gender; and finally, we analyze pitch-passing networks to uncover shifts in spatial and organizational strategies over time.

\subsection{Evolution of passing, pace, and gameplay}\label{sec:results_stats}

To further examine the tactical evolution trends identified in our analysis, we conducted a detailed investigation of passing statistics across different team performance tiers over the five-season period. \autoref{fig:passes1} displays the results of the analysis of passing statistics across team tiers, showing that top teams consistently completed the highest average number of passes per year (approximately 550 on average), followed by mid-tier teams (around 450 on average), and bottom teams (around 400 on average). This ranking is mirrored in \textit{pass accuracy} (the overall percentage of successful passes), with top teams achieving the highest accuracy, followed by mid-tier and bottom teams. Notably, there is no clear trend in the number of passes or pass accuracy across the five seasons analyzed.

The analysis of passing statistics revealed distinct patterns across team tiers and temporal trends (\autoref{fig:passes2}). PPP, which quantifies the average number of completed passes during each team's ball possession, demonstrate a clear hierarchy with top teams averaging the highest values but also exhibiting the greatest variability, followed by mid-tier teams, while bottom teams show the lowest average values with smaller variability. All three groups display increasing numbers in passes per possession over time, particularly in recent years. The percentage of passes under pressure, remained stable on average across all team tiers, suggesting similar overall exposure to pressure situations. However, all groups exhibit a pronounced temporal increase in passes under pressure, clearly evident through rising median values and expanding interquartile ranges across seasons. PBS, indicating the number of completed passes preceding an attempt to shoot a goal, followed the expected performance hierarchy with top teams recording the highest averages, followed by mid-tier teams, and bottom teams showing slightly lower values, though all three groups demonstrated similar variability. This measure shows, if anything, only a slight increase over time.

In general, the percentage of accurate ground passes is different between men's and women's leagues, with men's leagues averaging between 90\%-95\% (\autoref{fig:passes_shots}A) and women's leagues averaging between 85\%-90\% (\autoref{fig:passes_shots}B)---with percentages increasing gradually in recent years, across most men's and women's leagues. Similarly, the average distance between the shot positions and the goal has gradually decreased in recent years, across most men's and women's leagues (\autoref{fig:passes_shots}C and \autoref{fig:passes_shots}D). This observation complements the parallel trends in recent years: shorter pass lengths and more passes before shots, which may suggest that the additional short passes are used to get closer to the goal prior to a shot.

