\subsection{Heterogeneities across league, season, and gender}\label{sec:results_hetero}

An advantage to the scope and size of the data used in this study, we are able to identify measures that uncovers heterogeneities across leagues, season, genders, and team performance. \autoref{fig:many_subplots_pressure} shows the Percentage of completed passes under pressure for all leagues across team tiers and gender over the last five years, showing a clear increasing trend over the years for the majority of the leagues. In particular, for both English and Spanish leagues, independent of gender, this increase is especially consistent and strong. On the other hand, we find differences in the Italian and German leagues, where the change for the women's leagues are more notable than for the male leagues.

The Italian Serie A Women league's top teams have, on average, lower percentage of passes under pressure (approximately 10\%), followed by slightly higher percentage for the mid tier team (approximately 11\%), and bottom teams has the highest rate (approximately 12\%). The corresponding male league has, on average, 10.5\% of passes under pressure and does not show the increasing pattern as the women's league (compare Panels \autoref{fig:many_subplots_pressure}G and \autoref{fig:many_subplots_pressure}H). Furthermore, the German Frauen Bundesliga's mid-tier teams exhibit a large increase in the 2024-2025 season and the percentage of passes under pressure is for the last season even the highest across all years and genders (see mid tier pattern in \autoref{fig:many_subplots_pressure}F).

Neither the male or female leagues in the United States show a consistent increasing or decreasing percentage of passes under pressure year-to-year, nor consistent variability between team performance levels. The absence of consistent trends in passing under pressure within United States leagues likely reflects the distinct developmental trajectory of football in North America. As a younger and rapidly evolving football ecosystem, American leagues may be experiencing different phases of tactical maturation, where structural changes in league composition and coaching methodologies create more variable patterns rather than the linear evolutionary trends observed in established European competitions.

In sum, the scale of data used in this study offers more precise comparisons between leagues, seasons, genders, and team performance; we see significant evidence that the women's game is changing---sometimes in ways that are commensurate with the men's game and other times not. Along the particular measure used in this analysi, percentage of passes played under pressure, this suggests that both male and female games are becoming more physical or high-pressure in recent years.


\subsection{Analysis of pitch passing networks}\label{sec:results_nets}

In this section, we construct and analyze pitch passing networks for characterizing and comparing teams' styles of play. \autoref{fig:network_outreach} compares the \textit{normalized network outreach}, the sum over all outgoing link weights, multiplied by the Euclidean distance between the pair of nodes, between leagues and genders. This measure captures the average combination of passes between two regions, prioritizing distant but well-connected nodes. Among both men's and women's leagues, the network outreach has decreased in recent years, but this trend is especially clear among women's leagues (\autoref{fig:network_outreach}B). For both genders, the Spanish leagues have, on average, the highest network outreach.

