In addition to the network outreach, there are a range of possible network measures that can characterize the pitch passing networks in this study. \autoref{fig:network_ppn}A shows the distribution of largest eigenvalues of the pitch passing networks, separated by team tier (top, mid, bottom) and season, between the 2020-2021 and 2024-2025 seasons. Higher eigenvalues indicate a stronger and more robust passing network and structural cohesion of the overall ball passing of a match. Top teams exhibit the highest maximum eigenvalue values, while bottom teams show the lowest values, with mid-tier teams falling between these extremes. The variability in maximum eigenvalue is highest for top teams, moderate for mid-tier teams, and lowest for bottom teams. Across the five seasons, there is no consistent temporal trend for any tier team. However, bottom teams display a slight increase in maximum eigenvalue values during recent seasons from 2021-2022 to 2024-2025, suggesting a modest improvement in network robustness and structural cohesion among lower-performing teams over time.

\autoref{fig:network_ppn}B compares the \textit{average shortest path lengths} across top, mid-tier, and bottom teams. The average shortest path length in a pitch passing network captures how easily the ball can circulate through the team's passing structure, with shorter paths indicating more efficient ball circulation. On average, top teams exhibit the shortest passing path length, indicating more efficient passing networks and pointing to their ability of reaching any region of the pitch more easily. Mid-tier teams show slightly higher values, while bottom teams display the highest path lengths. Moreover, top teams show a less spread value distribution with less variability, mid-tier teams exhibit moderate spread, and bottom teams display the most dispersed distributions. In recent seasons, top teams demonstrate a clear increasing pattern in average shortest path length during recent years. This observation is aligned with the fact that the average pass (Euclidean) length has decreased during the last years, probably reducing the existence of shortcuts in the network. In contrast, mid-tier teams show no substantial change across the five seasons, while bottom teams exhibit a possible slight decrease in average shortest path length, indicating a potential improvement in their passing game.

