While the aforementioned trends characterize the general evolution of football across our dataset, it is important to note that not all patterns hold uniformly across every league or gender division. Examining specific competitions reveals distinct evolutionary trajectories that merit individual consideration. For example, the Serie A Women's league exhibits a markedly different pattern in pitch advancement compared to the aggregated trends (\autoref{fig:serieA_women}). The Italian women's competition shows a clear progressive increase in pitch advancement from 2020 to 2025, with the median vertical position advancing from approximately 51 to 55 over this period. Additionally, the variability in pitch advancement, as indicated by the interquartile range, decreases substantially over time, suggesting increased tactical consistency within the league. This contrasts with the relatively stable pitch advancement patterns observed in the aggregated data from other competitions, where median values remain consistent across seasons. Such league-specific variations highlight the heterogeneous nature of tactical evolution in football and emphasize the particular value of analyzing women's competitions separately, as they may be undergoing unique developmental processes distinct from their male counterparts or other women's leagues.


\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

This study analyzed an extensive dataset of football matches spanning the past five years across elite European and American leagues, including the English Premier League, Women's Super League, Major League Soccer, National Women's Soccer League, Bundesliga, Frauen Bundesliga, Serie A, Serie A Women, La Liga, and La Liga F. Our primary objective was to examine the evolution of football across different seasons while identifying meaningful differences between competitions and genders.

To address these research questions, we employed established statistical analysis and network-based methodologies from the sports analytics literature to illuminate patterns of change and consistency across seasons, leagues, and gender divisions. Our analytical approach operated at multiple levels, aggregating data by gender and league while stratifying teams into top-, mid-, and bottom-tier categories for each season. We conducted both cross-gender comparisons and within-gender analyses to draw comprehensive conclusions about competitive dynamics.

Our findings reveal particularly novel and significant insights regarding female competition, where we leveraged an unprecedented volume of match data including multiple seasons to uncover evolving gameplay patterns and strategic approaches to the sport. The comprehensive nature of our dataset, combined with our multi-tiered analytical framework, has enabled us to identify distinctive trends in how football has developed across different competitive contexts.

Our analysis reveals four fundamental trends that characterize the contemporary evolution of football across all competitive levels and genders. From the statistical perspective, pressure intensity is systematically increasing across seasons, indicating a sport-wide shift toward greater physicality in the game. Pass lengths are being progressively reduced, suggesting teams are prioritizing ball security over territorial advancement. Third, the number of passes per possession is steadily increasing, a trend that has been shown to be increasing from before our chosen time period, reflecting more patient, methodical build-up play \cite{gonzalezrodenas2023tactics}. Remarkably, these trends persist regardless of team quality, appearing consistently among top-, mid-, and bottom-tier teams across all leagues studied.

These findings point to a fundamental strategic paradigm shift in modern football: teams are prioritizing ball possession above all else. By reducing pass distances, teams minimize the risk of losing possession through interceptions or misplaced passes, even at the cost of immediate attacking progression. This conservative passing approach naturally extends possession sequences, resulting in longer, more sustained periods of ball control. The corollary to this possession-focused strategy is an intensification of pressing when out of possession. Teams seek to recover the ball as quickly as possible, implementing high-intensity pressing systems that mirror the tactical philosophies popularized by coaches like Pep Guardiola, whose influence on modern football's tactical evolution toward possession-based, high-pressing play is evident in these league-wide trends \cite{violan2014pep}. This tactical convergence across different competitive tiers suggests that modern football's strategic evolution transcends individual team resources or capabilities, representing a sport-wide adaptation to contemporary tactical thinking and player development methodologies.

The network-based analytical approach, based on well-established pitch network representations \cite{garrido2020consistency}, leverages network analysis to gain insights about trends and aspects of modern football. The analyses reveal that network parameters largely do not capture the temporal evolution of football over the past five years. Only network outreach shows a monotonic evolution, decreasing over the years, which aligns with our statistical finding of reduced pass lengths. However, network parameters effectively distinguish between top and bottom-tier teams. Two key results emerge: first, the largest eigenvalue is higher for top teams, indicating that elite teams possess stronger passing networks characterized by more passes and enhanced connectivity between high-centrality network regions \cite{aguirre2013successful}. Second, the average shortest path length is lower for top teams, revealing that elite teams move the ball more efficiently across pitch regions, with higher probability of reaching any area of the pitch.

While our general findings demonstrate consistent trends across competitive levels and genders, a closer examination of specific leagues reveals important nuances that merit attention. For instance, examining the Serie A Women's league (\autoref{fig:serieA_women}) shows a distinct evolutionary pattern in pitch advancement compared to the aggregated trends observed across other competitions. The Serie A Women displays a progressive increase in pitch advancement over the seasons from 2020-2025, with notably higher variability in the earlier seasons that stabilizes in recent years. In contrast, the aggregated data from other leagues shows relatively stable pitch advancement patterns with consistent variability across the same period. This divergence highlights the importance of league-specific analysis, particularly for women's football, where tactical evolution may follow different trajectories than in established male competitions. Such findings underscore the value of our comprehensive dataset in capturing the unique developmental patterns within women's football, suggesting that female competitions may be undergoing distinct tactical modernization processes that warrant dedicated analytical attention rather than broad generalizations across all competitive contexts.

Our results suggest that modern football is evolving toward greater intensity and tactical sophistication. The ability to maintain possession under increasing defensive pressure, combined with the rising number of passes per possession sequence, indicates that both individual player performance and team coordination are improving systematically. These developments enable teams to sustain ball control for extended periods while operating under heightened competitive pressure. Examining performance across different competitive tiers reveals that higher-ranked teams demonstrate superior ball retention capabilities and more efficient attacking sequences. Elite teams exhibit enhanced ability to maintain possession under pressure, though the relationship between pass quantity and scoring efficiency requires careful interpretation. Higher-ranked teams consistently show tactical advantages in their possession management and space utilization across the pitch. The trend of increasing passes per possession, documented from the 2008/09 season onward \cite{gonzálezrodenas2023tactical}, continues to characterize elite football, with our findings extending this pattern through recent seasons across multiple leagues and both male and female competitions.

Several methodological considerations warrant discussion. The comprehensive dataset utilized in this study provides unprecedented analytical opportunities, though data accessibility through proprietary licensing may present reproducibility challenges. The manual annotation methodology employed by data providers ensures high accuracy but may introduce minor observational limitations based on video frame visibility. Our pitch network analysis employed a 5×10 grid configuration (50 nodes total), selected based on established research demonstrating optimal analytical resolution for meaningful network insights while maintaining computational efficiency \cite{garrido2020consistency}.

These trends appear universally across competitive levels and gender divisions, suggesting sport-wide adaptation to modern tactical paradigms. The network analysis reveals that while temporal evolution is subtle at the network level, clear distinctions exist between teams of different competitive tiers, with elite teams exhibiting more efficient and connected passing networks. These findings provide valuable insights for coaches, analysts, and researchers seeking to understand the evolving landscape of modern football tactics and performance.


\paragraph{Future work.}

The methodological framework developed in this study demonstrates considerable versatility and can be readily adapted to investigate tactical evolution at the team level, potentially revealing individual tactical signatures and club-specific patterns. Furthermore, the approach can be extended to incorporate additional football metrics such as defensive actions, set-piece analysis, or player positioning data, providing a flexible foundation for comprehensive football analytics across various competitive contexts.

Several limitations warrant consideration for future research directions. Extending the temporal scope of analysis would strengthen our findings, particularly for men's football, where the professional landscape spans decades and may exhibit longer-cycle evolutionary patterns that our five-season window cannot fully capture. While our study provides valuable cross-gender insights, we recommend that future investigations place greater emphasis on identifying trends and patterns within each gender division rather than direct comparisons between them. This approach acknowledges that men's and women's football, while sharing fundamental tactical principles, operate within distinct professional ecosystems with different developmental trajectories and contextual factors that influence tactical evolution. The women's professional game, being a more recently established competitive environment, may demonstrate unique evolutionary patterns that merit dedicated analytical attention. Additionally, future studies could benefit from normalizing metrics by effective playing time rather than total match duration, accounting for variations in ball-in-play time across different competitions and seasons, which would enhance the precision of temporal trend analysis \cite{spyrou2023interpreting}. This normalization is particularly important as effective time may be higher for teams with superior ball control and possession, while simultaneously accounting for the physical demands and fatigue accumulation that result from extended periods of active play, both of which can influence tactical performance and decision-making patterns \cite{altmann2023effective,tojo2023effective}.

Future work can extend this study in several meaningful directions. Team-specific analysis using pitch passing networks offers a promising approach to identifying unique tactical identities and stylistic signatures. A deeper investigation into evolving trends, particularly the rapid transformation within women’s football leagues, warrants focused attention. Recent studies have highlighted increased investment, professionalization, and tactical sophistication in women’s football, all of which contribute to shifting dynamics in match play. Exploring how these changes manifest in passing networks over time could provide quantitative evidence of this evolution. The development of additional pitch network-based measures could enhance our ability to differentiate elite teams from others. Prior work \cite{pena2012network} has shown that network topology correlates with competitive success, suggesting that more sophisticated descriptors may provide predictive power or deeper tactical insights. Furthermore, efforts should be made to validate and understand why the game is becoming increasingly physical, as suggested by rising averages in the percentage of passes made under pressure. Finally, applying machine learning techniques to pitch passing networks opens opportunities for both descriptive and predictive modeling. 

