\subsection{Node-level measures}\label{node-level}

In the main text, we summarized node-level measures into network-level means and standard deviations. In our initial analyses, we computed the following measures:


\begin{description}
\item[] \textbf{Outreach} is defined as the sum of outgoing link weight times Euclidean distance of the link. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
O_\text{out}(i) = \sum_{j\in \mathcal{N}(i)} w(i, j) l(i, j)
\end{equation}
where $l(i, j)$ is the Euclidean distance. This measure represents a combination of passes between two regions, prioritizing distant but well-connected nodes.

\item[] \textbf{In-Strength} is defined as the sum of weights of all incoming links to a node. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
S_{in}(i) = \sum_{j\in V} w(j,i)
\end{equation}
This metric represents the total number of passes received by a pitch position and measures how often the ball arrives at this location.

\item[] \textbf{Out-Strength} is defined as the sum of weights of all outgoing links from a node. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
S_{out}(i) = \sum_{j\in V} w(i,j)
\end{equation}
This metric represents the total number of passes originated from a pitch position and measures how active this location is in ball distribution.

\item[] \textbf{In-Closeness Centrality} is defined as the average distance from all other nodes to node $i$. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
Cl_{\text{in}}(i) = \frac{1}{\sum_{j \neq v} d(j,i)}
\end{equation}
where $d(j,i)$ is the shortest path distance in number of steps. The distance of a link is the inverse of the link's weight. This measure indicates how easily the ball arrives to node $i$ from any other node in the pitch.

\item[] \textbf{Out-Closeness Centrality} is defined as the average distance between node and all other nodes. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
Cl^{\text{out}}(i) = \frac{1}{\sum_{j \neq i} d(i,j)}
\end{equation}
where $d(i,j)$ is the shortest path distance in number of steps. The distance of a link is the inverse of the link's weight. This measure indicates how easily the ball arrives to any other node $j$ in the pitch from node $i$.

\item[] \textbf{Betweenness centrality} measures the extent to which nodes in the network are on paths between other nodes. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
B(i) = \sum_{s, t\in V} \frac{\sigma(s,t|i)}{\sigma(s,t)}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(s,t)$ is the number of total shortest paths between nodes $s$ and $t$, and $\sigma(s,t|i)$ is those passing through $i\notin \{s,t\}$. This metric identifies the most commonly passed through region of pitch.

\item[] \textbf{Eigenvector centrality} of node $i$ is obtained as the $i$th element of the eigenvector associated to the largest eigenvalue of the weighted adjacency matrix. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
E(i)=v_1(i)
\end{equation}
where $v_1$ is the eigenvector associated to the largest eigenvalue of the weighted adjacency matrix. This measure represents the importance of the pitch-position within the team's passing network. In programming implementation, a small offset is used to avoid problems with sinks by adding small weight to non-links.

\end{description}

\subsection{Graph-level measures}
In the main text, we compared the network outreach, largest eigenvalue, and the average shortest path lengths of the pitch passing networks. However, in our initial analyses, we computed the following measures:

\begin{description}

\item[] \textbf{Isolated nodes} are defined as the number of isolated nodes. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
|I| = |{i\in V : deg(i) = 0}|
\end{equation}
This measure identifies which areas/positions are not used to pass or receive a ball.

\item[] \textbf{Sink Nodes} are defined as the number of sink nodes. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
S_\text{sink}(G) = |{i \in V: deg^{\text{out}}(i) = 0, deg^{\text{in}}(i) > 0}|
\end{equation}
This measure identifies which areas/positions receive the ball, but never have outgoing passes.

\item[] \textbf{Source Nodes} are defined as the number of source nodes. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
S_\text{source}(G) = |{i \in V: deg^{\text{in}}(i) = 0, deg^{\text{out}}(i) > 0}|
\end{equation}
This measure identifies which areas/positions pass the ball, but never have incoming passes.

\item[] \textbf{Self-Loops} are defined as self-loops in the network. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
L(G) = \{(i, i) \in E\}
\end{equation}
This measure represents the number of passes that start and end in the same region. A ball is considered to start/end in a region with top-left corner at $(x_\text{min}, y_\text{max})$ and bottom-right corner at $(x_\text{max}, y_\text{min})$ if for its start/end-coordinates $(x_B, y_B)$ holds $x_\text{min} < x_B \leq x_\text{max}$ and $y_\text{min} \leq y_B < y_\text{max}$. The total number, the value of the maximum self-loop, and the average weight of self-loops are calculated.

\item[] \textbf{Strength} is defined as the sum of weights of all links. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
S(G) = \sum_{(i,j)\in E} w(i,j)
\end{equation}
This measure represents the total number of passes.

\item[] \textbf{Average Shortest Path Length} is defined as the average over all shortest paths of the network, where the distance of a link is the inverse of the link's weight. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
SP_\text{avg}(G) = \frac{n}{n - 1}\sum_{i, j \in V: i\neq j} d(i,j)
\end{equation}
with $d(i, j)$ being the shortest path from node $i$ to $j$, and $n$ is the total number of nodes. This measure captures how efficiently the ball can circulate through the team's passing structure.

\item[] \textbf{Degree Assortativity} is defined as the correlation coefficient between (in-/out-) degrees of nodes and the (in-/out-) degrees of connected nodes. In directed networks, four different assortativities can be calculated according to the degree of the starting and ending node of a given link: in-in, out-out, in-out, out-in. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
A(G) = \frac{\sum_k e_{kk} - \sum_k a_kb_k}{1 - \sum_k a_kb_k}
\end{equation}
where $e_{k k^\prime}$ is the fraction of links in a network that connects a node of (in-/out-)degree $k$ to one of (in-/out-)degree $k^\prime$, and $a_k$ the sum of all links departing in a node with (in-/out-)degree $k$, and $b_{k^\prime}$ the sum of the links ending in nodes with (in-/out-)degree $k^\prime$. This measure indicates whether pitch positions that start/receive many passes tend to connect to other positions that also start/receive many passes.

\item[] \textbf{Largest Eigenvalue} is defined as the principal eigenvalue of the weighted adjacency matrix. The formula is 
\begin{equation}
E_\text{val}(i)=\lambda_1(i)
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_1$ is the largest eigenvalue of the weighted adjacency matrix. The largest eigenvalue is related to the network strength, robustness, and structural cohesion.

\end{description}





\subsection{Example interpretation of the pitch-passing network}  We employed multiple approaches to interpret the Hudl Statsbomb data for our analysis. One such method is the pitch passing network, as illustrated in \autoref{fig:fig1}. This visualization provides insight into the game's dynamics by capturing and representing passing patterns across different areas of the pitch.\\

\begin{itemize}
    \item[] \textbf{Summary of Match:} Arsenal and Liverpool played to an exciting 2-2 draw, with both sides exchanging leads and showcasing contrasting styles, Arsenal's controlled possession and Liverpool's rapid transitions.

    \item[] \textbf{Explaining the Pitch-passing Network:} 
    Arsenal’s passing network reveals frequent self-loops, indicating short, repeated exchanges between defenders and midfielders such as Rice and Merino. These patterns highlight a patient, possession-based style of play, where the ball is often recycled through central areas to maintain control and build attacks gradually. In contrast, Liverpool’s network under Arne Slot features fewer self-loops. Their approach relies on quick, progressive passing, with fullbacks like Alexander-Arnold and Robertson frequently pushing the ball into advanced positions. This style reduces repeated passes to the same player and emphasizes fast, forward movement.

    \item[] \textbf{Explaining self-loops:} Arsenal’s passing network would display prominent self-loops, representing short and repeated passes between players such as defenders and midfielders like Rice and Merino. These patterns reflect their patient buildup and possession-oriented approach, where play is often recycled through central areas. In contrast, Liverpool’s network would show fewer self-loops. Under Arne Slot, their style focused on quick and progressive passes, especially from fullbacks like Alexander-Arnold and Robertson into more advanced zones. This approach minimized repeated passes to the same player and prioritized rapid ball movement up the pitch.


\end{itemize}


