\paragraph{Parallelization} %
The GPU-accelerated feasibility check is outlined in \cref{algo:lb_dffs_par}.
% and it is equivalent to parallelizing the first two outermost loops of \cref{algo:lb_dffs}.
The core of the parallelization is a kernel named \emph{calcDffLowerBound}, which is responsible for calculating the lower bounds derived from the different parameters of a DFF and keeping track of the tightest one (see \cref{algo:lb_dffs_kernel}).
%\todo[color=yellow]{In algo 3, $lb$ is the result of "reducing" the lower bound from each DFF right? Taking the $\max$. COrrect? If so, can we make this explicit?}
Concurrently running different copies of the kernel, each working with a different DFF, parallelizes the first outermost loop of \cref{algo:lb_dffs}. Using the threads to calculate the lower bounds parallelizes the second outermost loop (see \cref{fig:lb_par}).
Each thread is responsible for a distinct parameter value, requiring that $nThreads \geq \overline{\lambda} - \underline{\lambda} + 1$. 
This condition is satisfied by launching each kernel with $\left\lceil\frac{\overline{\lambda} - \underline{\lambda} + 1}{\ncs}\right\rceil$ blocks of size $\ncs$, where $\ncs$ is the number of CUDA Cores per Streaming Multiprocessors.  With a bin capacity of $2000$, a current high-end GPU  achieves full parallelization of up to 8 DFFs, with a linear performance penalty for each additional DFF.

