However, since the expressions (\ref{Sb1}-\ref{Sb9}) from bubble diagrams in Fig.\ref{fig:Fig41} (\ref{bub9}) contain type of integrals which are not UV finite by power counting rule the Lebesque’s theorem does not hold, thus the complete loop integration and limit $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ in {\rm(\ref{bub9})} do not commute. Consequently, after the full $3D$ integrations the above vanishing of numerators (\ref{limNbub}) does not hold anymore for (\ref{bub9}), i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{\theta\to 0}\;S^{bub}=\lim_{\theta\to 0}\;\sum_{r=1}^9 S^{bub}_r (p_1,p_2,p_3;\theta)\not=0,
\label{limSbub}
\end{equation}
and, due to its non-planar parts, the $S^{bub}$ is IR unstable.  

So, at this point we shall not continue with full computation of the 4-point function $S^{bub}$ (\ref{bub9}) since it contain IR divergent tadpole type (\ref{B.1}) of integral $I_1^0(\tilde k)$ (\ref{B.6}). To handle that situation we isolate non-planar parts of the sum $S^{bub}$ and add it to the non-planar part of tadpole diagram $S^{tad}$, yielding surprisingly good result. Presentation of  that we continue in next section.

\section{Non-planar $(\cal NP)$ bubble and tadpole contributions and $\theta^{\mu\nu}$ limits}

\subsection{$\cal (NP)$ bubble diagram contributions to the 4 scalar field 4-point functions}

Using results from Figs.\ref{fig:Fig41} and Eqs. (\ref{Sb1}-\ref{Sb9}), after some lengthy algebra, we can write the following total bubble contribution coming from the sum in dimensional regularisation non-planar diagrams,  
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\sum_{r=1}^9 {(\cal NP)}S^{bub}_r(p_1,p_2,p_3;\theta)=\delta_{A_2}^{A_1}\delta_{A_3}^{A_4}
%delta_{A_2}^{\;\;A_1}\delta_{A_3}^{\;\;A_4}
\nonumber\\
&\cdot&
\Big\{
\Big[-4\cos{\frac{(p_1+p_3)\theta(p_1+p_2)}{2}}+16\cos{\frac{(p_1-p_3)\theta(p_1+ p_2)}{2}}\Big]
I^0_1(\tilde p_1+\tilde p_2)
\nonumber\\
&+&\Big[8\cos{\frac{(p_1+p_2)\theta(p_1+ p_3)}{2}}-32\cos{\frac{(p_1-p_2)\theta(p_1+ p_3)}{2}}\Big]
I^0_1(\tilde p_1+\tilde p_3)
\nonumber\\
&+&4(p_1+p_2)^2\cos{\frac{(p_1+p_3)\theta(p_1+ p_2)}{2}}
I^0_2(p_1+p_2;\tilde p_1+\tilde p_2)
\nonumber\\
&-&
4(p_2+p_3)^2\Big[
e^{-\frac{i}{2}(p_1-p_3)\theta( p_1+ p_2)}
I^0_2(p_2+p_3;\tilde p_1+\tilde p_2)
\nonumber\\
&&\hspace{2cm}+e^{\frac{i}{2}(p_1-p_3)\theta(p_1+ p_2)}
I^0_2(p_2+p_3;-\tilde p_1-\tilde p_2)\Big]
\nonumber\\
&-&
4(p_1+p_2)^2
e^{-\frac{i}{2}(p_1+p_3)\theta( p_1+ p_2)}
I^0_2(p_1+p_2;\tilde p_1+\tilde p_3)
\nonumber\\
&+&8(p_1+p_3)^2\cos{\frac{(p_1-p_2)\theta( p_1+ p_3)}{2}}
I^0_2(p_1+p_2;\tilde p_1+\tilde p_3)
\nonumber\\
&+&
4(p_2+p_3)^2\Big[
e^{-\frac{i}{2}(p_1-p_2)\theta(p_1+ p_3)}I^0_2(p_2+p_3;\tilde p_1+\tilde p_3)
\nonumber\\
&&\hspace{2cm}+e^{\frac{i}{2}(p_1-p_2)\theta( p_1+ p_3)}I^0_2(p_2+p_3;-\tilde p_1-\tilde p_3)\Big]\Big\}
\nonumber\\
&+&\Big\{same\;\;as\;\;above\;\;  with\;\;(2\leftrightarrow 3)\Big\}.
\label{tnpbub}
\end{eqnarray}
Solution to the above tadpole type integral $I^0_1(\tilde k)$ (\ref{B.6}) shows linear IR divergence when $\tilde k\to0$, while bubble type integral $I^0_2(p;\tilde k)$ (\ref{B.8}) has well defined $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to0$ limit with finite value given in (\ref{B.9}).

\subsection{$\cal (NP)$ tadpole diagram contribution to the 4 scalar field 4-point functions}

Using six-scalar fields vertices in Fig.\ref{fig:FigFRCorr} from Appendix A, with $\pi-,\sigma-$permutations of all fields expressed in Feynman rule (\ref{V6}-\ref{V1234}), we compute all one-loop diagrams shown generically as one 1-loop tadpole diagram in Fig.\ref{fig:Fig4-6tad},
\begin{equation}
{(\cal NP)}S^{tad}(p_1,p_4,p_2,p_3;\theta)=\int \frac{d^D \ell}{(2\pi)^D}
\frac{V_6(p_1,p_4,\ell|\ell,p_2,p_3;\theta)}{\ell^2},
\label{Stad}
\end{equation}
in terms of external momenta, where contracted terms from (\ref{V6}), and (\ref{V1234}) are as follows:
\begin{figure}[t]
\begin{center}
%\includegraphics[width=12cm,angle=0]{S1tad.pdf}
\includegraphics[width=12cm,angle=0]{S1tad.eps}
\end{center}
\caption{Scalar 6-field tadpole diagram contributing to the 4-correlators (4-point function) $S^{tad}$. Arrow on the diagram lines indicate the flow of the charge, while separate uparrows indicate flow of all incoming momenta.}
\label{fig:Fig4-6tad}
\end{figure}
\begin{eqnarray}
&&V_6(p_1,p_4,\ell|\ell,p_2,p_3;\theta)=\delta_{A_2}^{A_1}\delta_{A_3}^{A_4}
\nonumber\\
\cdot
\Big\{&-&6\;e^{-i\ell\theta(p_1+p_2)}e^{\frac{i}{2}(p_1\theta p_2+p_3\theta p_4)}
+12\;e^{-i\ell\theta(p_1+p_3)}e^{\frac{i}{2}(p_1\theta p_3+p_2\theta p_4)}
\nonumber\\
&-&6\;e^{i\ell\theta(p_1+p_2)}e^{-\frac{i}{2}(p_1\theta p_2+p_3\theta p_4)}
+12\;e^{i\ell\theta(p_1+p_3)}e^{-\frac{i}{2}(p_1\theta p_3+p_2\theta p_4)}
\Big\}
\nonumber\\
&+&\Big\{same\;\;as\;\;above\;\;  with\;\;(2\leftrightarrow 3)\Big\}.
\label{VTadpole}
\end{eqnarray}

Taking into account the $\pi-,\sigma-$permutations of all fields expressed in Feynman rules (\ref{V6}-\ref{V1234}) and (\ref{VTadpole}) we found that noncommutative limit for numerator holds:
\begin{equation}
\lim_{\theta\to 0}\; V_6 (p_1,p_2,p_3;\theta)=0.
\label{limtad}
\end{equation}
However, since the tadpole type integrals are not UV finite by power counting rule, note again that the loop integration and limit $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ do not commute. Finally, after changing variable $\ell\to-\ell^\prime$ in the first line of nominator  (\ref{VTadpole}) and integrating (\ref{Stad}), the total tadpole contribution being non-planar in dimensional regularization is: 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&{(\cal NP)}S^{tad}(p_1,p_2,p_3;\theta)=
\delta^{A_1}_{A_2}\delta^{A_4}_{A_3}
\nonumber\\
&\cdot&
\Big\{-12I^0_1(\tilde p_1+\tilde p_2)\cos{\frac{(p_1-p_3)\theta( p_1+ p_2)}{2}}
+24I^0_1(\tilde p_1+\tilde p_3)\cos{\frac{(p_1-p_2)\theta(p_1+ p_3)}{2}}\Big\}
\nonumber\\
&+&\Big\{same\;\;as\;\;above\;\;  with\;\;(2\leftrightarrow3) \Big\}.
\label{Stad1}
\end{eqnarray}

\subsection{The $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ limits for the $\cal (NP)$ part of bubble plus tadpole diagrams}

It is plane from previous subsections that the limit $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ of each box (\ref{B.4}) and triangle (\ref{B.3}) types of integrals is well-defined, and that this limit agrees with corresponding result in three-dimensional ordinary Minkowski space. Indeed,  if one sets $\theta^{\mu\nu}=0$ in the integrand of the integrals which make up those Feynman diagrams one obtains a collection of integrals which are all UV finite by power counting, and therefore the Lebesgue’s theorem holds, i.e. the limiting procedures and integrations do commute, thus the limit $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ can be taken under integrals. 

On the other hand, if one sets $\theta^{\mu\nu}= 0$ in the integrand of each bubble diagram, one obtains a Feynman integral which is UV divergent by power counting, and therefore the non-planar contribution coming from that integral gives rise to the noncommutative IR divergence, since the non-planar part of tadpole diagram for $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ diverges \footnote{Each bubble diagram contribution (\ref{Sb1}-\ref{Sb9}) contain integral $I_1^0(\tilde k)$ (\ref{B.5}), linearly divergent for $\tilde k \to0$ (\ref{B.6}), which is source of the UV/IR mixing effect in any Moyal based NCQFT \cite{Martin:1999aq,Minwalla:1999px,Hayakawa:1999yt,Matusis:2000jf,VanRaamsdonk:2000rr,VanRaamsdonk:2001jd,Schupp:2008fs,Horvat:2011bs,Martin:2020ddo}.}. So, the limit $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ of each bubble diagram does not exist, limits actually diverges.

However, taking into account that integrals of $I^0_2(p,\tilde k)$ types from (\ref{tnpbub}) has finite value (\ref{B.8}), i.e. it has well-defined limit for $\tilde k \to 0$ (\ref{B.9}), and since tadpole type integral $I^0_1(\tilde k)$ (\ref{B.6}) in $3D$ has a form: 
\begin{equation}
I^0_1(\tilde k)=\frac{Constant}{\sqrt{\tilde k^2}},
\label{1I_1}
\end{equation}
it is clear that the only source which realizes as noncommutative linear IR divergent contributions to the scalar 4-correlator, is the above integral $I^0_1(\tilde k)$. In Eqs (\ref{tnpbub}) and (\ref{Stad1}) such integrals are $I^0_1({\tilde p}_1 + {\tilde p}_2)$ and $I^0_1({\tilde p}_1 + \tilde p_3)$. And yet, by expanding in power series the cosines in Eqs (\ref{tnpbub}) and (\ref{Stad1}), after summing up all of them and extracting IR divergent $I^0_1$ integrals only, we obtain the following leading contribution
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\hspace{-1cm}{(\cal NP)}\Big[S^{bub}(p_1,p_2,p_3;\theta)+S^{tad}(p_1,p_2,p_3;\theta)\Big]
\nonumber\\
&=&{\Big\{\delta^{A_1}_{A_2}\delta^{A_4}_{A_3}
\big[-4+16-12\big]+\delta^{A_1}_{A_3}\delta^{A_4}_{A_2}
\big[8-32+24\big]\Big\}I^0_1({\tilde p}_1+{\tilde p}_2)}
\nonumber\\
&+&\Big\{\delta^{A_1}_{A_3}\delta^{A_4}_{A_2}\big[-4+16-12\big]+\delta^{A_1}_{A_2}\delta^{A_4}_{A_3}\big[8-32+24\big]
\Big\}I^0_1({\tilde p}_1+{\tilde p}_3),
\label{I_1+2}
\end{eqnarray}
Sum of all these non-planar divergent contributions obviously vanishes, so it is not divergent when $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$, but what is more important, it has a well-defined finite limit.

The next-to-leading  and higher order contributions from the sum of bublble $S^{bub}$ (\ref{tnpbub}), and tadpole $S^{tad}$ (\ref{Stad1}),  are of the type
\begin{equation}
\frac{{\tilde k}^{\mu_1}}{\sqrt{{\tilde k}^2}} {\tilde k}^{\mu_2}...{\tilde k}
^{\mu_{2n}}T_{\mu_1...\mu_{2n}}(p_1,p_2,p_3),
\label{tildek1}
\end{equation}
with $n\geq 1$, ${\tilde k}^{\mu}=({\tilde p}_1 + {\tilde p}_2)^\mu$ or
$({\tilde p}_1 + {\tilde p}_3)^\mu$
and $T_{\mu_1...\mu_{2n}}(p_1,p_2,p_3)$ being a Lorentz tensor which is a polynomial in terms of external momenta $(p_1,p_2,p_3)$. 
Each of these contributions vanishes as ${\tilde k}^{\mu}=\theta^{\mu\nu}k_\nu\to 0$.   

We then conclude that the limit $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ of the non-planar diagrams obtained after summing bubble (\ref{tnpbub}) and tadpole (\ref{Stad1}) ($\cal NP$) contributions exists and coincides with the result that is obtained by setting $\theta^{\mu\nu}=0$ in the integrand of relevant integrals arising from the bubble and tadpole diagrams in Figs.\ref{fig:Fig41} and \ref{fig:Fig4-6tad}, respectively. %$\phantom{XXXXXXXXXX..}$Q.E.D.\\

$\phantom{So, in this Sections we have shown vanishing of all discussed scalar-gauge XXX}$Q.E.D.\\

Let us stress another fact here, that is that the cancellation of the noncommutative IR divergence upon adding all non-planar contributions does not imply by itself that the limit $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ exists for a contributions of the type
\begin{equation}
I^0_1(\tilde p_1+\tilde p_2)\sin{\frac{(p_1-p_3)\theta(p_1+ p_2)}{2}},
\label{5.9}
\end{equation}
which are not divergent as $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$, but its value depends on the way $\theta^{\mu\nu}$ approaches to zero. It is the fact that cosines, rather than sines, occur in (\ref{tnpbub}) and (\ref{Stad1}), which is instrumental in having a well-defined limit.

