The UV degree of divergence $\cal D$ of each diagram in Fig.\ref{fig:Fig6-pointfirstclass}, from power counting formula\footnote{See some explanation from  Eq. (4.1) in Section 4 of ref. \cite{Martin:2017nhg}.}, is zero. This degree is obtained by taking into account polynomial coming from $\epsilon^{\mu\nu\rho}(\ell-2 p)_\nu\ell_\rho$, which has quadratic power in the loop momenta $\ell$. But this quadratic polynomial vanishes due to the antisymmetry of the Levi-Civita symbol. So the integrals with a non-vanishing integrand  contributing to each diagram in Fig.\ref{fig:Fig6-pointfirstclass} are UV convergent by power-counting and therefore absolutely convergent for non-exceptional momenta. Hence, the Lebesgue's theorem can be applied and one can take the limit $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ under the loop integral sign, thus each diagram in Fig.\ref{fig:Fig6-pointfirstclass} converges to commutative counterpart.

Type of Feynman diagrams that are in the second category are shown in Figs.\ref{fig:FigCS6-p-loop}, and they have UV degree of divergence ${\cal D}$ also equal to zero. However this zero value comes from a bit of numerator of the integrand which, as a factor, has the following polynomial in the loop momentum $\ell$:
%\begin{equation*}
$\ell^a\epsilon_{\mu a\nu}\ell^b\epsilon_{\nu b\rho}\ell^c\epsilon_{\rho c\mu}.$
%\end{equation*}
This degree is due to the contribution to the numerators of their integrands which have the common structure,  
%\begin{equation*}
${\rm tr}\big[\slashed{\ell}\slashed{\ell}\slashed{\ell}\big]$, 
%\end{equation*}
as a factor. Since that ${\rm tr}$ in the previous expression stands for the fermi-statistic trace, we conclude that 
previous polynomial in $\ell$ vanishes. So, actually the integrals with a non-vanishing integrand generated by diagrams in Fig.\ref{fig:FigCS6-p-loop} are UV finite by power-counting and, hence, they are absolutely convergent for non-exceptional momenta. Again, here the Lebesgue's theorem can be applied to each diagram and one can take the limit $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ under the loop integrals, producing its commutative  counterpart.\\

In summary we have proven in this Section that all integrals with a non-vanishing integrand from Figs.\ref{fig:Fig6-pointfirstclass}, and \ref{fig:FigCS6-p-loop} which contribute to 1PI scalar 6-point functions are UV finite by power-counting and hence absolutely convergent  for non-exceptional momenta. The Lebesgue's dominated convergence theorem than guarantees for each individual diagram that performing limiting procedure ($\tilde{p}^{\mu}_i=\theta^{\mu\nu}p_\nu\to 0$) and integration over the loop momenta procedure do commute. 

This implies that the scalar 6-point function of the NCABJM theory in $\theta^{\mu\nu}\to 0$ limit are given by the scalar 6-point function of the corresponding ordinary ABJM theory.
$\phantom{XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX.}$Q.E.D.

