\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{T} he Linux kernel, powering 96\% of the top 1 million web servers \cite{linux_stats} and over 3.3 billion Android devices \cite{android_stats}, is increasingly utilized in spaceborne computers \cite{miller2023space, leppinen2017current, beck2024evaluation, ramesh2004embedded, prieto2004linux} using commercial-off-the-shelf (COTS) system-on-chip (SoC) platforms.
The paradigm shift is due to cost-effectiveness and the rapid deployment cycle that Linux (software) and COTS SoCs (hardware) enable in the NewSpace era \cite{commandfuture, sweeting2018modern, peeters2024paradigm, sehovic2018newspace} to their radiation-hardened counterparts.
NASA's 2020 Ingenuity Mars Helicopter ran Linux on a COTS Snapdragon 801 SoC, completing 72 flights in $\approx$ 33 Earth months, 14 times its initial 5-flight goal \cite{balaram2018mars}.
However, modern COTS SoCs, designed for low-radiation terrestrial environments, are inherently susceptible to radiation-induced soft errors (transient errors) \cite{baumann2005radiation,budroweit2021risk, estela2019cots}.
These transient faults \cite{556863}, caused by a single bit flip, can trigger immediate Linux crashes \cite{freschl2005swif}, such as those caused by NULL pointer dereference.
Thus, investigating how the unmodified Linux kernel handles soft errors (without ECC) in COTS SoCs is crucial for identifying kernel's inherent fault-handling weaknesses and guiding reliability improvements to complement ECCs in spaceborne computers.

