\section {Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\subsection{Irradiation Experiments: Testing Linux on Modern SoCs}

* Corley et al. \cite{corley2023accelerated} conducted a neutron irradiation study to compare the SEU and SEFI cross-section of Raspberry Pi 3B+ (ARM Cortex-A53 SoC) and Raspberry Pi 4 (ARM Cortex-A72 SoC), both running Linux.
For SEUs, they ran benchmarks in two suites, Suite I and Suite II. Suite II modified Suite I's March code with varying test array sizes from tiny to large test size bytes (i.e. 40 bytes to 12.8 MB).
They ran tests in three settings: running one benchmark on one of the four cores, running four similar benchmarks on all four cores, and running four benchmarks on one core for differing array sizes.
For Suite I, the SEU/bit and SEFI/device for one process on one of four cores varied by up to 5× and 2×, respectively. 
In Suite II, with larger memory footprints, these differences increased to 8× and 6×, respectively, primarily due to proportionally higher single-bit upsets (SBUs) from larger array sizes.

In their study, kernel paging request errors and kernel Oops remained dominant, similar to their previous test on Pi 3B+.
For kernel Oops, custom signal handlers were employed within the test benchmarks, including SIGSEGV, SIGILL, and SIGFPE, to avoid simple classification of Oops as SEFI errors.
Further analysis showed that some SEFI-like events were due to SBUs in the offending instructions, indicating the need for the appropriate classification of SEUs as not SEFIs.
They concluded that Pis, without ECC, exhibit moderate, yet insufficient, resilience to neutron-induced faults and that although the Linux OS offers easy error classifications, the OS memory footprints themselves increase SEU susceptibility.

* In a NASA study by Guertin~\cite{guertin2022raspberry}, Raspberry Pi 3B Plus and Pi Zero were subjected to SEE and TID analysis.  
For SEE analysis with heavy-ion exposure, ion energy around 20~MeV/amu was chosen, and the linear energy transfer (LET) were around $0.58{-}8.95~\mathrm{MeV{\cdot}cm^2/mg}$.  
The processors running Linux were reported to crash at all times under the beam with Linux exceptions or error messages.  
However, no such Linux error logs were collected, and no bit errors were examined.  
Their SEFI cross-section reveals an issue with the particle's range to traverse the flip-chip fully around $6{-}7~\mathrm{MeV{\cdot}cm^2/mg}$, although for LETs below $6~\mathrm{MeV{\cdot}cm^2/mg}$, the SEFI cross-section rates appeared nearly comparable.  
Interestingly, an automatic reset caused by SEFI distorted the boot screen's video output.  
Notably, the Pi Zero series employs either a Package-on-Package (PoP) or System-in-Package (SiP) die-packaging setup, which vertically stacks the SoC and DRAM.  
This design presents a testing challenge that requires the test particles to traverse the stack to reach the flip-chip or impact-sensitive junction.  
Guertin introduced the Raspberry Pi for Space Guidelines \cite{guertin2022raspberryguide} and the Board Level Proton Testing Book of Knowledge \cite{guertin2017board}, which offers insights into proton irradiation and system reliability for Raspberry Pis.

* Miller et al., in their irradiation experiment \cite{miller2023space}, evaluated the reliability of their Space Operating Linux (SOL).
SOL is a minimal Yocto-based OS for the Nvidia Jetson TX2i, designed for LEO orbits, featuring a TMR-verified boot process to mitigate radiation-induced corruption on critical boot files.
Their fault-injection experiments confirmed that corrupt bits were found in the boot partition and were corrected with each reboot. 
However, without the TMR-boot procedure, the standard Linux for Tegra (L4T) on the same SoC failed to reboot without such a TMR mechanism, highlighting the efficacy of their method. 
Furthermore, they proposed RAM-based file systems to reduce the dependence on eMMC flash storage, which is known to be highly susceptible to radiation effects. 
The expected error rates for SOL devices were also reduced by roughly a third compared to L4T under solar minimum and solar maximum projections.
They estimate less than four reboots per year of up-time on SOL for ISS orbits and assess the overall reliability of the TX2i SoC as adequate to operate under radiation effects. 
However, they suggest decoupling the chip from a few unreliable SoC components, such as the vulnerable embedded MultiMediaCard (eMMC), a non-volatile memory.

* Wyrwas~\cite{wyrwas2017proton,wyrwas2019proton} analyzed Linux SEE responses on NVIDIA Jetson TX1 and TX2 SoCs (20 nm) under 200 MeV proton irradiation. 
For TX1, they employed particles and fluidsGL simulations to maintain low CPU usage and high GPU performance under radiation. 
Upsets occurred in all 16 test runs across varying proton fluences, fluxes, loads, and temperatures, with distinct Linux crash conditions, such as “Invalid Instruction”, “Undefined Instruction”, “Network failure”, “Kernel paging request Error”, “Level 2 translation fault”, “Level 3 permission fault”, or “Crash of Wi-Fi driver, bus failure, etc.” 
Memory analysis revealed corrupted virtual addresses, including NULL pointer access (PGD NULL), likely due to page table faults. 
Some runs showed 8-bit flips, suggesting cascaded errors from a single upset.
For TX2, all tests resulted in SEFI failures requiring power cycling. 
In their estimation, TX2 exhibited a smaller cross section than TX1, by at least an order of magnitude. 
Jetson SoCs are renowned for AI/ML acceleration in edge computing.

* Wali et al. investigated how running a Linux OS on an SRAM-based FPGA SoC with a RISC-V core influences the system's resilience to SBUs in the FPGA configuration memory ~\cite{wali2020analyzing}.
In their fault injection experiment, they inject 10,000 random faults across the FPGA's configuration memory, particularly to track the occurrences of masked errors, SDCs, and SEFIs, and detect any architectural internal failures (AIFs).
They found that employing OSes increases the SEFI rate by 3.6 times that of the bare-metal setup, highlighting the downside of the long reboot time of GP-OSes.
Furthermore, they argue that configuration memory upsets are mainly independent of the workload nature, suggesting that upsets predominantly disrupt control logic rather than data paths.
