\subsubsection{DUT 1:Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W (40 nm CMOS)}
The DUT~1 showed a Linux-SEFI cross-section \(\sigma(\text{Observed})\) of $6.95^{+6.85}_{-3.99}\times10^{-9}$ at 30 MeV, the highest among DUT 1 and 2, and $2.84^{+1.70}_{-1.18}\times10^{-9}\ \text{cm}^2\quad\text{(at 58 MeV)}$ its lowest, as detailed in Table~\ref{tab:rpi_cc}'s (95\% confidence intervals), derived from Table~\ref{tab:rpi_pl}. 
At the upper bound, DUT 1's cross-section of $6.95^{+6.85}_{-3.99}\times10^{-9}$ cm$^2$ is closely comparable to DUT 3's at $7.63^{+6.87}_{-4.23}\times10^{-9}$ cm$^2$.
Nearly all tests, except one at 25 MeV, resulted in Linux crashes, as summarized in Table \ref{tab:rpi_pl}.

During irradiation, the proton beam targeted the SiP (RP3A0-AU), containing a quad-core ARM Cortex-A53 SoC and 4 Gbit Micron LPDDR2 RAM.
SiPs are known for their cost-effective 3D die-stacking packaging, requiring particles with sufficient LET to penetrate deeper layers of the die and induce radiation-induced failures, as depicted in Figure \ref{fig:rpi_sip} of the RP3A0-SiP structures.

\subsubsection{DUT 2: NXP i.MX 8M Plus Processor (14 nm FinFET)}
DUT 2 consistently exhibited the lowest Linux-SEFI cross-section, 5–14x lower than DUT 1 (which also features the ARM Cortex-A53 SoC) and DUT 3, as detailed in Table \ref{tab:imx8mp_cc}.
For instance, the highest $\sigma$ was observed at 30 MeV ($1.41^{+1.88}_{-0.96}\times10^{-9}\ \text{cm}^2$) and lowest at 20 MeV ($1.98^{+1.30}_{-0.87}\times10^{-10}\ \text{cm}^2$), i.e. $14.4\times$ lower than DUT 1’s lowest $\sigma$ (Observed) $2.84^{+1.70}_{-1.18}\times10^{-9}$ cm$^2$ at 58 MeV.
Notably, 3 out of 37 Linux test runs completed without crashes in their intended beam run-time, as shown in Table \ref{tab:imx8mp_pl}.
The i.MX 8M Plus SoC, built on a 14 nm Low-Power Compact (LPC) FinFET process, is known for improved current control and reduced charge leakage with its 3D gate structure to bulk CMOS \cite{nxp_neural_processing}.
During irradiation, the proton beam targeted the SoC (from the top) and 4 GB LPDDR4 module. 
The SoC integrates a GPU, NPU, connectivity interfaces, and various I/O peripherals.
Unlike SiPs, SoMs arrange components horizontally onto a shared PCB substrate, ensuring a relatively uniform exposure to the incident radiation beam.
A Weibull fit for this DUT was estimated from its $\sigma_{\text{min}}$–$\sigma_{\text{max}}$ range, as shown in Table \ref{tab:weibull_params}.

\subsubsection{DUT 3: OrangeCrab v2 FPGA (40 nm CMOS)}
DUT 3 exhibited the highest Linux-SEFI cross-section \(\sigma(\text{Observed})\) of $7.63^{+6.87}_{-4.23}\times10^{-9}$ cm$^2$ (at 30 MeV), approximately 5.4× larger than DUT 2's highest cross-section, as shown in Table \ref{tab:ocv2_cc}.  
Its lowest \(\sigma(\text{Observed})\) of $1.18^{+6.87}_{-0.38}\times10^{-9}$ \text{cm}$^2$ is approximately 2.4x lower than DUT~1's lowest value of $2.84^{+1.70}_{-1.18}\times10^{-9}$ \text{cm}$^2$.
This analysis demonstrates that the Linux implementation on an FPGA fabric using RISC-V SoC has a comparable cross-section to its hardcore counterpart, the Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W, in our setup.
Furthermore, it highlights the robustness of the emerging RISC-V ISAs as a viable alternative to established ISAs like ARM and x86 used in COTS processors.
The OrangeCrab FPGA, based on the Lattice ECP5 FPGA (40 nm CMOS process, Ball Grid Array (BGA) package), was irradiated with the proton beam targeting its FPGA fabric.

