\begin{abstract}
% Data analysis is a multifaceted task requiring the integration of natural language understanding, logical reasoning, program synthesis, and result interpretation. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown potential in this domain, progress with open-source models remains limited. In this work, we systematically explore strategies to improve the data analysis capabilities of open-source LLMs. Building on pilot observation that fine-tuning with high-quality, synthetic data can enhance reasoning performance, we collect a seed dataset of realistic data analysis scenarios and evaluate open-source models across three core capabilities: data understanding, code generation, and analytical planning. Through empirical studies, we identify key factors affecting generalization to out-of-distribution tasks. Leveraging these insights, we synthesize new training data and retrain the model, demonstrating significant performance improvements. Our findings provide actionable strategies for advancing open-source LLMs in the data analysis domain.
Large Language Models (LLMs) hold promise in automating data analysis tasks, yet open-source models face significant limitations in these kinds of reasoning-intensive scenarios. In this work, we investigate strategies to enhance the data analysis capabilities of open-source LLMs. By curating a seed dataset of diverse, realistic scenarios, we evaluate models across three dimensions: data understanding, code generation, and strategic planning. Our analysis reveals three key findings: (1) Strategic planning quality serves as the primary determinant of model performance; (2) Interaction design and task complexity significantly influence reasoning capabilities; (3) Data quality demonstrates a greater impact than diversity in achieving optimal performance. We leverage these insights to develop a data synthesis methodology, demonstrating significant improvements in open-source LLMs' analytical reasoning capabilities.
% Leveraging these insights, we synthesize new training data and retrain the model, achieving substantial performance improvements. 
% Our approach provides actionable strategies to advance open-source LLMs for complex, reasoning-intensive data analysis tasks.

\end{abstract}





