\begin{abstract}

\noindent Financial institutions increasingly rely on large language models (LLMs) for high-stakes decision-making. However, these models risk perpetuating harmful biases if deployed without careful oversight. This paper investigates racial bias in LLMs specifically through the lens of credit decision-making tasks, operating on the premise that biases identified here are indicative of broader concerns across financial applications. We introduce a reproducible, counterfactual testing framework that evaluates how models respond to simulated mortgage applicants identical in all attributes except race. Our results reveal significant race-based discrepancies, exceeding historically observed bias levels. Leveraging layer-wise analysis, we track the propagation of sensitive attributes through internal model representations. Building on this, we deploy a control-vector intervention that effectively reduces racial disparities by up to 70\% (33\% on average) without impairing overall model performance. Our approach provides a transparent and practical toolkit for the identification and mitigation of bias in financial LLM deployments.

% risk of social bias in mortgage lending decisions made by Large Language Models (LLMs). We find that, despite existing safeguards, LLMs can absorb and amplify systemic biases from their training data. This study focuses on racial bias in LLMs when evaluating credit applications, revealing that LLMs demonstrate significant bias against Black applicants, exceeding the bias in current lending data. Through representation engineering—adjusting how the model encodes concepts without altering its core parameters—we interpret the model's perception of race in financial contexts and explore control vectors as a way to reduce bias. Our findings underscore the need for careful oversight when deploying LLMs in sensitive financial decisions.

%Given the growing appetite for the use of LLMs across a variety of fields including in economic research and financial services, we explore the potential of LLMs to exhibit undesirable social bias. LLMs are trained on vast text datasets and are likely to learn undesirable social biases. Red-teaming and other efforts to mitigate the biases may work at a general level, but may not remove bias in specific settings. We examine whether LLMs exhibit undesirable social bias in financial settings. We focus specifically on whether models exhibit racial preferences when tasked with making credit and lending decisions. We find that, across several models tested, LLMs exhibit racial bias against black applicants and that this bias is more pronounced than the bias found in current lending data (see \cite{Popick:2022aa}). We use representation engineering \cite{zou2023representation} to interpret the model's understanding of race in a financial setting and discuss the use of control vectors as a means to remediate the bias. This paper examines how LLMs fit into this evolving landscape of credit decision-making.

\end{abstract}
