\section{Foundations of Social Bias in LLMs}

There are a few reasons to expect LLMs to exhibit social bias in financial settings. There is a large body of economic research on unequal outcomes between different social groups, highlighting two key forms of discrimination: taste-based and statistical, and these forms of discrimination provide a framework for understanding how biases might arise in LLMs.

Taste-based discrimination, as discussed in \citet{becker1957economics}, shows how personal preferences can lead to discriminatory practices in economic settings, even if it comes at a cost to the discriminator. This type of discrimination is based on personal biases or tastes. For example, a lender might prefer to offer loans to individuals from a certain group not because they are more qualified, but simply because of a personal prejudice against other groups. In the context of this research, we could then expect that LLMs exhibit taste-based social bias because they have learned social prejudices from the expressions of prejudice found in the LLM training data (e.g. extremist views expressed on social media).

Statistical discrimination, on the other hand, arises when differences exist between social groups at an aggregate level and are used to make inferences about the attributes of individual members of the group \citep{arrow1971theory,phelps1972statistical}. In an abstract economic sense, this notion of discrimination has been argued to be rational (as in profit-maximizing) when the attributes inferred from group membership are otherwise unknown or costly to measure. In the context of this research, LLMs might exhibit social bias because they have learned statistical associations between social group membership and certain types of ``soft'' information.\footnote{\cite{liberti2019information} define ``soft'' information as private, qualitative, and hard-to-verify. ``Hard'' information, on the other hand, is  quantitative, easy to store and transmit in impersonal ways, and its information content is independent of its collection.}

Going beyond this framework, we might expect LLMs to exhibit social bias due to their generative nature. Consider that LLMs, as generative models, learn a distribution over their training data. When asked, they generate an output that seems like the most appropriate response to the prompt at hand -- a response that emulates the distribution of the training data.\footnote{Indeed, much of the work of alignment is essentially convincing the model that outputs that seem like an appropriate response are responses that contain the \emph{correct} response to the prompt.} Thus, when prompted to make a financial decision, the response that seems most appropriate may be the one that perpetuates long-running differences between social groups learned from the training data. 

We should not be surprised that LLMs exhibit social bias, as this phenomena has long been observed across a wide array of  artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) applications.\footnote{See, for example, \cite{hurlin2024fairness} for a broader discussion and a framework to detect, interpret, and mitigate fairness issues in credit scoring models.} For example, facial recognition technologies have consistently misidentified people of color at higher rates, revealing racial bias \citep{Buolamwini:2018aa}. Similarly, speech recognition tools often struggle to accurately transcribe the speech of marginalized groups \citep{koenecke2020racial}. AI tools like the COMPAS recidivism predictor have also shown significant disparities, such as predicting higher recidivism rates for Black defendants, which led to harsher sentencing outcomes \cite{Angwin:2016aa}. These patterns are not new and reflect broader issues of bias in AI systems.

A direct way to understand the drivers of bias in an LLM would be to analyze its training data. LLM developers, however, generally protect this information, sharing only broad details about the data's composition. As a result, our ability to pinpoint the specific type of social bias—whether statistical, taste-based, or rooted in historical inequalities—remains limited. Despite this limitation, we can still use prompt experiments to draw qualitative insights about the nature of the bias and attempt to control for it by supplying the model with additional ``hard'' information about the applicants.

