\begin{abstract}

    With the recent success of Large Language Models (LLMs), Descriptive Multimodal Emotion Recognition (DMER) has garnered increasing attention, which aims to describe a person's emotional state using free-form natural language. Unlike traditional discriminative methods that rely on predefined emotion taxonomies, DMER offers greater flexibility in emotional expression, enabling fine-grained and interpretable emotion representations. However, this free-form prediction paradigm exposes significant challenges in evaluation. Existing methods either depend on ground-truth descriptions that require substantial manual annotations or simplify the task by shifting the focus from evaluating descriptions to evaluating emotion labels. However, this simplification overlooks critical aspects such as emotional temporal dynamics, intensity, and uncertainty. To address these limitations, we draw inspiration from Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) and propose \textbf{DMER-Ranker}, a novel evaluation strategy that reformulates the traditional ``prediction-ground truth'' comparison into the ``prediction-prediction'' comparison, eliminating the need for ground-truth descriptions. We then employ the Bradley-Terry algorithm to convert pairwise comparison results into model-level rankings. Additionally, we explore the possibility of automatic preference prediction and introduce \textbf{DMER-Preference}, the first preference dataset specifically designed for human emotions. Our work advances the field of DMER and lays the foundation for more intelligent human-computer interaction systems.

\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
Descriptive Multimodal Emotion Recognition (DMER) \cite{lian2025affectgpt}, also known as Explainable MER (EMER) \cite{lian2023explainable}, aims to use free-form natural language to describe emotional states. Unlike traditional discriminative methods that depend on predefined emotion taxonomies \cite{lian2023survey, lian2024merbench}, DMER adopts a generative paradigm, offering greater flexibility in emotion representation. This enables fine-grained and interpretable emotional expression, presenting significant opportunities for advancing emotion-intelligent human-computer interaction technologies \cite{brave2007emotion}. Recent advancements in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs), with their rich vocabularies and multimodal understanding capabilities, have made this task feasible. Figure \ref{fig:task_comparison} shows the distinctions between descriptive and discriminative MER.

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{image/demo.pdf}
	\caption{\textbf{Task comparison.} Discriminative MER assigns a label from a predefined taxonomy to represent a character's emotional state. In contrast, descriptive MER provides a more detailed description, incorporating multimodal clues, fine-grained emotion labels, and emotion intensity.}
	\label{fig:task_comparison}
\end{figure}

However, evaluating the quality of such open-ended descriptions remains a non-trivial task. Current evaluation strategies can be broadly classified into two categories. The first approach leverages ground-truth emotion descriptions and utilizes LLMs, such as OpenAI GPT \cite{hurst2024gpt}, to measure the similarity between predicted and ground-truth descriptions \cite{lian2023explainable}. Nevertheless, emotions are inherently linked to diverse multimodal cues, including facial expressions, (micro-)gestures, head movements, hand actions, and vocal tones \cite{noroozi2018survey, chen2023smg, li2020deep}. Consequently, generating a comprehensive and accurate description of a person’s emotional state is inherently challenging. Moreover, obtaining high-quality ground-truth descriptions entails substantial annotation costs \cite{lian2023explainable}. Given these challenges, researchers propose bypassing the evaluation of emotion descriptions and focusing solely on emotion labels \cite{lian2025affectgpt, lian2025ov}. While this approach is more cost-effective, it neglects key aspects of emotional expression present in descriptions, such as temporal dynamics, intensity, and uncertainty. Furthermore, it fails to consider the existence and reasonableness of clue-based analysis, resulting in an incomplete assessment of emotion descriptions. Figure \ref{fig:existing_evaluation_strategy} summarizes the existing evaluation strategies for DMER.

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{image/previous_metrics.pdf}
	\caption{\textbf{Existing evaluation strategies for DMER.} (a) Description-based evaluation, which depends on expensive human-annotated descriptions; (b) Label-based evaluation, which focuses on emotion labels; (c) Compared to description-based methods, label-based evaluation neglects critical aspects, such as emotional temporal dynamics, intensity, uncertainty, and the existence of multimodal cues, resulting in an incomplete assessment of emotion description.}
	\label{fig:existing_evaluation_strategy}
\end{figure}


Drawing inspiration from Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) \cite{ouyang2022training}, we transform the complex problem of designing indicators to measure semantic similarity with ground truth into a simpler problem of learning human preferences. Specifically, we propose \textbf{DMER-Ranker}, shifting the evaluation paradigm from the ``prediction-ground truth'' comparison to the ``prediction-prediction'' comparison. In our annotation process, we provide two descriptions for a video and ask \emph{human annotators} to determine which description better captures the character's emotional state. We then employ the Bradley-Terry algorithm \cite{hunter2004mm} to transform these pairwise comparison results into model-level rankings. Compared to previous methods (see Figure \ref{fig:existing_evaluation_strategy}), our approach addresses the need for costly ground-truth descriptions. Furthermore, it directly assesses model performance based on descriptions, rather than relying solely on emotion labels. Therefore, this method provides a valuable complement to existing evaluation strategies.


Beyond the evaluation strategy, we also investigate whether the aforementioned human annotation process can be automated using MLLMs, thereby further reducing evaluation costs. Current research on LLMs-as-judges primarily focuses on pure-text or vision-language inputs \cite{chen2024mllm, gu2024survey}, with limited exploration in the domain of human emotions, which is characterized by multimodality, temporal dynamics, and subtle variations \cite{cacioppo1999emotion, picard2000affective}. To address this gap, we conduct a pioneering study to examine the feasibility of using MLLMs as judges for DMER. We first construct a preference dataset, \textbf{DMER-Preference}. To build this dataset, we provide two emotion descriptions for each video and recruit multiple annotators to evaluate which description better aligns with the character’s emotional state. Only samples with unanimous agreement among the all annotators are included in the final dataset. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first preference dataset specifically designed for human emotions. We then perform a comprehensive evaluation of various MLLMs on the preference prediction task and explore strategies to improve their consistency with human annotations. The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:


\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{(Ranking Strategy)} This paper draws inspiration from RLHF and introduces \emph{DMER-Ranker}, a novel ranking strategy specifically designed for DMER. In contrast to prior approaches, our method eliminates the reliance on ground-truth descriptions but can still focus on evaluating complete emotion descriptions rather than isolated emotion labels. This work represents a significant advancement and valuable complement to existing evaluation methodologies.

    \item \textbf{(Automated Evaluation)} This paper presents a pioneering study on automatic preference prediction for human emotions. We construct \emph{DMER-Preference}, the first preference dataset in this domain, and conduct a comprehensive evaluation of multiple MLLMs on preference prediction. Our work makes a great contribution to the research on MLLMs-as-judges.

    \item \textbf{(Discovery)} This paper proposes multiple strategies to enhance the performance of automatic preference prediction. Furthermore, we propose several evaluation metrics and examine their relationships and effectiveness within the context of DMER.

\end{itemize}


\section{Ranking Strategy: DMER-Ranker}
\label{sec:ranking_strategy}
This paper draws inspiration from RLHF and proposes a ranking strategy that eliminates the need for ground-truth descriptions. The core idea is to transform the traditional ``prediction-ground truth'' comparison into the ``prediction-prediction'' comparison. Section \ref{sec:practical_implementation} provides an example to illustrate this ranking process. Specifically, suppose there are $M$ models, and let $m_i$ denote one of them. Consider pairwise comparisons between model $m_i$ and model $m_j$ as an example. To ensure reliability, we conduct preference comparisons across multiple samples. For each sample $x^k, k\in[1, K]$, we prompt model $m_i$ and model $m_j$ to generate emotion descriptions for $x^k$, resulting in $d^k_i$ and $d^k_j$, respectively. We then recruit multiple annotators to assign preference scores to $d^k_i$ and $d^k_j$, and their majority voted results are denoted as $p^k_{ij}$. Figure \ref{fig:annotation_layout} illustrates the annotation platform layout, where \emph{description 1} and \emph{description 2} correspond to $d^k_i$ and $d^k_j$, respectively.
\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{image/annotation.pdf}
	\caption{\textbf{Annotation platform layout.} We provide annotators a video along with two descriptions and then ask them to determine which description better reflects the character's emotional state. They can choose from three options: \emph{description 1}, \emph{description 2}, or \emph{tie}.}
	\label{fig:annotation_layout}
\end{figure}

By aggregating human-annotated results across all samples $p^k_{ij}, k\in[1, K]$, we can determine the overall performance comparison between models $m_i$ and $m_j$. For instance, if humans prefer the outputs of $m_i$ 100 times and $m_j$ 200 times, we consider $m_j$ to outperform $m_i$. This preference score is then binarized into 0 or 1 and recorded in a matrix $\mathbf{W}$:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{W}_{ij} = \begin{cases}
        1, m_i \;\text{beats}\; m_j\\
        0, m_i \;\text{loses to}\; m_j\\
    \end{cases}.
\end{equation}
Next, we apply the Bradley-Terry algorithm to infer model rankings from the preference matrix $\mathbf{W}$. In this algorithm, each model $m_i$ is associated with a positive parameter $\theta_i$, which quantifies its relative strength or advantage. The probability that model $m_i$ outperforms model $m_j$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}
    P(m_i \; \text{beats} \; m_j) = \frac{\theta_i}{\theta_i+\theta_j}.
\end{equation}
Combined with the observation $\mathbf{W}$, we estimate $\theta$ by maximizing the following likelihood function:
\begin{equation}
    \max L(\theta) = \prod_{i<j}\left(\frac{\theta_i}{\theta_i + \theta_j}\right)^{\mathbf{W}_{ij}}\left(\frac{\theta_j}{\theta_i + \theta_j}\right)^{1-\mathbf{W}_{ij}}.
\end{equation}
For computational efficiency, we optimize the log-likelihood instead:
\begin{equation}
    \min - \sum_{i<j}\left[\mathbf{W}_{ij} \ln{\theta_i} + (1-\mathbf{W}_{ij})\ln{\theta_j}-\ln{(\theta_i + \theta_j)}\right].
\end{equation}
Finally, we use gradient descent to estimate the advantage scores for each model. Models with higher scores are ranked higher.


\section{Automatic Preference Prediction}
\label{sec:automated_preference_prediction}
The above ranking strategy (see Section \ref{sec:ranking_strategy}) relies on human annotations. In this section, we investigate the possibility of automating human feedback entirely. Specifically, we first construct a high-quality preference dataset tailored for human emotions, named \textbf{DMER-Preference}. Leveraging this dataset, we then reveal the feasibility of employing MLLMs for automatic preference prediction.


\subsection{Preference Dataset: DMER-Preference}
\label{sec:preference_dataset}
To build the dataset, we first collect 1,368 video samples and provide two emotion descriptions for each. Then, we recruit multiple annotators to assess these descriptions in the context of the corresponding video content and determine which one more accurately reflects the character’s emotional state. To ensure annotation quality, all annotators must pass a preliminary test focused on emotion recognition. Finally, we retain only samples with unanimous agreement among the all annotators, resulting in a total of 574 samples. This strict selection strategy ensures the reliability of the preference labels.

