\subsection{MLLM-based Solutions}
\label{sec:solutions}
Since human expresses emotions through multiple modalities, we only select mainstream open-source and closed-source MLLMs that support at least audio or video inputs. We then propose four distinct strategies to realize preference prediction.

\paragraph{Strategy 1 (S1).}
We directly input the video along with two descriptions into MLLM and prompt it to identify the preferred description. Figure \ref{fig:s1} illustrates the template used in S1.

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{image/s1.pdf}
	\caption{Template for S1.}
	\label{fig:s1}
\end{figure}


\paragraph{Strategy 2 (S2).}
S2 decomposes the preference prediction process in S1 into two steps. Specifically, we first prompt MLLM to generate a detailed description of the video, with a particular focus on the character’s emotional state. We then treat the output from the first step as the reference and ask the model to determine which of the two candidate descriptions aligns better with this reference. Figure \ref{fig:s2_s3} illustrates the detailed template used in S2.

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{image/s2_s3.pdf}
	\caption{Template for S2 and S3.}
	\label{fig:s2_s3}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Strategy 3 (S3).}
The second step in S2 relies solely on text input, which can be implemented using an external LLM. The main reason for introducing this alternative is that the current training process of MLLMs typically begins with a pretrained LLM, followed by additional multimodal training. While this enables the model to understand other modalities, the suboptimal training strategy may harm its text processing capability. Therefore, we opt to use an external LLM for the second step.


\paragraph{Strategy 4 (S4).}
We further divide the second step (in Figure \ref{fig:s2_s3}) into two steps, requiring the model to perform an additional reasoning process rather than directly outputting a preference decision. Our goal is to investigate whether incorporating this intermediate reasoning step can improve performance in preference prediction. Figure \ref{fig:s4} provides the detailed template used in S4.

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{image/s4.pdf}
	\caption{Template for S4.}
	\label{fig:s4}
\end{figure}


