\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{sec:evaluation_metric}

\paragraph{Recognition Performance.}
This metric measures the consistency between model predictions and human annotations. The DMER-Preference dataset contains three labels: (1) the first description is preferred; (2) the second description is preferred; (3) the descriptions are tied. As discussed in Section \ref{sec:preference_dataset}, ties are often ambiguous and uncertain, making it difficult for annotators to reach consensus. Therefore, we report both weighted average F1-score (WAF) and accuracy (ACC) for both two-class (excluding ties) and three-class (including ties) conditions. In the following experiments, when only one metric is reported, we default to the two-class WAF.


\paragraph{Flip Consistency.}
In our experiments, we observe that reversing the input order sometimes leads to different results. Take Figure \ref{fig:reverse_consistency} as an example. Ideally, if we reverse the order of two descriptions, we expect the model to prefer the same description. However, in practice, some models exhibit distinct preferences. We hypothesize that this may be because their preferences are driven by the presentation order rather than the content of the descriptions. Therefore, we introduce \emph{flip consistency}, a metric that measures the consistency of prediction results for forward and reverse orders. This metric evaluates the model's robustness to position bias, ensuring reliable preference predictions.


