\section{Results and Discussions}
In this section, we present comprehensive experimental evaluations and detailed analyses of the results. \textbf{We first investigate the potential of automatic preference prediction, identifying strategies to enhance its performance. Subsequently, we integrate the optimal automatic preference prediction approach with DMER-Ranker for model-wise ranking.} All experiments were conducted on an A100 GPU. To mitigate randomness, each experiment is repeated twice, with both average scores and standard deviations reported.


\subsection{Impact of Different Solutions}
\label{sec:impact_of_different_solutions}
Section \ref{sec:solutions} presents four MLLM-based solutions, and their performance differences are compared in Figure \ref{Figure9}. For most models, S3/S4 outperform S1/S2. The key distinction between these strategies lies in whether they leverage an external LLM. These findings highlight the benefits of incorporating an external LLM, as MLLMs may struggle with natural language understanding. However, some exceptions exist. For instance, Qwen2.5-VL and Qwen2.5-Omni perform better with S1/S2 than with S3/S4. This is because these models retain strong language processing capabilities due to their advanced training methodologies, allowing them to effectively interpret complex prompts. In such cases, the longer inference chains in S3/S4 may introduce error accumulation and degrade performance. Thus, a trade-off emerges: while using an external LLM can compensate for the limited language processing abilities of some MLLMs, longer inference chains may also increase error accumulation. Consequently, the optimal solution depends on the specific model.


