\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}
As discussed in Section \ref{sec:impact_of_different_solutions}, different MLLMs exhibit preferences for distinct solutions. Table \ref{Table3} summarizes the performance of each model when using its optimal strategy. These results reveal that current MLLMs demonstrate relatively weak alignment with human annotations, underscoring the challenges of automatic preference prediction. Notably, flip consistency and recognition performance show low correlation. For example, Otter \cite{li2023otter} achieves high flip consistency but performs poorly in recognition performance. It indicates that these metrics capture distinct aspects of model behavior: recognition performance evaluates the model's accuracy in preference prediction, whereas flip consistency ensures that predictions are based on content rather than input order. Ideally, a model should excel in both metrics. Among all models, AffectGPT \cite{lian2025affectgpt} demonstrates the best overall performance, achieving 68.90\% on the two-class WAF and 85.45\% on flip consistency, highlighting its superior ability to understand human emotions.


