\subsection{Model-Based Crowdsourcing}
\label{sec:model_based_crowdsourcing}
Given the limitations of MLLMs in preference prediction (see Table \ref{Table3}), we propose enhancing their performance through model-based crowdsourcing. Our approach consists of two key steps: 1) Filtering models based on their recognition performance and flip consistency, retaining only those that exceed 60\% in both metrics. 2) Applying majority voting to aggregate predictions from the selected models. In Figure \ref{Figure10}, we rank the filtered models by performance and select the top-$N$ models for aggregation. Our experiments demonstrate that this strategy improves preference recognition performance. But as the number of models increases, performance initially improves and then declines. These findings highlight the importance of selecting an appropriate $N$: a large value introduces excessive noise, while a small value limits the effectiveness of the approach. As shown in Figure \ref{Figure10}, $N=4$ is a balanced choice that achieves promising results without excessive noise.


