\subsection{Combination of Forward and Reversed Predictions}
As demonstrated in Section \ref{sec:choice_of_llms}, current models demonstrate suboptimal performance regarding multi-run consistency and flip consistency. This observation naturally raises the question:  \emph{Can we enhance the reliability of the results by aggregating the outputs of multiple rounds of forward and backward inputs?} To evaluate this hypothesis, we execute each method twice in forward order and twice in reverse order, generating four preference descriptions $\{p_1, \cdots, p_4\}$. We then employ majority voting to determine the final outcome. Figure \ref{Figure20} presents the impact of this strategy across different MLLMs. Experimental results demonstrate that most models benefit from this approach, including OpenAI GPT and Gemini. However, some exceptions also exist, such as Chat-UniVi and Qwen2.5-VL, indicating that the effectiveness of this strategy is model-dependent.
