\subsection{Practical Implementation: DMER-Ranker with Automatic Preference Prediction}
\label{sec:practical_implementation}
This section illustrates our ranking process through a concrete example. We first select $M=10$ models $\mathbf{U}=$ \{{Chat-UniVi}, {LLaMA-VID}, {mPLUG-Owl}, {Video-ChatGPT}, {Otter}, {SALMONN}, {Qwen-Audio}, {Video-LLaVA}, {VideoChat}, {VideoChat2}\} and conduct preference comparisons across $N=332$ samples. This process requires a total of $C(M, 2) \times N=14,940$ comparisons. To optimize evaluation efficiency, we apply the strategy described in Section \ref{sec:model_based_crowdsourcing}, utilizing the top four performing models $\mathbf{V}=$ \{AffectGPT, Qwen2.5-VL, Qwen2.5-Omni, Gemini-1.5-Flash\} for automated preference prediction. Notably, Gemini-1.5-Flash is the only closed-source model in this group. To minimize evaluation costs, we limit automatic preference prediction to the remaining three open-source models. \textbf{In this example, we deliberately use different MLLMs for ranking and preference prediction, i.e., $\mathbf{U} \cap \mathbf{V}= \emptyset$.}


Figure \ref{fig:raw_w} summarizes the final preference prediction results. We then transform these predictions into the matrix $\mathbf{W}$, yielding the results shown in Figure \ref{fig:mapped_w}. Applying the Bradley-Terry algorithm to matrix $\mathbf{W}$, we derive the advantage score $\theta$ for each model. Based on these scores, the models are ranked in descending order as follows: \emph{\textcolor[rgb]{0.93,0.0,0.47}{{Chat-UniVi}, {LLaMA-VID}, {mPLUG-Owl}, {Video-ChatGPT}, {Otter}, {SALMONN}, {Qwen-Audio}, {Video-LLaVA}, {VideoChat}, and {VideoChat2}}}.
