\section{Discussion: DMER-Ranker with Manual Preference Annotations}
As illustrated in Figure \ref{Figure10}, the automatic strategy achieves a maximum accuracy of only approximately 70\%, highlighting its inherent limitations for preference prediction. Consequently, human annotation remains indispensable for obtaining reliable ranking results. To balance accuracy requirements with evaluation costs, we propose two practical strategies for human annotation. 1) Reduce the number of comparison samples; 2) Implement a hierarchical comparison strategy. For example, we can divide $M$ models into two subsets, conduct pairwise comparisons within each subset to identify the top-performing model from each, and then perform a final comparison between the two subset winners. This hierarchical approach significantly reduces the total number of required comparisons while maintaining the reliability of the final ranking. Further experiments involving human annotations will be explored in our future work.


\section{Conclusion}
This paper proposes \textbf{DMER-Ranker}, a novel ranking strategy for the DMER task that eliminates the need for ground-truth descriptions. To achieve this, we shift from the traditional ``prediction-ground truth'' comparison paradigm into the ``prediction-prediction'' comparison paradigm, subsequently employing the Bradley-Terry algorithm to derive model rankings from pairwise comparison outcomes. To enhance evaluation efficiency, we explore the potential of automatic preference prediction and introduce \textbf{DMER-Preference}, the first preference dataset focused on human emotions. Extensive experiments across multiple MLLMs demonstrate that current models struggle with accurate preference predictions. Although model-based crowdsourcing improves performance, the predictions still show limited consistency with human annotations. Therefore, at the current stage, automatic evaluation should be treated as a reference rather than a definitive measure. For reliable ranking results, human annotation remains essential.


