\subsection{Training Dynamics}

QMetric also tracks training behavior using the \emph{Training Stability Index} (TSI), which compares variability in training and validation losses, and the \emph{Training Efficiency Index} (TEI), which measures epochs needed to reach a target accuracy relative to model size.

Quantum-specific diagnostics include the \emph{Quantum Gradient Norm} (QGN) and \emph{Barren Plateau Indicator} (BPI), both of which expose vanishing gradients linked to deep or poorly initialized circuits. To compare hybrid and classical models, relative metrics such as \emph{RQLSI} and \emph{r-QTEI} quantify differences in training efficiency and stability under aligned conditions. Together, these metrics support targeted diagnosis of underperformance, guide ansatz design, and enable meaningful evaluation across model types.

\section{Case Study: Hybrid vs Classical on MNIST}

To demonstrate QMetric’s diagnostic value, we applied it to a binary classification task on MNIST digits \texttt{0} vs \texttt{1}, comparing a classical feedforward network to a hybrid quantum-classical model using Qiskit's \texttt{ZZFeatureMap} and \texttt{RealAmplitudes} circuits. Inputs were reduced to 3 dimensions via PCA to match the quantum input size, and both models were trained for 30 epochs using Adam. The classical model reached 99.8\% validation accuracy by epoch 5 and stabilized at 99.6\%, while the hybrid model plateaued at 69.6\% and never surpassed 90\%. Despite this gap, the hybrid model showed smoother convergence with a much lower Training Stability Index (TSI = 0.0023 vs. 0.7147), though its Training Efficiency Index (TEI) was infinite due to non-convergence.

QMetric revealed that the hybrid model’s underperformance stemmed not from poor circuit design but from encoding limitations. Quantum Output Sensitivity (QOS = 6.74) was high and Activation Diversity (QLAD = 0.00) was absent, indicating a collapsed feature map insensitive to input variation. Compression (FMCR = 3.0) and effective feature dimension (EDQFS = 1.0) were also low. In contrast, architectural metrics were strong: the circuit showed high expressibility (QCE = 0.94), perfect simulated fidelity (QCF = 1.00), and moderate entanglement (EEE = 0.50) and mutual information (QMI = 1.00). These results highlight QMetric’s ability to pinpoint specific failure modes.



\section{Outlook}
QMetric provides a structured approach for diagnosing hybrid quantum-classical models beyond conventional performance metrics. It highlights key aspects such as training behavior, encoding robustness, and circuit design quality. Future developments will include migration to Qiskit’s Estimator v2, support for additional platforms like PennyLane, and expanded metric coverage for multi-class tasks and generative models.

